      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Old. I.abar CHICALA</head>
        <p>Old. I.abar CHICALA</p>
        <p>THE DRY FEED FOR CHICKENS.</p>
        <p>÷ .14 .1- POULTRY PEN 'l. ÷ • 44 .14 .14 + Hints and Interesting Facts and + .14 Chat for Amateurs. •I. .1. .14 ..x..14,14.144.14.14.14++.1.4 .14.1444,14.14.14.14.14+ 4.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>ESSENTIAL FACTORS.</head>
        <p>ESSENTIAL FACTORS.</p>
        <p>There is one thing the British poultry-keeper must realise, and that fully, namely, that if he is going to secure the highest prices for his pro- duce, then the eggs must be marketed in the best manner, and they must, moreover, be abso- lutely fresh. An ,egg more than three days old is not, nor has it any right to be called, new laid, although I am afraid this is not the stan- dard of every shopkeeper and dealer. The eggs should be sent to market with the least possible delay; they should be clean and graded into sizes, and they should be packed in clean straw, or,etter still, in wood-wool. An egg is just as .perishable an article of food as milk, and until this fact is realised the best prices will never be secured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WHERE THE CR'OSS-BRED SCORES.</head>
        <p>WHERE THE CR'OSS-BRED SCORES.</p>
        <p>When birds are kept absolutely pure year after year there is - a tendency to develop the exhibition type, which is detrimental to the economic qualities, after a certain point, of purity is reached. It is found that when birds show signs of failure, either in size or fecundity, an introduction of foreign blood will rapidly restore their lost vigour. This is one of the chief points in favour of crossing, and un- doubtedly a very important one. Crossing should not be done merely for the sake of cross- ing, but some clearly defined object should be in view, such as the strengthening of an im- paired constitution, an increase in the size of body, or 'an improvement in the laying or table qualities. All this can be accomplished if there is some method in the selection of the birds to be crossed. yowls are sometimes used for this purpose without any forethought as to what the progeny of the union will be. There is small doubt that cross-bred fowls are hardier than their more tenderly bred brethren, and are better able to withstand a cold, ex- posed situation, and the 'rough usage of life on a farm, where very often the housing and general management are not of the .best. One of the most important points to be observed in crossing is to see that the stock to be mated are old-established breeds, in which the type is thoroughly fixed. This applies to both sexes, but more especially to the male bird. The older the breed, the stronger is its propotency, and- its own distinctive characteristics are im- pressed upon the Progeny to a. remarkable ex- tent when they_Age crossed with birds of less fixity of type. Take, for instance, an old English game, Dorking, or Houdan male, and mate it with some" of the newer breeds, and the type of the former is. very manifest, while for generations after the introduction it is ap- parent.—" Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>MARKETING OF EGGS.</head>
        <p>MARKETING OF EGGS.</p>
        <p>Although their proximity to consuming centres places British- producers of eggs in a much more favourable position than their Con- tinental competitors, unsatisfactory methods of marketing have prevented them -from reaping the full benefit of the advantage. The com- plaints of retailers that home supplies of eggs are less reliable than, and often inferior to, the better grades of the imported produce are un- fortunately too often justified. It is true that during recent years there has been a marked improvement in the methods of marketing. The establishment of egg collecting depots has de- monstrated the benefit or marketing on co- operative lines, and the work of itinerant poul- try instructors has led many poultry keepers to adopt better methods. It is probable, however, that by far the greater number of eggs pro- duced in this country still reach the consumer lacking in one or more of the qualities which indicate a first-grade egg. This state of things contrasts unfavourably with the steps taken by foreign shippers to improve the quality of the eggs placed on the British market, and is the more unfortunate because the introduction of a more efficient organisation for marketing would enable the British producer to take full advan- tage of his position. No foreign eggs can be marketed in this country under four days, and the number imported so quickly is very small indeed; it appears probable that the demand for eggs fresher than this is practically un- limited and restricted to native supplies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Eggs, Poultry, etc.</head>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, etc.</p>
        <p>BUFF Orpington Eggs, 3s. 6d. dozen; carriage paid; winners of Laying Competitions; send for list.—Browne and Saffery, Radnor Poultry Farm, Folkestone</p>
        <p>EGGS for sitting, 5/- dozen, from very typical birds; unfertiles replaced.—Lawes Warburton, Croakl gpecialist, Verwood, Dorset.</p>
        <p>EGGS, 3s. dozen I Chickens from Sixpence each. Leghorns, Wyandottes, Orpingtons, Minorcas, Rhode Island Reds; 10 breeds: broodies; best layers only; no fancy stock or prices; carriage sixpence.—Gill, Heath-road, Penketh, Warrington.</p>
        <p>- - FINE Vigorous Utility Cockerels—White Leghorns, White Wyandottes, Buff Rocks; all good stock birds; approval; 7s. 6d. Eggs for sit- ting 35, 6d.—Fhillipson, Liverpool-road, Skelmers- dale,</p>
        <p>RHODE Island Reds, Cockerels, rose-comb, splendid colour, March hatched, Solomon- Cooper strain; 7s. 6d. each.—Mrs. Bardley, Bolors House, Wellington, Salop •</p>
        <p>RHODE ISLAND RED single-comb Cockerels, bred from prize-winners and layers with record of over 200 eggs. Healthy stock birds, splendid type and colour; Scott-Solomon-Goode strain; worth 21s. each; price 7s. 6d. and ss. to clear. Sitting eggs from best pens, 55, dozen,—Rev. .Hobbes, Wroxeter, Shrewsbury.</p>
        <p>TO Farmers and Poultry-keepers. Buy now. England's best hatching machine, "The Gloucester Incubator." Ask for catalogue. "White Rose," delivered in 10 to 100 gallon lots at 9d. per • gallon. Minced Beef, ideal egg producer; no waste, no trouble-, 12s. cwt., Gs. 6d. half cwt., carriage Sutcliffe, 1124;;;Whitigate Drivep.Black• pool.</p>
        <p>Telephone 115.</p>
        <p>DOO,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>FARM AND GARDE.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDE.</p>
        <p>• BACON CURING ON THE FARM.</p>
        <p>In rural districts there has of late years been a great decline in the home-curing of bacon owing to the more extensive production of mild- cured bacon in factories, for which the general public have acquired a taste. In times such as these, however,l no means of increasing the food supply should be neglected. Home-cured bacon and hams of the highest qualitiy can be pro- duced with suitable management, and it would undoubtedly be an economical proceeding for many small farmers and cottagers to undertake the curing of their own bacon. The home-curing of bacon is best performed during the autumn and winter months, from November to February, and although niild-cur- ing may be satisfactorily effected during the summer months, where specifil facilities are available, yet at that time of year the disadvan- tages of the warm weather and the prevalence of flies call for the exercise of additional care. Advice as to the kinds of pigs, slaughtering, preparation of carcase, curing by various systems, pickling, smoking, &amp;e.,41s contained in</p>
        <p>leaflet 285, which will be sent free and post free on application to the Secretary, Board of Agri- culture and Fisheries, Whitehall-place, Lon- don, S.W. Letters of application so addressed need not be stamped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>TILL UP THE LAND.</head>
        <p>TILL UP THE LAND.</p>
        <p>On the outbreak of the war the Board of Agri- culture issued, instructions as to how all spare land could be utilised for increasing our food supplies. Here is the advice given in advance OS to</p>
        <p>Sowing or Planting in February and March.-:- The following crops are suitable for land that has been lying vacant during the winter. Work may begin in February or as soon as genial weather sets in: 1, Potatoes, :early varieties; 2, beans, broad and kidney; 3, peas, early and mid- season; 4, cauliflowers, early; 5, round spinach; 6, ;spinach beet. ,</p>
        <p>1. Potatoes: The land should be thofouollv dug and forked during the Winter and Worked into a friable condition. The common aistance to plant for first early varieties is 4in. in depTh, 12in. between the sets, and about 20in. between the rows. For mid-season and late varieties.the sets may be 15in. ipart and 24in. to 30in. be- tween the rows. For further information see leaflet 173 on "Potato Growing." 2. Broad beans: Plant nin. to 4in. deep, with ISin. to 21in. between the drills and 4in. between the seeds.'</p>
        <p>Kidney beans: Plant 2in. deep, 2in. to 4in. apart in the drill, and 18in. to 27in. apart in the rows. One pound will plant 150 ft. to 200 ft. of drill. 3. Peas: Sow in a flat drill from 2in. to 3in. in depth according to soil—in stiff soil shallow, and in light soil deep. Dwarf varieties should be sown in rows 18in. to 2ft. apart. Tall varie- ties further apart according to height. Early peas require 11b. to 70ft. Of drill. Late varieties 11b. to 85ft. of drill. , 4. Cauliflowers: The distances are the same as in the case of cabbage.</p>
        <p>5. Round spinach Sow loz. of seed to 65ft. of drill, about lin. deep, in rows 12in. to 15in. apart. •</p>
        <p>6. Spinach beet: Sow as for round spinach, but thin out to Bin. or 10in, apart in the rows. Information as to the application of fertilisers to these crops will be found in leaflet 106. These leafleti may be had free on application to the Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>AGRICULTURAL CO-OPERATION.</head>
        <p>AGRICULTURAL CO-OPERATION.</p>
        <p>"It is a most remarkable fact that big farmers and the older men are becoming almost as keen to co-operate as the rising generation. There is a farmer in a certain county in the South of England, who.farms 'several thousand acres, who for-some years refused to have any- thing to do with the co-operative society in his county. Not long since he joined, and recently speaking at a dinner, stated, `Before I was a co-operator, when I looked over my neighbour's hedge and saw he had a bad year I was glad; now that I am a co-operator and look over my .neighbour's hedge and find that he had a bad year I am sorry, and I want to help him.' There you have the educational value of the move- ment. This is a striking instance of the com- plete change in the mental outlook 'brought about by contact with the co-operative principle in practice; through association. the barriers of prejudice were broken down. Co-operation means money, but it means more than money. It means better business, better living, broader minded people, and more toleration each of the other, higher ideals of neighbourliness and sociability." —" Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>HERBACEOUS PHLOXES.</p>
        <p>I cannot understand why the herbaceous phlox is so seldom seen, as a large group is very effective in the autumn, when yellow flowers, as a rule, preponderate. Phloxes, to see them in good condition, must have deeply dug and well-manured ground, :and where a certain amount of moisture prevails, dryness at the roots being all against them. For this reason in soil that is inclined to be light it is a good plan to surround each plant with a heavy mulch of cow manure, if to be had, and flood with water in dry weather. . After about three years the plants are liable to become, weak, and should be lifted and divided, choosing for replanting thei pieces on the out- side and pulling them apart, not cutting with a knife, as this treatment very often destroys many of the young and vigorous shoots. • If lifted, divided, and •replanted now they will have got a hold of the fresh soil before the spring, which very often is dry, and prevents the free rooting of the plants. It is well, too, just before the plants come into flower, to renew the mulch, and if .the weather is at all dry to water freely. The selection of varieties is now a very wide one, the colours ranging from the purest white to the richest carmine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>BULBS FAILING TO FLOWER.</head>
        <p>BULBS FAILING TO FLOWER.</p>
        <p>Now that the season of flowering bulbs is with us, complaints are all too frequent as to the non-development of the flower spikes in such as hyacinths, tulips, &amp;c. Many amateurs in their anxiety to get the bulbs into flower place them in heat before they have had time to make any roots to assist the development of the flower spikes. Bulbs, after having bon potted, should be plunged in the open air in coal ashes or leaf soil to the depth of at least six inches. Here they should remain for at the least six ,weeks—more if possible, as on the amount of roots depends in a great measure the flowering. After such treatment the bulbs may with every chance of a good flowering be intro- duced into the greenhouse, seeing to it that they do not suffer. froin - the want of water.— "Wick" iu `,` Farm, and Home?!</p>
        <p>Established 183 IL</p>
        <p>PIGEON,</p>
        <p>Poultry &amp; Ciiicken Specialities.</p>
        <p>, - • ' ,;•;,. (..e--"*..:...:-. . ff• , 4.,,- -.7 ~ ACEit ' , 'rte, SianiZZA...._,M)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>, V ,4% e E R LT ", •</head>
        <p>, V ,4% e E R LT ", •</p>
        <p>corn, Flour and Provender Stores. Corn Millers, &amp;c., WARRINGTON. Household Flour at Wholesale Prices.-- Oatmal at Wholesale Prices.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>• 4.44, 044+44 0 04,004)+4).+4 4,+.40.4.4.4.0+ 04&gt; 0 ++44 0 04. +0•:•04.000 0 0 0 •:•+ 0 0 0 0 0 00 .I'. • 0 0 0 4. 4, 4. 4. 4. • 4. 4. 4. 4, ••4. 4. • .). 4.4,0 4. 4. 444. +4. 4.4. ••4.4.• 4. 4. • 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. +4,4.4. 4. •4.4.4. • • 4.4. 4 4.• 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. • 4. • 4. • • 4. • 0 4. 4. • 4. • 0 4. 0 •0 0 • 0 0 . • 0 o</head>
        <p>• 4.44, 044+44 0 04,004)+4).+4 4,+.40.4.4.4.0+ 04&gt; 0 ++44 0 04. +0•:•04.000 0 0 0 •:•+ 0 0 0 0 0 00 .I'. • 0 0 0 4. 4, 4. 4. 4. • 4. 4. 4. 4, ••4. 4. • .). 4.4,0 4. 4. 444. +4. 4.4. ••4.4.• 4. 4. • 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. +4,4.4. 4. •4.4.4. • • 4.4. 4 4.• 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. • 4. • 4. • • 4. • 0 4. 4. • 4. • 0 4. 0 •0 0 • 0 0 . • 0 o</p>
        <p>womAN 3 S , _ . 11, t#</p>
        <p>neutral tones .prevailing. The -War still influ- ences a subdued taste in dress, and although new 'fashions are desirable and help to keep us young, novelty and startling changes are not to be expected. . Navy serges, black and white, check, and the more sober shades will predomin- ate,. and we shall have to depend upon little vivid touches of colour to strike a note of cheer- fulness in the world of dress, for the simple reason that. the supply of bright materials is likely to fail. For these we have hitherto de- pended on German dyes, and already the short- age' is making itself felt.. The dTapers of Lon- don and the country have been discussing how 'best to deal with the dilemma, but if our manu- facturers have to fall back on the old logwood dyes, that woula settle the question of 'colours, for.searcely anything else but black and brown would be procurable. It will not be Madame la Mode, but trade' conditions that will. largely determine the colours and materials to be worn th 4 year.</p>
        <p>Feathers, Flowers and Bows. It is quite astonishing to note the continued popularity of the ostrich feather in its many forms and guises. The feathtr ruche is as much in vogue now as it was months ago, prob- ably because it is shoWn in so many widths. To-enumerate all the varieties of ostrich mounts would take a considerable time, but some Of the newest deserve mention. Such are the rolnd and oblong ostrich tufts, mounted On stems, and the beautiful ostrich flowers with ceAtres of knotted silk, or iridescent sequins, al io the curled ostrich quills. Quite a new decoration is composed of a high wired ribbon with an edging of stuck ostrich fringe. Stuck ostrich is also arranged in the form of a Malt- ese Cross, a cen.tre composed of beautiful beaded work adding to its attractions. Flowers were never more in evidence; and the favourites for the moment are flat velvet roses with foliage, dahlias, and full-blown roses in silk and velvet. Ribbon bows are much in evidence, but there is 'a difference this year in their arrangement. More loops are used to form a bow, and if any- thing the later is highei than last year.</p>
        <p>The Vogue for Flowers. Flowers real and artificial are used in greater quantities as decorations for the home and. the toilette every year, In all kinds of embroidery, flower designs predominate, and in white work the old-fashioned, well-padded satin stitch has been revived. For this, the new lustre threads</p>
        <p>are exceedingly effective, more especially when cleverly combined with broderie Anglaise. Wild roses are aja2Vourite-deSigri, and these are boldly defined, veined with loose stem-stitch, strewn carelessly over a night‘dress satchet, or they may have a slightly raised background of close large cross stitches. This arrangement is decidedly new and very effective. For domestic and church embroideries there is a vogue for flowers in applique work; all sorts of materials being used for this purpose, including such unlikely fabrics as kid and leather. Trimmings of all kinds are effectively made use Of by the thrifty and skilful embroidress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Statues of Women. Not a little curiosity has been aroused in London with regard. to a. high . hoarding erected north of Pall Mall. Very few of the many pedestrians in Waterloo-place are aware that a new statue of Florence Nightingale. is being erected there. It is to constitute a Part of the nationll tribute to her niagt..ificent life-work, and its erection has necessitated a se-arrange- ment of the memorial to Crimean heroism.- Statues of women are rare in this country, even of Queens. Queen Anne and Queen Victoria are Most honoured in this way. The statue of the former that stands immediately before St. Paul's Cat,hedral is well known, but not so the mena;rials to this last monarch -Of the Stewart line, which have been placed at Queen Anne's Gate, and in Queen's-square, Bloomsbury. Of Que.', Alexandra 7;tele is h beautiful statue Ttthin env grounds of L -,11..!.0n. Hospital, and of Queen Mary there,are several In India, Bc,r enjoying toe distinction of being the first Princess of Wales to visit our Indian Empire.</p>
        <p>The Peasants' Art Fellowship. A number of Men and Women' have banded themselves together with the worthy object of doing their best to counteract the 'materialism in work, so characteristic of this age. In coun- try districts in olden times work was a joy to the worker, and an artistic gratification. To restore this spirit as far as possible Under moderil Conditions is the endeavour of the society 'that bears the name of the Peasant Arts Fellowship. In en unobtrusive fashion its members are doing • good work. They wish to 'instil t love of work for the work's sake, which the era of industrialism has killed. At present work is a means for earning a livelihood, and asi money is its only reward, it is often scamped •in order to crowd more into a given time.</p>
        <p>Colours and Materials. New ailor-mades in warm materials. are being introduced for the spring, blue serge and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>HOME NOTES</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes and Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Vegetable Soup. Eight onions, four carrots, one turnip, one cabbage, one or two leeks, 3oz. dripping, 6 pints water, 4oz. bread, two potatoes. Prepare the vegetables, and cut them in slices, then wash and dry them. Melt the dripping in a sauce- pan, and fry the vegetables till of a nice brown. Add the water, pepper, and salt, and let it boil without a lid. Boil for half an hour, then sim- mer gently for one hour and a half. Break the bread in small pieces, pour the soup over, and serve. A little tomato sauce may be added before serving.</p>
        <p>Simple Food. A great variety of foods at one meal exerts a potent influence in creating a love of eating, and is likewise a great temptation to over-eat. Let us have well-cooked, nutritious, and palatable food, and plenty of it; variety from day to day, but not too great a variety at each meal. The prevalent custom of loading the table with a great number of viands upon occasions when guests are to be entertained in our homes is one to be deplored, since it is neither conducive to good health nor necessary to good cheer, but, on the contrary, is so laborious and expensive a practice that many are debarred from social in- tercourse because they cannot afford to enter- tain after the fashion of their neighbours. • Upon this subject a well-known writer fias aptly said: "Simplify cookery, thus reducing; the cost of living, and how many longing indi- viduals would thereby be enabled to afford themselves the pleasure of--culture and social intercourse! When the barbarous practice of stuffing one's guests shall have been ,abolished, a social gathering will not• Then imply, as it does now, hard labour, expensive outlay, and dyspep- sia. Perhaps when that time arrives we shall be sufficiently civilised to demand pleasures of a higher sort. True, the entertainments will then, in one sense, be more costly, as culture is harder to 'come by than cake. Beets, Baked or Cold. If crisp and fresh they need not stand in fresh water. If the least wilted or tough, put them in cold water until tender, then scrape</p>
        <p>them thoroughly, and cut into thin slices. Put some butter into a baking dish, and let it melt —say, a tablespoonful of butter to ten small, young beets. Then put in the beets, and salt and pepper them. Shake up about every ten minutes till the butter and seasoning get well worked into the beets, and bake in a moderate oven for two hours. After the first hour do not touch the beets; the shaking up is all to be done in the early stages of the cooking. Cold boiled beets diced make a delicious salad, the mayon- naise combining tastily with the sweet beets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER. zi „)tz Conducted by UNCLE NED. cvsiYaKvArb-t,</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER. zi „)tz Conducted by UNCLE NED. cvsiYaKvArb-t,</p>
        <p>FIRST VICTORIA CROSS.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN,-</p>
        <p>:).340Se'o (</p>
        <p>Last week I told you about the first Iron Cross, and now I think you would like to hear about the first Victoria Cross.</p>
        <p>When King George presents this dearly-prized medal to soldiers who are actually "in the field," he may to some extent have been influ- enced by the original order, which provides that under certain conditions the decoration shall be conferred " on the spot where the act to be rewarded by the grant of stich decoration has been performed." The first presentation of the Victoria CrOss, however, took place in Hyde Park in Jule, 1857, when Queen Victoria with her own hinds pinned it on the coats of 62 Crimean heroes. It is recorded that the Queen wore a red and white feather at the side of her round hat, "a scarlet body made nearly like a military tunic," and a dark blue skirt. She was on horseback, with Prince Albert on her left, who made a deep bow to each wearer of the V.C.</p>
        <p>I will now tell you about the origin of an- other German joke—the Goose Step! A Paris paper gives an amusing explanation of the manner in which the goose-step was introduced into the Verman army. Every year, at the ap- proach of Christmas, immense float; of geese were dispatched from Pomerania to Berlin. In order to save money the geese made the journey on' foot. To protect them against the rigours of the road the geese had their feet dipped in glue and covered with fine gravel. By the time this operation had been repeated a dozen times the geese had acquired semi-artificial feet, which they roised with a -jerky, staccato movement :et-ay time they started walking. One -day the kaiser met a flock of these Christmas victims on the march, and he wa4 so delighted with the spectacle presented by the feathered tribe that he ordered his soldiers to copy them. Shortly after the goose-step was introduced into the German army. What would you think. of King George if he did anything so silly? Your loving UNCLE NED..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>THOUGHT FOR THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>THOUGHT FOR THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>"Every kind deed we do is only a deposit in the heavenly bank, my dear."—Cuarie YoRKE.</p>
        <p>PLENTY OF PRACTICE.</p>
        <p>Stage Manager: "Remember, Bangs, we are depending on your baby to cry lustily in the</p>
        <p>third act. Do you think he'll do his part?" Actor Father: "He ought to. He's been prac- tising every night for months!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>The proof of the pudding mab, also be in the amount left over.</p>
        <p>It costs more to revenge injuries than to bear them.—Bishop T. ,Wilson. A life with the one desire to do,good finds no spare room for discontent.—W. Stewed Itlyston. • A few more smiles of silent sympathy, a few more tender words, a little more restraint on temper, may make all the difference between happiness and half-happiness to those I live witl..—Stopford Brooke.</p>
        <p>Plenty of Cats. Husband: Holy Moses! What has happened? Wife: What do you think! The cat has eaten every bit of the meal that I have just cooked! Husband: The poor thing! But never mind; I will get you another cat. All She Possessed.</p>
        <p>• Mrs. Taylor was away and Mary Ann was ill. Therefore upon Mr. Taylor devolved the duty of dressing five-year-old Marjorie. 'For two hours he wrestled with the toughest problem he had ever encountered. " Going " was slow and arduous. By the time he reached the shoe stage he had become exasperated. "Those," he commanded, "you shall put on yourself !"</p>
        <p>Of course she promptly endeavoured to put the left shoe on to her right foot.</p>
        <p>"Marjorie," he roared, don't you know any better than that? You're putting your shoes on the wrong feet!" - - -</p>
        <p>Then the -tiny child burst into a flood of tears.</p>
        <p>"I tan't help it, dady!" she-Cried. "Dey's all de foots I dot!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>"AND OBLIGE HIS MOTHER."</head>
        <p>"AND OBLIGE HIS MOTHER."</p>
        <p>A mother sent this somewhat satirical note to the teacher of her small son: "Pardon me for calling your attention to the fact that you have. pulled Johnnie's right ear until it is getting longer than the other. Please pull his left ear for a while, and oblige his mother."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>CRIME OF BLINDNESS.</head>
        <p>THE CRIME OF BLINDNESS.</p>
        <p>In China it is ,considered a crime to be blind. In the Blind Boys' School in Foochow City is a little blind boy "who, just because he was blind, was tied up to a tree in the jungle to be eaten by the wild beasts. Someone heard him crying and rescued him." "Another ,boy, whose father was drowned, and whose mother was burnt to death, because he was blind, was htinded over to the beggars, who made him sit in the filthy street all day long begging. If at the end of the day he had not gathered twenty. cents he dared not return to the wretched place called home, as the beggars would beat him. Poor little fellow! From the age of three until he was nine this was his' daily life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>MAKE YOUR OWN SUNSHINE.</head>
        <p>MAKE YOUR OWN SUNSHINE.</p>
        <p>Jacky Adams was an office boy, and not a very fortunate one—at least as far as outside appearances were anything. to go by. For 'Jacky's master was a morose, hard-faced, rough-voiced man, who, seemed to take little notice of Jacky except to scold him. When Jacky was not sweeping( or dusting the office, or running errands, he was set to copying or adding up long columns of figures, and this he had to do in a tiny, close, dark room, in which there was scarcely space to turn round. Some- times he had nothing at all to do, except fo wait in this little room until he was wanted for something else. And you might have tifought that a very dreary task indeed if you had seen the room, and the sooty, dark little back street into which its small window opened.</p>
        <p>No doubt you are thinking that Jacky must have been a miserable boy, and that I am going to tell you something very sorrowful about him. But that is not the case at all. Perhaps there never was a happier boy than Jacky Adams. And what I have to tell you about him is not sad, but is full of gladness and joy. Perhaps this seems curious. But the fact was that Jacky's heart was just as full of sunshine as his little office room was devoid of it.</p>
        <p>The way Jacky worked things out was this. When it poured with rain outside he said to</p>
        <p>himself what a good thing it was to be indoors, and sheltered from the storm. When he was running errands he thought ho Ny fine it was to be earning his daily bread, and at the same time enjoying fresh air and healthful exercise. When he was busily bending over his desk he knew that it was splendid to be busy, so that no mischief might he found for idle hands of his to do. And when he was unoccupied he took the opportunity of drawing from his pocket a book, and making the most of the •precious minutes at his command. For this sensible boy always had some helpful book at hand, so that time which might very easily have been wasted was turned to good account. - Jacky lived to become a rich and famous, and better still, a much-loved man; thus reap- ing a great reward for his sunshiny industry and smiling patience. Though we cannot all become rich and famous, we can try to carry out Jacky's rule of life, and learn to "soar above" the little clouds which now and then darken our days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Coiffures.</head>
        <p>Coiffures.</p>
        <p>Good taste demands just now a simple and quiet style of hairdressing. One of the newest and prettiest coiffures has a short side .parting, behind which the hair, rises sontewhat on • the crown of theli.ead. Around the sides and at the back it is softly swathed, the shape of the temples and the natural contour of, the neck and head being clearly visible. Strands of hair are interlaced at the bacl, and stand out somewhat from the head. Another coiffure suggesting the Pompadour style for the hair is prettily waved and drawn softly ,back, at the sides: Young girls in their early teens who possess long, broad foreheads, are' also turning their hair hack in Pompadour fashion, the sides being brought a little forward just above the -temples. The whole of the hair.is drawn to the back and held together by a clasp or slide from which it falls in a kind of tail.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>Old ,French Dance Programmes.</head>
        <p>Old ,French Dance Programmes.</p>
        <p>Carnets de bal as seen in collections of old French curios, were holders for the dance pro- gramme of Court beauties-, anit were extrava- gantly splendid. Not unlike a cigarette case of the present day, or the card cases of a genera- tion ago, they were often ornamented on the outside with exquisite miniatures framed in diamonds, and with inscriptions also in precious stones. The Carnet de "'oat when opened dis- closes ivory tablets fastened by gold or jewelled buttons, and a tiny pencil of gold. Not 'a few are equipped with mirrors and scent receptacles for powder, rouge, patches or breath perfumes. Some of the cases are a veritable lace work of perforated ivory, and when: miniature land- scapes, figures or portraits are used as decora- tions, they are always the work of painters of note, such as .Sicacradi, Wester, II uet, Carl Vernei,, or Cosway.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful Information in Popular</p>
        <p>Language.</p>
        <p>Bright, If Normal. One fact they discovered—and proved—is that there is no such thing as a " lazy " child. The normal child is interested, alert, bright-minded, full of .waking curiosities about the world into which he has come, and endowed with an al- most unquenchable physical vivacity. What then is the matter with the little inert, stupid, dull-eyed creatures for ever at the bottom of the class, who never put a question or seem to pass a given point? The matter is that they are ill. Some of them are hungry, and it is as hopeless to expect a child to study well when he needs food as it would be to expect a fire that needs fuel to burn well. The hunger may, be due to an actual lack of food or it may be that the teeth are in such poor condition that the child cannot chew and therefore does not digest. Or it may be that the food offered to it at home is entirely un- suited to its needs.</p>
        <p>Progressive Enlightenment. Everybody who has been brought into inti- mate contact with the people cannot help recognising what a powerful influence general education .is having in bringing about the de- cline in mortality and in sickness. As an in- stance of this better knowledge oinhe part of the people, one would mention the fact that the back-to-back courtyard houses of this city are every year becoining more difficult to let, while the demand for decent suburban dwellings is in- creasingly great. Again, it is much more diffi- cult, and in some cases almost impossible, to let houses with pail closets or privies, while formerly such houses were tolerated and thought to be reasonably healthy. A very large number of other instances might be given to bear out the general contention that it is in- creased knowledge on the part of the people which is inducing them to seek for better houses and better surroundings."—Dr. Robertson, medical officer of health for Birmingham, who adds that it is not quite so evident, but equally true, that there is progressive enlightenment in all matters which make for improved health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>BOY AND HIS WAYS.</head>
        <p>THE BOY AND HIS WAYS.</p>
        <p>When you hear a fearful racket Like a miniature cyclone, With some sounds so strange that surely Their like was never known, ' While the mother listens calmly, Even with a smiling face, You may know that it is nothing But the boy about the place.</p>
        <p>When there's sawdust on the carpet, And some shavings on the beds; • When the rugs are tossed in corners,. And your chairs stand on their heads; While, if a tool you're needing, you All round the house must raze, You may know he's milking 1 something, Is the boy about the place.</p>
        <p>When the house is full of sunshine On the darkest kind of day; And you have to laugh at seeing Some outlandish boyish play; And when eyes so bright and loving Oft are raised to meet your face, You will, pray, I know, God bless him; Bless our boy about the place.</p>
        <p>A Wrinkle For Making Delicious Puddings and Pastry</p>
        <p>use Shredded ATORA Beef Suet—always at hand ready to pour straight from the packet into the flour, without chopping. Better than raw suet, and goes further. Ask your grocer for it; refuse substitutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>o:ljurrij</head>
        <p>o:ljurrij</p>
        <p>ELECTRIC LIGHT FOR ST. JAMES'.</p>
        <p>In the current issue of the St. James' (Latch- ford) Parish Magazine, the Vicar (the Rev. H. B. Firth) announces that the next object the parishioners are to be asked in assisting to accomplish is the instalment of electric light into the church, and that the proceeds of the next annual sale of work will be devoted to that highly desirable improvement. The last sale of work, he adds, yielded a profit of £l2O—X9O of which has been placed to the credit of the St. Hilda's Building Fund, £2O going to the sewing parties to provide material to work into articles for the next sale, and £lO to the churchwardens to Meet special expenses incurred during the past year.</p>
        <p>BEER, TOBACCO, AND .I‘,IIFSE-</p>
        <p>MENTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Vicar of St. James' and Lenten</head>
        <p>Vicar of St. James' and Lenten</p>
        <p>In his Parish Magazine for the nyesent month the Rev. Ti. B. Firth, vicar 'Of St. James', Latchford, makes a strong appeal to -his Parish- ioners to abstain during Lent from the use of all alcoholic beverages, smoking, and from at- tending places of amusement. "Will you, who- ever you happen to be," he writes, "resolve. that Lent shall. be a time Tvi-n you will as far as lies in your power, c.ricentrate your atten- tion upon 'the things that are not seen,' but which are eternal? Let there be the revival among us of the spirit of discipline, and for a time let there be a complete detachment from the luxuries and amusements which always occupy too great a space in the national life, and especially at such a crisis in the history of our country as the present. "I would recommend that eyery one, unless advised to the contrary by medical authority, should abstain from the use of alcoholic bever- ageS, that men should refrain from smoking, that people in general should cease from attend- ing places of amusement. The Church of En,,- land, in common with other branches of the Catholic Church, ordains that the forty days of Lent should be kept as days of fasting. If you are a devout son or daughter of the Church you will not lightly disregard this injunction of your spiritual mother.</p>
        <p>, "Let us try to make this Lent a time of spiri- tual revival' in the parish, and let everyone make a good and regular use of the means of grace provided by the Church. I hope that all the services will show visible signs of increased congregations, and that dire communicants of the parish will be more frequent in their recep- tion of the Holy Communion?'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>DRAW THE LINE AT MURDER. . The Quaker View-point of the War.</head>
        <p>DRAW THE LINE AT MURDER. . The Quaker View-point of the War.</p>
        <p>Another of the ,series of addresses on "Christianity and War," arranged by , the Society of Friends, was given in their Meeting House in Buttermarket-street on Sunday even- ing, the speaker this time being Principal J. W. Graham, of Dalton Hall, Manchester. War, the speaker postulated, was a belief in the power of material things, and was an abso- lute denial of the spiritual nature of man, and the maxims of the Sermon on the Mount would not do as the maxims for the strategists of an army. They could not imagine Jesus Christ as a Uhlan or working machine guns to mow down His opponents. The German soldiers' pocket- book was an elaborate and detailed denial of the Golden Rule; it upheld the methods of doing unto others exactly what one would not like others to do unto him. War denied the Brotherhood of Man; it was against the whole cosmic system—against the belief that "we are members of .one and another." Defining the attitude of the Society of Friends, Principal Graham said it must not be supposed that the Society was against all use of force. To say that there was no difference between the aboli- tion of force and the abolition of war was like making no distinction between gambling and reasonable business enterprise, or between wage- earning and slavery. The Friends were not like Tolstoi, who disbelieved in all force; whose positions were all extremes out of touch with real circumstances. Force, the Quaker realised, had a small, a diminishing, and a poor place, but still a place, in human affairs. They used it in dealing with the lunatic, tile criminal, and to some extent the child and the animal; they vere believers in the policeman and in the force of the law, He believed that they of , the Society of Friends were called to national ser- vice, but it as a different kind of service to that about which so much was heard in these days.</p>
        <p>Helpful National Service—But Not Slaughter. He believed that they were called upon to do helpful work for their nation, but on his own part, he would *draw the line for himself, and for his sons who were of military age, at mur- der, at slaughter, at killing. As Friends,, they felt that within themselves there was a Person- ality—a . self which had, indeed, some of the lineaments of Jesus Christ—a Living Being that would be torn, violated and killed by taking a share in slaughter, and so they could not fight. The law of the Loving Christ prevented them taking a share in the present wickedness, even though the call of the nation be strong. The call might be strong, but they felt that they must follow the Higher Law, and although their position might be somewhat anomalous—as the position of a minority must always be—they would hold the trench until the reinforcements of the nations came to their aid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>IN TOUCH WITH THIE INVIOLATE. A Comforting Reflection in Time of War;</head>
        <p>IN TOUCH WITH THIE INVIOLATE. A Comforting Reflection in Time of War;</p>
        <p>An excellent attendance marked an open meet- ing of the Wycliffe P.S.A., held on Sunday afterndon. Councillor J. 'P. Locker presided, and a very helpful address was given by Mr. William Mort, of Lymm. The Rev. T. T. James led the prayer and Mr. J. W. Travis read the lesson. Mr. Fred Fieldhouse gave beautiful renderings of the solos "Through love to light" and "Nazareth," Mr. W. H. Payton accom- panying on the organ. The congregation included the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Peacock).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Beneficial Work.</head>
        <p>Beneficial Work.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, in the coarse of a brief ad- dress, said that when the secretary of the Brotherhood had asked him to take the.chair at that meeting, he felt it his duty to accept the invitation, because he knew what a beneficial work the institution had for years been carry- ing on. (Hear, hear.) They were now all in .for strenuous times, and although that Brother- hood had passed through many experiences and troubles, there had been nothing comparable to that of the present moment. The thought was always with them, and when they entered the house of prayer it came home to them even more, He knew they all offered sincere prayers for the soldiers and sailors who were going through this bitter struggle. He hoped we should eventually prove to the world that Eng- land was the great nation she set herself up to be, even in this war which she had not sought. (Applause.) -- -- A War Caused By One _Man. -</p>
        <p>Mr. Mort, in cdmmencing his address, told the congregation that it had been said one became most eloquent upon the subject one felt most deeply about—and that was the war. They were not setting out with the war in a very optimis- tic manner; he did not say that they as a nation were getting downhearted—(hear, hear)—but six dayE of every week now they ;Were lost in the</p>
        <p>at.,,cphere of a war caused by one 'man. One day each week, however, they could breathe the air of the Lord, and that was something for which, they could be grateful. He had always been astounded by. Christ's th.a•ings with men, for he could not forget that every man was a member .of that great brother- hood of Christians, which extended the length and breadth of the world. "The fool hath said in his heart there is no ,God," it was Written. But it was also written that "the glory of the Lord shall be revealed," and there was in the heart of the Christian believer a consolation which no war could ever shake. Even now ihey were in the midst of miracles, and despite the contraband of war they were in touch with 'forces that were inviolate. Theirs was an agree- ment which could never be torn aside. (Ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Greetings For A Warrior.</head>
        <p>Greetings For A Warrior.</p>
        <p>Mr. B. Farrar (secretary) thanked the chair- man, speaker and soloist for their kind services, and voiced' the Brotherhood's appreciation of the chairman's sentiments' regarding it. . also gave a hearty welcome to a soldier p.s.A.- •ite home' on fiirlough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>TV t() I 'SEFE 'L YEARS.</head>
        <p>TV t() I 'SEFE 'L YEARS.</p>
        <p>Rev. 'F. 'F. James' Work Eulogised at Conuremational Party.</p>
        <p>An eloquent tribute to the two years' faithful, and untiring labour which the liev. T. T. James—pastor of the Wycliffe Congregational ChurCh, Warrington—has just completed, con- stituted a pleasant feature of this year's annual congregational tea-party, which was held on ,Monday evening in the Afgmorial Buildings. About two hundred of the church members sat down to tea, and there were also present several friends from the Elmwood-avenue Church and a number of the Belgian refugees at present residing in the town. After the. repast the gentlemen repaired for a while to the smoking-room. Later, an excel- lent concert was provided by members of the choir andachurch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Year of Loyal Service.</head>
        <p>A Year of Loyal Service.</p>
        <p>The Pastor—who presided—delivered a short address in the course of which he said that they must all be profoundly thankful that they had been enabled \to carry on another year's work through the medium of that church. A. great deal of loyal service had been rendered, and he was very glad that they could meet again in comradeship and fellowship. He wanted to give a very hearty welcome to their friends who were there—friends from a country which had passed through, and which was passing through, a terrible tragedy. He felt sure that their Belgian friends—although per- haps they could not understand the words which were being spoken—would know from the warmth of their welcome how glad the people of Wycliffe were to have them in their midst (Hear, hear, and applause.) Mr. James concluded by saying that-one of the things which they were always grateful for was the splendid programme which the choir annually provided. This, year's was as good as ever; it did them credit. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Entertainers.</head>
        <p>The Entertainers.</p>
        <p>The excellent programme which was then pro- ceeded with certainly- did do them credit, and a feature which specially delighted the audi- ence was. the -:number of rousing patriotic, items which it included. ' The artistes who contri- buted Were: Mrs. Smallshaw, the Misses M. E. Atkinson, A. Hall, Haviour, and E. Hewitt; - Messrs. J. Ainsworth, P. 0. Bailey, A. Davies, Fred Fieldhouse, H. Hawthorn and J. Poole ( solos) ; Miss Hewitt, Mrs. , Epworth, Messrs. Bailey and Epworth; Mists Atkinson, Mrs; Smallshaw, Messrs. Davies and Poole; and Messrs. Ainsworth, Bailey, Fieldhouse and Haw- thorn (quartets). Miss Corney recited in fine style, and "Madame Pat Murphy" (Mr. H. M. Ashton) also created great amusement with " her " short address on "Love and Marriage." Mrs. H. .Haviour earned loud applause with a violin solo, as did Mrs. J. Hawthorn and Mr. W. H. Payton at the conclusion of their piano- forte duet. Mr. Payton accompanied all the songs on the piano.</p>
        <p>Pastor and Mrs. James Congratulated.</p>
        <p>During a pause in the entertainment,</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Wallington (a deacon) congratu- lated Mr.. and Mrs. James, in the name of the church and congregation, upon the successful. completion of two years' labours at Wycliffe.. For some time now, he went on, the pastor had been working amongst them under physical difficulties and under very depressing condi- tions; and the congregation offered him their. heartfelt sympathies. He wished to assure 3.1 r.. James that those who attended the church were very proud of their minister, and fully appre- ciated his ministrations. They were very happy as a church, and there had been every indica- tion that things were making satisfactory pro- gress when they were startled by the declaration of war. That did not cause any cessation of work, but it, had "taken the heart out of it." However, the discourses Which Mr. James had given 'them had been very encouraging and helpful. They hoped the pastor would accept' their encouragement under these very depress- ing conditions, (Applause.) The audience signified their approval of these remarks by .standing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Difficult Days, But Better In Store.</head>
        <p>Difficult Days, But Better In Store.</p>
        <p>_ In a brief reply, the Rev. T. T. James said that, though they had been quite unprepared for them, both his wife and himself ,very much appreciated the sincere good wishes which had been so kindly expressed. The two years which they had spent in Warrington had been very happy ones, and, perhaps, best of all, was the fact that that night Mrs. James and he felt that they knew the people of Wycliffe more. closely and intimately than ever before. These- were difficult days for them all—difficult in church work, difficult in home life, dircult in national fife, and difficult in. respect of their faith. But he trusted they would, by God's help, come through in perfect safety, as a country, 'as a people, and as a church, to better' and more encouraging. times. (Applause.) And the thing that they needed to do just now was to pull together and do their best. He was sure that the future would reveal better things when they came to the end of the war. Let them hope and believe, as a church and as individuals, that God had the best of times in store for them. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>FREE OFFER TO LAMES.</head>
        <p>FREE OFFER TO LAMES.</p>
        <p>To prove the superiority of ELLIS'S PILLS, I will send a free trial packet,post paid. Prepared from the original recipe of a celebrated Nurse, and never fails in restoring regularity and correcting all disorders. Safe, certain and speedy pill that never disappoints. Thousands of letters of thanks testify to their speedy efficacy after all other things have been tried in Each purchaser sends back testimonial, as they afford relief in every instance. 'new is nothing to equal them. Is. 3d. per box. Special pills (reconunended), 4s. od., post paid, in plain wrapper, with full directions and advice. Supplied through the post only by MRS. ELLIS, 27, Surrey Lane, Battersea, London. An inter- esting guide, 48 pages, 50 illustrations, sent free with each 'box. Mention paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 1915.</p>
        <p>OUR LATEST WATER SUPPLY SCHEME.</p>
        <p>River to Augment Appleton for Manufacturing Purposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>VIOLENT SCENES IN THE COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>VIOLENT SCENES IN THE COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Councillors Pemberton and Roberts's Battle of Words.</p>
        <p>Another scheme for the improvement of the water supply of the borough for manufactur- ing purposes occupied the attention of the Warrington Town Council at its monthly meet- ing in the Town Hall on Tue,day evening, and gave rise to a long, and at on stage heated, dis- cussion.'</p>
        <p>Under the scheme, which was put forward by the Water Committee, it is proposed to pump water from the River Mersey and to distribute it to consumers from new works to be con- structed on land adjacent to the Electricity Station. The cost of the scheme is estimated at £11,500, and it is a modification of the one submitted some time ago. It is confidently hoped by those who have had charge of its promotion that, by enabling the Corporation to supply water for trade pur- poses at a reduced rate, the scheme ,will find favour and support at the hands of a sufficient number of the manufacturers of the town to more than warrant the Council in embarking, even in these days of tight money, upon the undertaking. • Some opposition to the scheme was forthcom- ing, but upon a division being taken, only three members of the Council voted against it, and the Water Committee will accoraiiiify-Pried with the„,preliminary measures necessary in order to put it into operation. The opposition was mainly based on the dual contention that adequate guarantees of sup- port from the manufacturers of the district had not been .forthcoming, and that the proposal was premature.</p>
        <p>The matter came before the Council on the rollowing minutes presented for confirmation. by the Water Committee "The committee (at their meeting on January 22nd) fully considered the recommendations of the Water Engineer on the scheme for improving the supply of water for trade purposes, and also the report and analyses of Dr. Thresh on the quality of the river water. It was resolved that the recom- mendations of the Engineer, as supported by Dr. Thresh's report, be approved. . . . It was also resolved that the Engineer be in- structed to prepare the necessary specifications, &amp;c., for plant and appliances for the prOvi- sion of new,works, to be situdfe-on land adjoin- ing the Electricity Station, and on the bank of the River Mersey, and that the Water Com- mittee be empowered to advertise for such plant and appliances at such time as they may determine."</p>
        <p>tity. It would not be fit for drinking purposes, and manufacturers should be instructed to have pure waterworks water laid on their premises. With regard to the public baths, the new supply would be an improvement and would tie as nearly pure as any water, such as river water, could be made; but Dr. Thresh, in his report on it, said, "If it were occasionally imbibed by accident, I think it would have no 'deleterious effect whatever." That statement did not imply an absolute certainty as to freedom from dan- ger. While there was no doubt that the new subply to the baths would be not only more economical, but purer than the Appleton water, and would reduce danger to a minimum, he thought individual bathers should consider that they also had a duty. If they got a good supply of water for bathing purposes, they ought to consider the safety of others by being cleanly themselves; they should abstain from the dangerous habit of spitting in the water; they should see that they were fairly clean before they engaged in public bathing.</p>
        <p>every phase of improvement they were attempt- ing in Warrington. Alderman BENNETT : I don't think that is quite fair. Alderman TINNION : Alderman Bennett said he would not like to swim in the river water, but I have a recollection of his attending a meeting of a committee for the consideration of a system of purification of sewage, and he had such confidence in the scheme that he actually drank some of the effluent. (Laughter.) I don't think he will deny it. I personally should not mind swimming in the water that I was prepared to drink. (Laughter.) The speaker'went on to say that one objection he personally had to the scheme proposed was that it did not provide for the smaller manu- facturers. (Hear; hear.) It was a scheme for the big people—the people who were big enough to look after themselves and could pump from the liter for their own purposes, as indeed many of them already did. He did not object to the principle of the scheme, but Ile felt that the charges proposed made too great a distinc- tion between the smaller and the larger con- sumers. While in general agreement with the principle that municipal undertakings should contribute something to the rates, he rather' objected to the Water Committee objecting to making a profit out of what he considered was one of the essentials to sanitation and decent, clean living. (Hear, hear.) It was necessary that they should have their water as pure and cheap as they could get it. He was not so sure as some people appeared to be that the water below the Weir was of as good a quality as that above it; indeed, he was rather of the opinion that the soft water from .the upper reaches of the Mersey was much better and purer than the water in the tidal part of the river could possibly be, and that, was one' great objection he had to the scheme. From his own know- ledge he could assure the Council that they would experience much greater difficulty in getting water that was suitable for manufac- turing purposes if they put their works below the Weir than they would do if :they 'had them above the Weir. He propoSed no amendment, because he thought the scheme should be a commercially sound one and because the water could be taken from above and, probably, below the Weir of sufficient good quality and in suffi- cient quantity to be. a great boon to manufac- turers. One advantage that would accrue from the new scheme would be that they would be able to get a more adequate supply of water for street watering purposes; indeed the scheme had many advantages, although, of course, it also had its disadvantages. For the making of beer—(laughter)—he believed the Winwick water was grand; in 'fact one of. the finest in the world; whilst' it was also 'excellent for domestic purposes; and if they could' make the supply they proposed to give to the .manufac- turera as good, from their point of view, as the Winwick water was, from the domestic point of view, and fix the price at a reasonable figure, the new scheme should be a great suc- cess. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>LATEST SCHEME.</head>
        <p>THE LATEST SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Alderman JOHN EVANS, in submitting the com- mittee's minutes, explained in detail the pro- posed scheme and the steps which led to its being formulated. The area of the town's, water supply, he said, was 65 square miles, reaching from Bedford Leigh on the north side to Preston Brook' on the south, Cadishead on the east, and Burtonwood on the west. The domestic requirements were supplied from the Pumping stations at Winwick and Delph-lane Ivith a water of exceptional quality. The 'ants of the manufacturers were met by draw- ing from the impounded reservoir at Appleton. For about six months ins' the year there was more than sufficient water impounded at Apple- ton to meet their requirements, but during the other portion of the year the demand was Con- siderably supplemented from the domestic sup- ply, which, he thought, was not advisable. The demand for both domestic and manufacturing purposes was now about three times as great as it was five years ago, and conseeptently- it be- hoved the committee to look ahead. Therefore 'UV had decided to recommend to the Council the adoption of the scheme of supplementing the manufacturers' supply from tile fiver. This scheme had been under the consideration of the committee for several years, and was explained to the Council some time ago, but the apathy of the manufacturers did not justify The com- mittee in proceeding with the scheme as then proposed. They now came with ft revised scheme and also a revised list of charges. In the original scheme it was proposed to abstract the water from the reach above ,the Weir, and convey it through a conduit to the station to be erected alongside the Electricity Station, where it would have been treated and pumped into the Appleton main. The present scheme pro- vided for the taking of the water from below the</p>
        <p>Weir. An equal quantity of compensation water must be returned above the Weir. Dur- ing the last twelve months a periodical analysis 'had been made of samples of water taken above and below the Weir, and it had been found that -the water in the lower reach was as good as, if not better than, that in the upper reach. There- fore, compensation pumping would not be re- quired, and the initial cost would be reduced by over .R 2,000 and the working by over 00 per ,annum.</p>
        <p>The estimated cost of the scheme as proposed was £11,500. As to the revised scale of charges, when the Engineer's report was written the loss ,on the first year's working was estimated to be .about .2300. Since that time, owing to the -application for, and promises to take more, water for manufacturing purposes, the loss had been converted into a profit. It was also sug- gested that the water supplied to the public baths should be reduced 50 per cent. in price, and for street-watering purposes 25 per cent., and that 7f per cent 7 discount should be allowed on certain of the charges. On behalf of the Water Committee, he submitted that, scheme with -wiry great confidence, as the best method -of preserving a very valuable supply of water for domestic purposes, and also of providing an increased supply for manufacturing purposes at comparatively small cost, thereby deferring -for a considerable time the heavy cost of pro- viding an extended scheme for domestic use. Councillor LINDLEY seconded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>DOCTOR'S WARNIN6s.</head>
        <p>A DOCTOR'S WARNIN6s.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY MAYOR (Councillor Joseph) said that whilst he could support the proposals of the committee "in an almost unqualified man- ner" he would like to know whether it was proposed to stirilise the water, lie would like to give one word of warning to ,manufacturers, and that was that the water would not be abso- lutely free from danger if drunk in any quan-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>iiiialliiii........._ --_____ _;., .</head>
        <p>iiiialliiii........._ --_____ _;., .</p>
        <p>( 01 •NCILLOR GOI .(;II AND THE "CUNNING COMMITTEE."</p>
        <p>The May Objections.</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH moved an amendment to the effect that the matter be considered six .months hence, but upon the Mayor pointing out that he was not in order in doing so, altered his amendment to one that the scheme be re- ferred back. To his mind, he said, the scheme was premature, and those who put it forward were ill-advised. At the present juncture the scheme was suggestive of a waste of public money. If ever there was a time when the Cor- poration should hesitate about spending .220,000 on a scheme that was not likely to prove pro- fitable, surely it was the present time, when the greatest war in the, history of the world was being fought, and no one could give a remote idea of when the end would be. There was a great demand for money,, and higher rates of interest were being asked. The Water Com- mittee, during the past 14 years had gained the reputation of being public spendthrifts, not worthy to be trusted with a great scheme of municipal trading. (The Mayor rang his bell, but Councillor Gough pursued his way un- heeding.) They reduced one of the most profit- able municipal trading assets the Corporation owned to a state of municipal bankruptcy. Take, for example, the Lawson contract. That ,was let at 0224,000, and some of the committee actualb- helped the contractor to make a claim against the Corporation for .272,000. It would be interesting to learn who was the father of .the present scheme. (Laughter.) He sub- mitted that there Inid been n demand by manu- facturers for the water. Most of the large firms were able to get their own water from the river, and were working it more Cheaply than the Corporation could supply them. The sup- porters Of the scheme had been cunning enough to reveal only half the truth.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Don't say "cunning," Councillor Gough. I am on that committee, and I won't allow that.</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH : I think you had better hear me further.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: I am not going to have you use the word "cunning." It is not good emiugh, and it is not fair at all.</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH went on to say that the Engineer had not submitted details as to capi- tal charges or the amount of land required and the cost. Moreover, the Council had never de- cided that the site selected was the best.or most suitable for the scheme. No amount was named in the report for renewals, upkeep, or working the plant. The promoters were ask- ing the Council to take another leap in the dark. He challenged any of the supporters of the scheme to show on paper how it was going to be made profitable to the Corporation. The amendment found no seconder, and, con- sequently, fell to the ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>POLLUTING RIVER.</head>
        <p>POLLUTING RIVER.</p>
        <p>A Deadly Sin, Says Alderman Bennett.</p>
        <p>Alderman 11ENNETT said that, in a way, the scheme commended itself. to him, for he felt that it was absolutely vital to the commercial success of the town that it .should have a good and cheap supply of water for the use of the manufacturers. At the same time, he realised that there was something to be said for the other point of view, viz.; that at this stage in the country's history they ought not to spend anything on schemes ,that could be post- poned. The report of the. specialist, Dr:Thresh, did not seem to be very reassuring. He- (the al(lerman) had been . living under a very pleasant delusion- for Some time past, and it was that the quality of the water of the river Mersey had so greatly improved that it was now almost innocuous. However, he found from Dr. Thresh's report that it was still very bad; indeed lie said that the water was very impure. Evidently there was considerable pollution of the river. He had thought that all tho authori- ties above' the Weir had been compelled to purify their sewage and to turn out something like a clear effluent. It seemed to him, if the report was correct, that either they were not carrying out the law, and the authorities needed bringing to book before the Joint Committee for the Watershed of the Rivers Mersey and Irwell, or else that Warrington itself had been contributing a very considerable -proportion of the pollution. That Corporation had always shielded themselves behind a legal immunity concerning towns on a tidal estuary, but such a law was absolutely barbarous and a disgrace to any civilised community. In mentioning that he was of course facing the possibility of a much greater expenditure than they dared contemplate, but the one logical outcome was that they and every other town ought to be made to treat their sewage; indeed it ought to be made, one of the deadly sins to pour con- tamination into such a stream as the Mersey: In spite of what Dr. Thresh said in his report about bathing, he (the -Alderman) must con- fess that he', fond of swimming as he was, would have some hesitation about disporting himself in the fiellucid product of all the filtra- tion of water taken from below the Weir. (Laughter.) -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>NO PROFITS FROM HEALTH</head>
        <p>NO PROFITS FROM HEALTH</p>
        <p>ESSENTIALS.</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion's</p>
        <p>A lderman TINNION said that there was not a sewage outfall within a quarter of a mile from the place from which it was proposed to draw the water for the purposes of the present scheme. That outfall, moreover, was below and not above the point. He did not know why Alderman Bennett should continually, in season and but of season, cry "Stinking fish" in Warrington.' It seemed to him that if it w,are possible for the alderman to deOry War- rington, or the work of any Council comiAttee, he invariably took the opportunity of doing so. Fortunately or unfortunately, probably fortu- nately, the alderman was not a member of many of the committees, and therefore he could not give them the benefit of his advice. 4itut he came there publicly, always to cry "stinking fish" with regard to every undertakirg and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>SPLENDID SCHEME.</head>
        <p>A SPLENDID SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Councillor OSBORNE said it was • time some- thing was done in the direction proposed, and he was satisfied from the information he had received from the committee that the scheme at present before the Council was considered to be the best possible, and that it was one that should prove of considerable utility and advantage to the town. With regard to the supply to the public baths, some members of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee, knowing the water of the Mersey, had an idea that not only would the bathers run a risk of being poisoned, but they would even have some difficulty in seeing themselves if that water were used. (Laughter.) He was, however, assured by the Chairman and the Engineer that, for the moment, it was not intended to use that water for the baths. Until they could show a clean bill of health for the river water they would continue to get their supply from else- where. If the 50 per cent. reduction in the charge for water suggested were accompanied by other similar economies, the committee would probably be able to reduce the charges to bathers. He thought the scheme had been splendidly worked out, and that considerable credit was due to those who had had it in hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>VIOLENT SCENE.</head>
        <p>A VIOLENT SCENE.</p>
        <p>Councillors Pemberton and Roberts Beat Recent Records.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON said that although the Council hitd been warned that the Water Com- mittee were going to do something to augment the supply at Appleton, he had not the slight- est idea that they would be asked, at such short notice, to sanction a scheme out and out. Personally, he was not quite certain that the scheme submitted was the best one, and he would have been better satisfied if more minute attention had been given to the possibility of utilising the great quantity of water which at present was allowed to run to waste at Apple- ton. When they constructed a reservoir there was practically only the initial cost, for the water would run down the pipes for ever after- wards; whereas if they went in for pumping they were face to face with a constantly recur- ring cost. One poipt which struck him very forcibly in connection with the scheme sub- mitted by the committee was that, as yet, they had no certainty that the water, when provided, was going to be taken. He thought that was the most vital part of the whole business, and he thought they ought to asCertain where they stood on the point. They had several times made a canvass of the manufacturers; they had tried all they could to get promises from the manufacturers to take this water, but,, so far, the promises had not been forthcoming, and the consequence was that, even if the Council went to the expense- of providing the water, the manufacturers could please themselves whether they took it or not. The Corporation were not legally compelled to supply water for manufac- turing purposes, as they were for drinking pur- poses, and in face of the serious position in which the country was at the present time, he thought they ought to be very, reluctant about spending the money, in constructing, a supply when they did not know whether such supply was going to be taken or not, and he moved that the recommendations of the committee be re- ferred back.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS submitted that as an amendment to the same effect had already been moved by Councillv Gough, it was out of order for Councillor Pemberton to bring forward an- other on the same lines.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said he was advised by the Town Clerk that Councillor Pemberton's amendment was quite in order, even though it was the same as an amendment that had been moved before, and had found no seconder.</p>
        <p>SERIOUCCHARGE-VIGOROUSLY- REPUDIATED.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : I can quite understand Councillor Roberts's action, because 'he repre- sents one of the firms who are so anxious to get this.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: I beg to give that a flat contradiction.'</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : It daresay. Councillor ROBERTS: I ask for the Mayor's protection for a minute. It is too bad. I appeal to the'cliairman of the committee to say- Councillor P=ELION ; I object to him inter-</p>
        <p>rupting. I always have done, and I always shall. I won't have it.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: He is rising to a lint of order.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : I object to him inter- rupting me. He always does it, and I wont have it.</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts again rose to his feet. The MAYOR: On a point of order only, Coun- cillor Roberts, please! Councillor ROBERTS: I appeal to the chairman of the committee to say whether it is not a fact that the committee have known from the first that the firm with which I am connected will have nothing to do with the scheme, because---</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : This ,is not speaking to a point of order.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS (with warmth): Well, your statement is a lie! .</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : That is what you say. You are used to telling them; not me. Councillor ROBERTS: You are cafe of the big- gest liars-</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : The public of War- rington know different.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: I am not going to allow you to say that, I can tell you!</p>
        <p>Councillor PEAIBERTON : I am not going to com- pete with you at lying. You are a past-master at that.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON; In cheek you can beat him.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : WS not cheek; you .have got a cast-iron face—not cheek. (Laugh- ter.) The councillor went on to say that .he agreed with Alderman Tinnion that the scheme was made for the large manufacturers and not for the small men, just as was the electricity scheme. The small men, like the ratepayers, were not considered, and although it was the ratepayers who were responsible for the tre- mendous amount of capital that was put into these public works, • they got nothing out of them. There were hundreds of thousands of pounds worth of capital in the water undertak- ing, and the ratepayers of Warrington hair never got a penny out of it. He would give place to nobody on the Council in the matter of doing everything possible for the manufacturers of the town and to encourage them— Councillor ROBERTS: Rot; absolute rot!</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : It is funny, really, to hear such an interruption, because it is so well known that, day in and day out, I have done everything I could to get manufacturers to come here. I have tried to get them conces- sions, but there comes a question of equity and a time when the little man must be considered, unless you Want to be. considering the man at the top all the time.</p>
        <p>If they were going to lie for ever pulling the small man "behind scratch," where did they expect he was going to be at the finish? Surely the little man ought to be considered. He could not help thinking that the scheme submitted that night ought to be thrashed out in the General Purposes Committee. He wondered what was the reason for rushing the matter before the Council that night. It was ad- mitted that the scheme was at present incom- plete, and what the further scheme was going to cost nobody knew; the total had not yet been estimated, apparently. The thing was being put before them piecemeal, whereas he thought they were entitled to have had the whole scheme before them at the one time. For instance, if they were to go in for a softening plant—which was in the original scheme, and which was cut out because it made the total cost look too big a figure to be swallowed at once—that would cost another £20,000. What was the use ol a report from a gentleman wh,b said that the water, when treated, would, in his opinion, be suitable for steam-raising and cooking pur- poses---</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MERSEY WATER FOR COOLING NOT</head>
        <p>MERSEY WATER FOR COOLING NOT</p>
        <p>COOKING.</p>
        <p>Alderman Jonx EVANS : I understand that that "cooking" is a misprint. It ought to fead "cooling." _(Laughter.) The Alderman ftirther pointed out that the expert gave it as his opinion that the water, when sterilised, could be used with safety in the swimming bath, as any small amount that might be swallowed would be perfectly harm- less.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : He says that if it is imbibed by accident he " thinks " it would have no deleterious effect. He only thinks, and I' am not going to trust my life to a man who only thinks a thing is not going to kill you. (Laughter.) I like a man who says "It will not kill you." (More laughter.) Surely, the wisest thing the Water Committee could do would be to with- draw thtir recommendation until they had found out whether it would be possible to get sufficient customers to make the scheme pay. Failing that he would propose as an amend- ment that the scheme be referred back, with a view to full discussion by the General Purposes Committee. Councillor Lashford seconded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS EXPLAINS.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS EXPLAINS.</p>
        <p>Coun. Pemberton's Statement "An Absolute Invention."</p>
        <p>PUBLIC APOLOGY CLAIMED.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS craved leave to "put a personal matter right, and put it right for ever." Councillor Pemberton had stated, or at any rate lie had understood him to state, that he (the speaker) was one of the strongest sup- porters of the scheme because it would be to the interests of the firm with which he .was connected that the scheme should go on. That statement was an absolute invention; thee was not the shadow of a shade of justification for its being made. It was a deliberate false- hood. He said, unhesitatingly and with a full knowledge of the responsibility which the words carried, that no man in that Council Chamber had ever more deliberately concocted a fa)sehood with intent to damage his personal reputation and to show a miserable pecuniary interest as the reason why he the speaker) supported a public scheme of that kind than had Councillor Pemberton that night. Be vas only sorry that Mr. Pemberton hag not made the statement somewhere else, so z hat he could have had the opportunity of pravinz it, or having it disproved. The prim:pie of the scheme had been befiire the. Council on previous occasions, and the underlying base of the whole matter Was the desirability of obtain:l,lg ater from the river to supplement the nitin.ifact-ar- ing water supply. That was the principle; everything else was a matter of detail; Fo again he had proved Councillor Pembertoi not to be speaking The truth in regard to the statement he had made to try and damage the scheme..</p>
        <p>"What are the facts?" continued Councillor Roberts. "They are. that Mr.. Gr.l%, firkt of all called my attention to the prolfoution. Ile ad- mitted that he was extremely desirous of ob- taining Messrs. Crosfield as customers. I don't think I have any reason to coneial the Let that we are the largest users if whter of ally firm in Warrington-7-very eonsiderAtily the largest. As you know, we nay... 3 a scheme, of our own, and the. lowest pri:e ti Wilait+x.e have to offer to eonsAniels' to-ni4lLt is cofl. siderably higher than the pri,l2 to us of secur- ing our own water from the river and treating and using it for boiler purpo,es.-</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION : You a.... on the La»lis. Councillor ROBERTS: ; we have a natural advantage in our .stuation. I mennon this not for the purpose of dc 'eying the scheme, but merely for the purpose of proving, if any- body has the slightest dolbt about the. state- ment I made earlier on, that I am not serving any personal interest, or !he •interest of any particular firm, by coming bete tc ;.:pport this proposition', Mr. Gray will bear out what I state.</p>
        <p>Continuing, Councillor Roberts said that when Mr. Gray called upon him oa the matter he (the speaker) raised two objecTions. The first was that .he did not think the Manchester Ship Canal Company wourd ever consent to the scheme, but he was now pleased to find that that company had consented, and that .his) anxiety on that score had been speedily wiped out. The second wls that ra firr. as the firm</p>
        <p>with which he was copnected was concerned, he saw no prospect at all of their becoming customers/ because they could do considelably better for themselves. Those, were the facts— as the chairman of the committee could attest —and he thought he was entitled to a public apology. Mr. Gray would verify i the fact that on two .or three occasions he called at Messrs. Crosfield's to discuss the proposition with them and see if they could not be got in as cus- tomers, and that on each occasion he had had to go away unsatisfied because there was no tempttltion in the proposals he put before the firm. It was not fair that he should have to defend himself against Councillor Pemberton not only that night but on every possible occa- sion when he could possibly make a personal attack. (Hear, hear.) MANUFACTURERS AND COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON.</p>
        <p>Proceeding, Councillor Roberts said Coun- cillor Pemberton had said that he was a friend of the Manufacturers of Warrington. Well, there was a saying that on some occasions one liked to he saved from one's friends. (Laugh- ter.) If there was a man whose name stank in the nostrils of the manufacturers of Warring- ton it was that of Mr.. Pemberton. Let him prove his friendship and they would be glad to4have it, but if he were going to prove it he would have to entirely alter his course of con- duct and, if possible, the constitution of his nature' too. (Laughter.) He (Councillor Roberts) supported the scheme before the Council, in the first place, because he could see no alternative of the manufacturers' 'supply of .water was to be amplified, except, of course, an altlinative which was very much more ex- pensive. They had an area of supply far in excess of their storage capacity; they ran to waste more water than they could take in the Appleton Reservoir. Some time ago a scheme was submitted for the provision of a second reservoir. It was defeated at a time when War- rington lost its head, and when the affairs of the town were in the hands of the biggest blackguards who ever sat on a public body. The Mayor rang his bell, to an accompani- ment of "Oh, oh's." Councillor ROBERTS: I am mentioning no names; but I am mentioning facts. The com- mittee have been careful not to venture on a scheme`which may be subject to the same kind of treatment, and they come forward: to-night with the most carefully considered scheme, from the economic point of view, and/from the point of view of the expenditure oreapital„ that could well have .been. conceived.. The point, the councillor continued, raised: by Alder- man Tinnion as to the treatment of small con- sumers was a very important one, but that was a matter Of detail that could lie subject to revision later on. The town had an excellent supply of water for domestic purposes, and they wanted to conserve that to the fullest possible extent. It -seemed. to him to be in the nature of a sinful waste that Water intended primarily for that purpose should be turned to manufac- turing uses when a water of an inferior char- acter, quite good enough for the purposes of the factories was so readily at hand. Unless they supplemented the present supply of water for manufacturing purposes they would be threatenihg the life of their supply, for domestic water, and would be bringing nearer and nearer the time when they would have to go in for an augmentation of supply which 'would prove to .be a vastly more costly matter than the present scheme. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>NEGOTIATIONS WITH CROSFIELD'S.</head>
        <p>THE NEGOTIATIONS WITH CROSFIELD'S.</p>
        <p>Answering Councillor Pemberton, Alderman JOHN EVANS said that the negotiations with Messrs. Crosfield bad gone through Dr. Arm- strong and not through Councillor Roberts, and that he could corroborate what Councillor Roberts had said about having no knowledge</p>
        <p>of the negotiations. , Councillor PEMBERTON asked that Mr. Gray should be called upon to give his version of what bad taken place.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION did not think it advisable that an official should be introduced into a dis- cussion of that character; it had never been done before. (Hear, hear.) It would place the official in a very awkward position if he had to interfere in a difference of opinion between two members.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : p. question of fact. Alderman TINNION : You can give notice-of the question, and it can be answered in writing. In reply to another question by Councillor Pemberton, the MAYOR (hating consulted the Engineer) said he thought he was quite right in saying that Messrs. Crosfield had been ap- proached, and that Councillor Roberts threw cold water on the scheme right from the com- mencement, so far as the firm were concerned. Since then negotiations had taken place with another member of the firm, who were con- sidering the matter at the present time.</p>
        <p>Councillor FIRTH LINDLEY said Councillor Pemberton had hinted at alternative schemes. If that councillor had such a scheme in view surely it was for him to have brought it for- ward. Councillor PEMBERTON : I am not a water engineer.</p>
        <p>Councillor LINDLEY : But he's a member of the committee.</p>
        <p>Alderman Ram EVANS, in reply to questions, said that the intention of the committee, as expressed in the plans laid before the Coun- cil, was to adopt a system which should be useful at the present time, and would be con- structed in such a form that as necessity arose units could be added to it. Councillor GOUGH said he was at a loss to know whi- the committee were attempting to press this thing through at the present time. He thought that was premature on their part, seeing that they had got no prospective custo- mers, not a single manufacturer having actu- ally made application. t` A VOICE: That's wrong. Councillor BROADHURST said that, after listen- ing carefully to the arguments. on both sides, he could find no reason for voting against the committee's recommendation. He believed that by carrying out the scheme proposed the Coun- cil would be working in the best interests of the small manufacturers of the town, and he certainly thought that the scheme was not premature. He was sorry to see it, but oppo- sition had always come from one quarter of the Council—at least, ever since he had been on it— to' every scheme that made-ior the progress of the town. Why should charges be levelled against this member or that member of the Council, that he was self-interested? Such charges were despicable. If Councillor Pember- „ton ever made a good move in his life—(laugh- ter)—and if he intended to work in the best interests of the small manufacturers, he should not ask the chairman of the Water Committee to withdraw his recommendation, but should, himself, withdraw his amendment. (Hear, hear.) By doing so, he would be making the most sensible move he had made for a long time. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) Councillor ARCHER counselled' postponement, pointing out that the Water Committee pro- Poscd tc? viabArk 911 PVchme which was of enormous consequence to ihe town without knowing whether it would turn put right or wrong, and without any guarantees from any manufacturers that they would take the water even if it were supplied. He thought the least they could de would be to first approach the manufacturers.</p>
        <p>Councillor Fox sought an assurance that the water supply for domestic. purposes‘would not be jeopardised for the benefit of the manufac- turers.</p>
        <p>Alderman JOHN EVANS replied that the supply at AVinwick up till now had been quite ade- quate, and that there had been more water at Appleton than they wanted, and 'that it had been allowed to go to waste owthg to the diffi- culty or impounding it. A scheme that was got out for impounding it was estimated to cost about £170,000.</p>
        <p>Upon being put, the amendment to refer the matter back to the Water Committee found only three supporters—Councillors Lashford, Pemberton and Goukh—and the committee's recommendations were therefore adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>500-IEAR-OLD MEETING PLACE.</head>
        <p>500-IEAR-OLD MEETING PLACE.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett's Prcposal to Restore Ancient Open Space</p>
        <p>FAILS TO FIND A SECONDER.</p>
        <p>At Tuesday's meeting of tli-e--Town Council, Alderman Bennett pleaded for the restoration of an open space in the heart iof the town—a space which, he said, for five hundred years constituted 'a place of meeting for the inhabit- ants, and which, to-day, ringed round with shops, could be made attractive to the-towns- people and a lucrative business site, whilst', at the same time, helping to relieve the cofiges- fion of traffic which, more particularly on Sat- urday nights, is at present experienced in the vicinity of Market Gate. His suggestion was that the present market shed should be demol- ished and the site of it left open, thus forming a fairly spacious public square, round the sides of which shops could be erected and the centre of which, to use his own words, could be "treated with some degree of ornamentation."</p>
        <p>The Alderman's suggestion was made by way of an amendment to the report of the Markets Committee, who reported that at their meeting on January 4th the Borough Surveyor sub- mitted to them plans of the proposed laying out of the fish market (which the Alderman desig- nated "the market shed") for butchers', cheese factors and other miscellaneous businesses which will be displaced on the demolition of the Market 'Hall, with the result that they re- solved to appoint a sub-committee to interview the tenants on the matter. That sub-com- mittee, the committee further reported, duly interviewed the tenants, and witiciffie excep- tion they agreed to the sites proposed for their stalls in the fish market, the Borough Surveyor being subsequently instructed to obtain tenders for the new stands. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>IF MARKET HALL GOES, WHY NOT THE</head>
        <p>IF MARKET HALL GOES, WHY NOT THE</p>
        <p>MARKET SHED':</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett, having drawn attention to the foregoing minute*, announced that it was in regard to the preservation of the old Market Shed and the proposed transformation of if into al place that would serve some such purpose as was at present served by the Market Hall that he more particularly wished to say a few words. At the outset he was against moving the' Market Han, but eventually, against his own inclina- tions, he became reconciled to its removal, and his regret now was that the Council did not go in for making a really good job of the business whilst they were about it. If they could dis- pense with the Market Hall, which cost £12,000 or £13,000, it was surely quite easy to dispense with the Market Shed also, and the doing away with it would afford them the opportunity of making a noble improvement in the centre of the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>AN OPEN SPACE FOR 500 YEARS.</head>
        <p>AN OPEN SPACE FOR 500 YEARS.</p>
        <p>The site of the Market Shed was an open space for five hundred years. All the old pic- tures depicted it as such, and made him feel what a grand thing it would be to revert to the old state of things and have an open space in the heart of the town. Such a space would do much to relieve the constant crowding about Market Gate and the Saturday night conges- tion; indeed, it would come as a tremendous relief to have an open. space in such close proximity to the spot where the congestion chiefly took place. They were afforded a unique opportunity of, once and for all, concentrating the whole of the Market premises under one roof, and of restoring to the town its old place of meeting. The opportunity was one such as did not often occur in a town</p>
        <p>like Warrington, which was too densely occu- pied with buildings to be really healthy or at- tractive. It would even be possible to make the site into a beauty spot, and he was certain it could be transformed into a very eligible site, for business premises—shops-could be built round it, and the centre could be treated with sowe degree of ornamentation. In a word, it could be made into a spot to which they could take visitors with a good deal of pride, and, at the same time, could be put to very practical use. If the Market Hall went, bethought the shed should go likewise, and he moved that the recommendations dealing with it be referred back to the committee.</p>
        <p>The Alderman's proposal found no seconder.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WOMAN'S TERRIBLE DEATH.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S TERRIBLE DEATH.</p>
        <p>Burnt Whilst Toasting Bread at</p>
        <p>Earlestown:</p>
        <p>An inquest was held by Mr. F. Brikhouse at the Warrington Police Station on Monday, upon the body of Sarah Jane Unsworth, 59 years, of 48, Derby-street,* Earlestown,- Ilho died on Thursday of last week from burns. Wm. Unsworth, general labourer, 'husband of the deceased, said his wife had enjoyed good health up to 12 months ago, when she suffered from bionchitis. About 5 p.m. on Wednesday, January 27th, he was in bed, when he heard his wife screaming downstairs. He hurried down to the kitchen and .saw his wife at the front door with her clothing all in flames. "He wrapped a coat round her and extinguished the flames: Dr. Elliott, of Earlestown, was called in almost immediately, and found that the woman was badly burned about the body. The injuries were attended to, and she was removed to the Warrington Infirmary in the ambulance at 9.45 p.m. Witness was present at the Infirmary when his wife died there on Thursday at 7.30 p.m. During a conversation he had with hi, wife as to how the accident happened, she told him that she was in front of the fire holding some bread on a knife and was kneeling on the fender when her apron saught fire. In her ex- citement she ran' into the street. Her clothes were almost destroyed by the fire.. She was wearinea flannelette underskirt at the time. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death," and expressed their sympathy with the relatives of the deceated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NEW BOOKS.</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKS.</p>
        <p>li. • . 7 .1". z I• • 11). •."4 PINE FOREST ~ IN EVERY HOME. Strenp efl • IheTifitOM, ,CHEST</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>1815 AND 1915.</head>
        <p>1815 AND 1915.</p>
        <p>Just as the Corsican pressed on the Continen- tal System, the conquest of Spain, and the in- vasion of Russia at one and the same time, so, too, the Kaiser, like him a prey to pride and megalomania, strives to grasp both Calais and Cairo.</p>
        <p>Even now, his Oriental' schemes should not be underestimated. Germany has long been work- ing through secret agents in North Africa, and if she succeeds in finding a true descendant of the Prophet, or a wonder7working Mandi, she may cause trouble in Egypt. True, the prece- dent of Napoleon's profession of Islam is not encouraging to his imitator; and it might not be amiss for us to adopt a device of Sir Sidney Smith: When off Acre, he distributed to the Christian tribes• of the Lebanon Bonaparte's Moslem proclamations to the Sheikhs-Or Cairo. Similarly, translations of the Kaiser's sermons on the Hohenzollern, or of his addresses to Christian pilgrims at Jerusalem, might temper the enthusiasm for a Holy War which he is now seeking to excite. An opportunist can generally be transfixed by his own weapons; and it is likely that the German mania for speed and thoroughness will in'any case bewilder the brains of slow-thinking Orientals.—Dr. J. Hol- land Rose, in the "Contemporary Review."</p>
        <p>The Ancient East. By 'D. E. Hogarth, F.8.A., F.S.A. London:• Home University Library. 256 pp. is. Williams and Norgate. •</p>
        <p>This volume is a sequel to an earlier work in this remarkable library, Professor Myres' "Dawn of History," and the two together form an excellent introduction to the study of the early history of the world. Mr. Hogarth surveys the East of the ancient world in a series of brilliant chapters, dealing with its aspect every two hundred years from 1000 B.C. to the time of Alexander's conquest. He tells the story of the rise and fall of the great ancient anpires, . the Babylonian, the Assyrian, the Persian, and the Egyptian, and of the movements of the smaller peoples. It is, of course, the story of the successive attempts and ultimate failures of powerful natioffs to dominate and subdue alien races, 'to extend their imperial 'sway until they covered the known world. And it is told with rare charm.</p>
        <p>In an epilogue, Mr. Hogarth attempts an estimate of the effect of Alexander's conquest of the East. He analyses Hellenism, and shows that though it implied "recognition by the in- dividual that the society of which he was a member had an indefeasible and virtually ex- clusive claim on his good will and good offices," its weakness lay in the fact Ulla "society," in this sense, was not a nation, but a city. The Greeks who settled in Asia founded many cities, and in this way Hellenism was an agent of poli- tical civilisation in the East. But, as Mr. Hogarth says, "the influence of their Hellenism was potent and durable only' in those newly founded, or newly organised, urban communi- ties and their immediate neighbourhood." But Hellenism had its effect in preparing the way for Christianity. "The instrumentality of Alexander and his successors," says Mr. Hogarth, "in bringing about or intensifying that contact and intercourse between Semite and Greek, which begot the philosophic morality of Christianity and rendered its westward expan- sion inevitable, stands to their credit as an historic fact of such tremendous import that it may be allowed to atone for more than all their sins."</p>
        <p>Mr. Hogarth's style is most fascinating, and the book should appeal to a wide public, more especially as present events are arousing an in- terest in some of the districts with which it deals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>"DAILY CHRONICLE" WAR SONGS.</head>
        <p>"DAILY CHRONICLE" WAR SONGS.</p>
        <p>EXCLUSIVELYASIGHT-TESTING AND SPECTACLE BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>We are experts in this Branch of optical science, and have .confidence in recommending our methods, which are the result of 25 years' practical experience.</p>
        <p>Under the title of "War Songs," the "Daily Chronicle" has published a selection of the best war poems which havc appeared in its., columns. This little anthology contains verses by such eminent writers as Sir Henry Newbolt, Alfred Noyes, Maurice Hewlett, Harold Begbie, William Watson, Eden Phillpotts, Jessie Pope, and Coulson Kernahan. In all there are some sixty poems, each having its inspiration in the stirring times through which we are passing. "War Songs" is printed on good paper, and has a very attractive, coloured cover, -and is sold at threepence.</p>
