      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 1915.</p>
        <p>BIRTHS. MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>'WHY WE WIN.</p>
        <p>Soldier's Message to Women Liberals.</p>
        <p>FOR PRACTICAL HELP.</p>
        <p>As is only to be expected, the Women Liberals' Sewing Class (per its indefatigable secretary, Mrs. Osborne) continues to receive a steady stream of letters heartily thanking the woriceis I.A weir handsome contributions of soldiers' garments and comforts to the many deserving causes—in other countries as well as our own—in aid of the brave "Tommies" and having some connection with Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>CHERBOURG HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>THE CHERBOURG HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>A fortnight ago we stated that the Women Liberals': Class .had forwarded a large consign- ment of body-belts, woollen helmets, and ban- dages to Miss Truda Crosfield, and Mrs. Eva McLaren—per the Liberal Women's Suffrage Union—for distribution amongst the soldiers at the Eva McLaren Hospital, France. It will be remembered that Mrs. McLaren is chairman of the Union Committee, and that -Miss Cros- field (who is rendering invaluable services with her at the hospital mentioned) is secretary. We also said that Mrs. G. S. Pendlebury and Miss Short were at the same time sending 150 packets of cigarettes to Miss Crosfield., who would distribute them amongst the wounded warriors in her care. Mrs. Osborne has just received the following pleasing letter: "Thank you very much for the parcels of beautiful garments for Mrs. McLaren's Hos- pital Fund which you so kindly forwarded on behalf of the Warrington Women's Liberal Association War Sewing Fund. Will you also please convey Mrs. McLaren's thanks to Mrs. Pendlebury and Miss Short for their kind gift, and for the novel manner in which the money for it was raised. Mrs. McLaren is so grateful to all those who are so generously respondirig to her fund. I shall certainly let Miss Cros- field know how good Warrington W.L.A. has been to the hospital.—Yours truly, A. L. DOUGLAS, assistant secretary."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>IRISH ARMY CORPS.</head>
        <p>IRISH ARMY CORPS.</p>
        <p>Our readers will relaiember that at the Women Liberal Auxiliaries' Social held in the John Crosfield Memorial Hall on Monday even- ing of last week Mrs. John Crosfield told the ladies. that she was on the committee of Dublin workers who Were making things for the sol- diers. A relative of hers; a captain in the Irish Fusiliers, was going to the front very shortly, and she had promised him that she would give a hundred pairs of mittens for one of his companies, Who wanted mittens very badly. She collected half of that number whilst over in Ireland, and said she would send the ether fifty along. She knew that the Liberal women in . Warrington were knitting for the Grand Duke Michael's scheme; but, perhaps, when they had completed the number they had promised to send to the Grand Duke, they would see whether they could not help her. She would be pleased to supply all the wool, and, if they could "see their way to help her, she would' be very glad indeed. " Mrs. Arthui H. Crosfield then said she was sure she was 'voicing the feelings of them all when she hastened to assure Mrs. Crosfield that she could have, for her Irish soldiers, fifty of the pairs of mittens the Liberal women of War- rington had already worked. These mittens have since been despatched from the class, and the following acknowledg- ment received from the hon. secretary of the Irish Army Corps Ladies' Clothing Committee: "I have to thank you very much indeed on behalf of our Clothing Committee for your very kind and generous gift of fifty pairs of beauti- ful mittens (per Mrs. Crosfield) for the Royal Dublin Fusiliers. They are quite the best we have yet received, and I know they will be welcomed with delight by Sir Frederick Shaw, who, - commands the Bth Battalion, and for whom we are collecting at present. Will you please express to the Women's Liberal Asso- ciation our deep sense of gratitude for their kindness in . contributing to the 900 pairs wanted?—Yours very truly, M. DICKSON." "TOMMY'S" UNBOUNDED DELIGHT. Meanwhile, the Warrington " Tommies" have of course not gone short in any way, and they also continue to forward grateful tributes to the work of their Liberal womenfolk. The following examples may be taken as quite typical of the sentiments of the comfort recipients:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>"Cannot Thank You Too Much."</head>
        <p>"Cannot Thank You Too Much."</p>
        <p>From Driver W. Isherwood, Royal Field Artillery, somewhere in Ireland:</p>
        <p>." I cannot express my thanks at the parcel of socks and belt, and hope Vie Ladies' Liberal Club will have a successful New Year. They are just the articles that I wanted, and I cannot thank you too much for the surprise parcel. Again wishing the club every success for the coming year." / " If Everybody was as Kind—"</p>
        <p>_ From Private _ Percy Green, 4th Battalion South Lancashires, North of England: "I thank you from the bottom of my heart for your kindness to me. I wish everybody was as kind as you; the world would have no trouble. I thank you again and again." From Rifleman P. Lawless, Bth Battalion King's Royal Rifles, South of England: "I thank you for your kind gift. The gar- ments came in most useful, as we were soaked to the skin the next day whilst waiting to be inspected by Lord 'Kitchener. We had each been given three pairs of socks on enlistment, but they soon get worn out by the marching we are doing at present. We have been pretty busy clearing the roads of snow." From Private Thomas L—, South Lanca- shire Regiment: "I received your parcel on Wednesday, and I am very thankful to you for sending it, because I only had one shirt, and I was darn- ing a pair of socks whbn I received the parcel. We have only had two shirts since we 'listed, and I have worn one out. .We are expecting to move to B— next week, and I am sorry, because the people of W— liked us very much, and we liked them. . I wish you all a happy New Year."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>"Weather Severe: Trenches Flooded."</head>
        <p>"Weather Severe: Trenches Flooded."</p>
        <p>From Driver J. Pownall, 22nd Field Ambu- lance, Army Service Corps. On active service: "-I was very proud of the socks and helmet. They are very acceptable during the cold nights out here. I wish your League the best of luck." From Private J. Pridden, Ist King's Liver- pool Regiment, on active Service:</p>
        <p>I received the shirt and socks all right, which I am very thankful for, as the weather has been very severe here this few weeks, and the trenches flooded. So your present came in very useful, and I thank you very much." Practical Help Towards Winning. • From PriVate G. P. Bennett, Grenadier Guards:</p>
        <p>"I hardly know how to commence my letter of thanks to you for your great kindness to me. The articles were just what a soldier going to the fighting line requires, and I feel too keenly my gratitude for the kind gifts to write a lot of thanks to you on paper. I only hope I shall have the pleasure—when we have won this great war—of thanking you in person. I sin- cerely hope to wear my muffler on the march to Berlin. I don't know that I have any more to say, but you know my silence means my heartfelt thanks. In conclusion I wish success to all and each in your good work. It is such practical help and thought as yours that makes your British soldiers win their battles, so I thank you once again."</p>
        <p>WORK CONTINUED. Garments, Comforts, and Cigarettes. In addition to the despatching, to all parts of the country, of the usual large consignments of garments and comforts, the Women Liberals have just received the splendid gift of 1,000 cigarettes from Mr. Felix Maginn. These Mrs. A. H. Crosfield has forwarded to Miss Truda Crosfield for the further delectation of the wounded warriors at the Eva McLaren Hos- pital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>VAIRFIELD AND HOWLEY P.T.C.</head>
        <p>VAIRFIELD AND HOWLEY P.T.C.</p>
        <p>An interesting rifle match, "first six v. next eight," was held in connection with the Fair- field and Howley Company of the Preparatory Training Corps on Rylands' range on Saturday, both teams scoring 312 points. At the Munici- pal range on Tuesday evening, H. Gaunt, with a score of 46, won a competition for the best five out of eight shots at a one-inch centre</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Presentation to an Oddfellow.</head>
        <p>Presentation to an Oddfellow.</p>
        <p>There was a large gathering of local Odd- fellows at.the Pelican Hotel on Saturday even- ing, the occasion being a visit of the district officers and the presentation to Bro. Harry Lawrence, LP.P.G.AL, of a P.PG.M.'s gold jewel and framed P.P.G.M.'s certificate, and framed miniature, Br.c., In tecegnition of his valuable services to the district during his re- cent term of office. Bro. Dudley presided, and</p>
        <p>the district officers present inclUded the Mayor (Bro. Peter Peacock), the Prov. Grand Master of the District (Bro. A. E. Spann), Deputy Prov. Grand Master (Bro. W. E. Clare), and the Prov. C.S. (Bro. Frank Webster), &amp;c. The pre- sentation of the jewel was made by the Prov. Grand Master, who said that it was very largely owing to the exceptional business ability of Bro. Lawrence that the cause of Oddfellowship had prospered in the Warrington district dur- ing the past year, notwithstanding the many claims for sickness and funeral benefits which had been met. The Deputy Prov. Grand Master made the presentation of the certificate and miniature, and on behalf of the members of the Lodge the Mayor further presented him with a gold merit jewel and a silver-mounted umbrella. The recipient suitably returned thanks for the gifts. During the evening songs were rendered by P.G.'s Webster, Reddish, Barnes, and Boscow, and recitations by P.P.G.M. James Massey and P.G. James A. Dutton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>STILL "AS USUAL."</head>
        <p>STILL "AS USUAL."</p>
        <p>We presume it is of no avail to appeal to some members of the Town Council to remember that, now we stand in face of a common danger, we should close up our ranks and postpone our wrangles upon local differences. If we did what we ought to do we should assume a virtue if we had it not. and be peaceably inclined. Last Tuesday's " scene " in the Council was perhaps the worst of the many that have taken place in the last two decades, and had its origin in a misunderstanding of facts that could have been prevented by • the exercise of a little care. It is not necessary, by any means, to agree with all the proposals that are brought forward or submit to all the statements made in public, but we might at least try to debate the points at issue with- out suggesting unworthy motives and calling one another names. All the heat on this occasion was generated over so cool a question as the water supply. It is proposed to augment the Appleton supply of water for manufacturing .purposes by draw- ing water from the river in the neigh- bourhood of the weir. By this means it is sought to reserve the Winwick supply altogether for domestic pur- poses. The object is 'an admirable one. Whether the present time is opportune to spend £lO,OOO to £20,000, and whether the scheme is the best that can be produced, were leading points involved that were somewhat obscured in a long discussion by the needless personal fireworks that were let off.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>TERRITORIALS--AND CAPTAIN APPLETON.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIALS--AND CAPTAIN APPLETON.</p>
        <p>During this week and last our Terri- torials have visited home in batches, the last batch of the battalion being now in town. The men have, without exception, a fine soldierly bearing, and are intent upon giving the enemy a run for their money. What the training has done for them was well demon- strated by Skelhorne, who, playing.for the Warrington Football Club last Saturday, sh6wed condition far above that of any other man on the field— and all are supposed to be in training. One fact we have been glad to learn during these visits is that the love the men had for Captain APPLETON did not die with that lovable man. We are told that it is impossible to visit the grave of the late. Captain without find- ing a crowd of several hundred men round the spot. " I don't know how it is," said a Territorial, "but I always make for the Captain's grave whenever I have the leisure, and I always find three or four hundred others there." Captain APPLETON was a big brother to all his "boys," and he still has the power, though dead, of attracting them towards him. The result of his work still lives, and will be felt in Bel- gium and Germany some day. More recruits are wanted for the reserve battalion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>ARE THEY REDUNDANT?</head>
        <p>ARE THEY REDUNDANT?</p>
        <p>Objection to be Offered to Renewal</p>
        <p>of Four Licences.</p>
        <p>The annual licensing meeting for the County Borough of Warrington was held in the Borough Police Court yesterday morning. the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) presiding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>HIGH DRUNKENNESS RATE.</head>
        <p>HIGH DRUNKENNESS RATE.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls), in the course of his annual report, stated that the number of licence-holders in the borough was 185—two less than last year—and that, cal- culated on a population of 72,166, there was one licensed house. to every 390 inhabitants. There was an increase of 61 in the number-615--oi persons proceeded against for drunkenness, compared with the previous year. In 263 cases the drunkenness took 'place on a Saturday. A table giving the number of persons proceeded against for drunkenness in eleven Northern towns during the year 1914 showed Warrington to be second with 615, or 8.25 per 1,000 of the population, Birkenhead coming first with 1,828, or 13.98 per 1,000. Burnley was lowest with 2.17. From August Ist to December 31st (the war period) 204 persons were charged with drunkenness, as compared with 270 for the same months of the previous year, showing a de- crease of 66.</p>
        <p>The Mayor said one licensee had been pro- ceeded against, and Warrington might con- gratulate itself upon the manner in which its licensed businesses were conducted. REFERRED FOR CONSIDERATION. The whole of the licences were then formally renewed, with the exception of those of the Baths Hotel, Sankey-street; the White Hart Hotel, Sankey-street; the White Hart Hotel, Lyme-street and Market-place; and the Smiths Arms, Legh-street, which the Mayor an- nounced were objected to on the grounds of re- dundancy, and would be considered at the Ad- journed Licensing Sessions. EASEMENTS FOR STATION BARS. _ _</p>
        <p>Messrs. Tait, on be-h-lf-ei-ihe- Railway Company, and Mr. -C. E. Pinfold, for the Cheshire Lines Committee, made formal application for "a reduction of the rate of charges under the Licensing Act of the contri- bution to the Compensation Fund," in respect of the refreshment bars at the companies' sta- tions.</p>
        <p>The Bench agreed to fix the rate charged as heretofore, at two-thirds of that charged in the case of other licences. DATES FIXED.</p>
        <p>The dates of TransfOr Sessions for the next twelve months were fixed as follows: May 3rd, August 9th, November Ist, and February 7th. The Adjourned Annual Licensing Sessions were fixed for March sth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>WOMEN'S PART.</head>
        <p>THE WOMEN'S PART.</p>
        <p>More Liberal Socials For Soldiers' Comforts.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S WARD.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Women's Liberal Associa- tion have this week continued their good work of raising funds for the purchase of materials out of which to make still more comforts for our gallant soldiers, by holding two further ward socials in the Crosfield Memorial Halt. The first of the twain took place on Monday evening, and was more especially set apart for Liberals from the St. John's Ward, though the large attendance included ladies and gentlemen from all parts of the town, and not a few from the surrounding districts. Like its predeces- sors, the St. John's social was an unqualified success from every point of view, a pleasant evening being spent with whist, dominoes, song and dance. The arrangements were admirably made by the members of the ward committee, led by their ward captain (Mrs. Dutton), who, despite the 'fact that she .is suffering from a broken arm, pluckily insisted upon being pres- ent and in taking her fair share of the many duties devolving upon the lady organisers of the function. The ward committee were assisted in their onerous task of carrying through the arrangements, and of waiting upon the numer- ous guests during the refreshment interval, by the following ladies: Mesdames Ellison, Miller, Gregory, Brown, Young, Archer, Carter, Lin- gard, Wright and Swindells. Mr. E. A. San- ders (Liberal agent) made a most attentive and courteous M.C., both for the games and the dancing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>HOW ALL CAN HELP.</head>
        <p>HOW ALL CAN HELP.</p>
        <p>Mrs. OsaoßNE—who has pla) ed a leading part in the Association's effort to provide large con- signments of soldiers' comforts for the front, and has also busied herself in the matter of making die arrangements for the highly suc- cessful series of ward socials—presented the prizes won at whist and dominoes, and, in doing so, took the opportunity of expressing her pleasure at seeing so many ladies and gentlemen present; it was a most encouraging sight, and would stimulate the workers to still greater efforts in the direction of turning out solders' comforts. She referred with regret to the fact that Mrs. Dutton had met with an accident, as the result of which her arm was 'broken, and said she was sure that the ward captain would have the sympathy of them all, as well as their best wishes for her complete recovery. (Hear, hear.) The object of those socials was a most worthy one; one in which they could all feel proud to assist. (Heal:, hear.) The Liberal women were glad to have the opportunity of doing their share to provide the soldiers with comforts, and everybody whom they had ap- proached in the matter of assisting them to do so had. ,been most kind. Still further assistance was wanted, and those who could not give it in other directions could hplp by making a point of attending one or all of the ward socials .which were still to be held, viz.: Howley next Monday, St. Austin's next Thursday, and Lateliford on Monday of the following week. The socials were not restricted to Liberals from the par- ticular ward in connection with which any one of them was held, but people from all wards were open to attend any or all of them—the more, the merrier! (Laughter and applause.) In order that they might carry on their good work and, if possible, do even more than they had so far done', the Women's Liberal Associa- tion wanted all the help they could get, for, as they all knew, there was still a tremendous de- mand for comforts, similar to those the ladies of the Association had been so • industriously turning out. From time to time the workers had received very nice letters from men at the front, whp had received comforts worked by the Liberal women of Warrington, and those letters were so full of gratitude that they could not but encourage the 'womenfolk to still further effort. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>In conclusion, Mrs. Osborne asked those pres- ent to show their appreciation of Mrs. Dutton's pluck in turning up and assisting, despite her injury, by giving her a hearty cheer. The request was complied with in full measure.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sanders then called for another cheer for Mrs. Osborne (for presenting the prizes), and for the ward committee and all the lady workers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>ENTERTAINERS.</head>
        <p>THE ENTERTAINERS.</p>
        <p>During the evening vocal items were ren- dered by Mrs. E. A. Sanders, Mrs. S. Dutton, Mr. Jos. Hannan and Mr. r. Pearce, whilst Mr. Fred Banner and Mr. Louis Hales played the piano—the , splendid new instrument presented to the Women's Liberal Association by Miss Schilizzi, and formally opened last week by Mrs. Arthur Crosfield.</p>
        <p>The prizes for whist and dominoes were given by Mrs. Dutton and Councillor Shaw (one of the representatives of the .ward on the Town Council),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Fairfield's Bill Party.</head>
        <p>Fairfield's Bill Party.</p>
        <p>The social in connection with the Fairfield Ward was held on Thursday evening and at- tracted a record attendance. The Ward Cap- tain (Mrs. J. T. Locker) had charge of the ar- rangements, and she was ably assisted by Mes- dames Shaw, Ashton, Webster, Birch, Stringer, Hastry, Warhurst, and Richardson, and Misses Gibson, Willett AO Fairhurst. During an int?rval in • the dancing, Mrs. Osborne, the hon. secretary of the Association, presented the prizes won at cards and dominoes, and given by Councillor J. T. Locker, who, in the course of a brief speech, proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Osborne, who, he said, was alwatys a willing helper in anything that made for the welfare of Warrington. The socials had so far proved such a success that, no doubt, at the end of the series the Association would have a good surplus in hand with which to continue their excellent work of making comforts for the soldiers.</p>
        <p>Couricillor Locker also expressed he thanks of the company to the lady helpers. In acknowledgment of the hearty round of applause awarded her, Mrs. Osborne referred with pleasure to the fact that the attendance that night constituted a record for the series of socials. sho was sure the presence of so many ladies and gentlemen at that social was evidence of the spirit all felt with regard to the matter of making comforts for the soldiers.</p>
        <p>Among those pr-egent during the evening were the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Alder- man Tinnion, and Councillors Wilkinson and Osborne. Councilor Locker and Mr. F. A. Sanders (Liberal agent) shared the duties of M.C.. and Messrs. Louis Hales and George rendlebury officiated at the piano. Between the dances songs were sung by Mrs. Sanders, Miss Winstanley, and Mr. J. Turton. Miss Locker- bie gave a violin solo, and Miss Lillie Weston recited.</p>
        <p>NEW NAVAL WARRANT OFFICER.</p>
        <p>Warrington friends and old comrades of the South Lancashire Volunteers will be pleased to learn that Mr. E. IL (Bert) O'Brien, chief</p>
        <p>engine-room artificer (son of Mr. P. O'Brien, Wilderspool-causeway), has been promoted to warrant rank in the Royal Navy as artificer engineer, his promotion dating from January Ist. Mr. E. H. O'Brien's promotion to Chief E.R.A. was as recent as November Ist, 1014.</p>
        <p>THE "COMRADES." Artillery to be Added to Form Division.</p>
        <p>Lord Derby has been requested by Lord Kitchener to raise in West Lancashire two bri- gades of field artillery, with their ammunition column, and a battery of heacry artillery. This will have the effect of raising tile Comrades to the dignity of a division. Pay and allowances ,will be as in the Regular Array. Care will be taken to keep friends together. This new de- velopment will prove attractive to many horse- men and others, to whom ordinary infantry duty does not especially appeal. Recruits should apply to all local recruiting offices throughout West Lancashire, or C.ciopers Buildings, Churet-street, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>WAR STORIES.</head>
        <p>WAR STORIES.</p>
        <p>A BRAVE SOLDIER. Chaplain's Tribute to a AVarringtou Man.</p>
        <p>DIED FOR OTHERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Robert Fogg, 18, West-street, who had two sons at the Front, have just re- ceived intimation that one of them, Private Hugh Fogg, whose wife and two children live at 1, West-street, has been killed at the Front. An additional sad note in this case is that the two children_ will shortly be increased to three.</p>
        <p>The Rev. C. M. Chavasse, son of the Bishop of Liverpool, and a chaplain to the Forces, has written the following letter to the deceased private's parents—</p>
        <p>Clearing Hospital, 31/1115.</p>
        <p>. -- Dear Mrs. Fogg,—You will by now have heard of the sad death of your son, Private H. Fogg, of the 2nd Rifle Brigade. He was brought into hospital here on the 29th, in the morning, badly shot in the body, and was in considerable pain. I went at once to him, and seeing his bad condition stayed with him for some time. He wanted me to write and tell you about him. He also told me lit had prayed and was trusting in Jesus Christ to take care of him. I prayed with him, and he seemed much com- forted, and in about an hour he mercifully be- came unconscious and peacefully passed away at 1 p.m. From the time the doctors had dressed his wound till he became unconscious I never left him, so you will know he was not quite alone. We buried him yesterday in the little cemetery of the town. A v.Neodezt cross, with his name and regiment upon it, marks the exact spot. A firing party attended, and we did all we could to do honour to a brave soldier. I pray that that Saviour in Whom your son trusted may be your comfort now, and always remember that, like Christ, he died in the ser- vice of others.</p>
        <p>I hope to be home on leave in a week's time, and if you will drop me a line, or come to. see me at the Palace, Liverpool, I will let you know all you wish.</p>
        <p>Yours in deepest sympathy, C. NE. CHAVASSE, C.F.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>OUR SAILOR BOY. How He Spent His Brief Furlough in</head>
        <p>OUR SAILOR BOY. How He Spent His Brief Furlough in</p>
        <p>‘Varrington.</p>
        <p>AFTER A "SLAP" AT THE ENEMY.</p>
        <p>Warrington, like many other towns, has a fair proportion of its brave sons doing their share in the great struggle that is being waged on land and sea.</p>
        <p>One of our representatives had the good -for- tune this week to meet a Warrington sailor boy, who, in his own phraseology, has had the satisfaction of "having a slap at them." By "them," of course, he meant "The Baby- killers." This Warrington. tar, Arthur Tom- linson, quite a youthful one, resides in Hale- street when he is not on the briny. It was at this address our representative found him, quite the merriest of a happy little household. "I never got those ' Woodbines,"', •he was telling his sister.</p>
        <p>. "Well, I sent them," "Erne said, "three weeks before Christmas. They cost me four shillings, and as you didn't get theita, I can't afford to throw more money away." • Young Tomlinson is quite philosophic about those "fags." "Never mind; I'll make sure of them this time tad take some with me," he said. After taking part in the North Sea action, he returned to port and managed to get 24 hours' leave. One of his little sisters almost clung to him while our representative was speaking to him.</p>
        <p>"She wouldn't let him go to sleep," an older sister said. • •</p>
        <p>" No ; he's only got a few lours with us, and you don't think I'm going to let him sleep when his stay's so very short, do you?" the little flaxen-haired girl retorted.</p>
        <p>Tomlinson is a model of modesty. He had done nothing, he said. It's his "nothing," as he termed it, along with the "nothing" of thousands of others, that is allowing those at home to get along so smoothly as they are doing.</p>
        <p>Stoked The Tiger. He is a stoker on the Tiger, one of the ships, of course, that took part in the recent battle squadron fight in the North Sea. It was the Tiger that first covered the Lion, when the latter_got slightly damaged. "When the Blucher had been done for," he said, "I came up from below to see what was going on. I saw the Derfflinger and the Seyd- litz were badly on fire, and if they ever got back to harbour, it's a marvel how they did it. We had been engaged with these two boats and the Blucher for a time."</p>
        <p>The Tiger was one of the battle squadron that turned out when the Germans bombarded Scarborough and the Hartlepools. "We sighted the enemy then, but the fog cheated us, and the Germans got away, but we had a bit of a 'slap' at them last time until the mines stopped us again. And we're hoping to get the chance again."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>GERMAN "BLIGHTERS."</head>
        <p>GERMAN "BLIGHTERS."</p>
        <p>Heartlessly Allow British Sailors To</p>
        <p>Go Down.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Vernon, who carries on business at Priestley-street, Warrington, recently received the following letter from his brother Edward, who is serving on board R.M.S. Oronsa "Valparaiso, Chili. Three weeks now have we been securely tied fore and aft, with not the slightest idea of when we are likely to move. I take the pleasure of writing to you my Christ- mas letter. To me it seems almost a mockery to wish the old, old wish under the present condi- tions, for God knows the season will indeed be a black one in thousands of European homes— one which we are not likely to forget. But I hope that it will be the worst you will ever see.</p>
        <p>• A Cowardly Excuse. "I suppose you read in the papers of the battle off this coast on November Ist, two of our ships being sunk—Good Hope and Monmouth— with all hands. The Germans, who were very strong, did not make the slightest effort to save any of our seamen, their excuse being that the sea was too heavy to lower boats. But we know for a fact that the sea at the time was like a duck pond, so it proves what ' blighters ' they are. We are to attend a memorial service to the British dead on Sunday at the English Church."</p>
        <p>The writer encloses the following greeffng, neatly printed in gold lettering upon a slip of brown paper: I am very hard up; in fact stony broke, Not much to eat or drink, not much to smoke; It's because of German cruisers that we are still here, Instead of near by all our friends so dear. Though eleven thousand miles of the ocean divide us, The greeting below can be sent to remind us; But, whenever you look on this common brown paper, Remember the cause of it is the fiend called the Kaiser.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ENTERTAINING THE WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>ENTERTAINING THE WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the wounded soldiers in the Wm' rington Infirmary were entertained to a de- lightful concert organised by Mr. W. B. Ran- dall, the contributors to the programme being Madame J. Armstrong, Miss Oakes, Messrs. Greet/1444i shd itirton, and raster Green- hough: .</p>
        <p>On Thursday Mr. Randall arranged for a Visit from a number of the artistes appearing at the HippocfrOm'e. this week, who pevo a 'first- rate entertairunent,, being assisted by Mr.. Delaney and MSS' Lilian Bate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>SININc TO "'THE PALS."</head>
        <p>SININc TO "'THE PALS."</p>
        <p>. , A number of theiiiberS of the Warringtoul Musical So-ciOfy ,jdurneYed over in motor cars',. kindly provided by Lady Greenall, to thO Knowsley Park camp on Thursday evening: arid in the Y.M.C.A. pavilion there entertained nearly a thousand members of the Liverpool "Pals" Battalion with an excellent musical programme, the contributors being the Misses Elsie Hewitt and Cissie Rigby, and Messrs. Fred Ffeldhouse, W. Hopkins, J. Draper and J. B. Poole. Mr. Percy Smith was the accom.' pwni;3C.•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Coming Confirmation. The Bishop of Liverpool has fixed March 11th as the date of a Confirmation to be held at Christ Church, Latchford. Already sixty can- didates in the parish are under instructilin.</p>
        <p>Appleton Angling Support for the Infirmary.</p>
        <p>The hon. treasurer, 311:: Roger C. Parr, acknowledges the receipt from Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., C.V.0., of a cheque for .f.,45 175., representing charges made for fishing in the Appleton Reservoir.</p>
        <p>Citizens' Guild of Help. The annual meeting of the Warrington Citi- zens' Guild of Help will be held in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall on Monday, Feb-: ruary 15th, when addresses will be given by two well-known social workers, Mrs. M. Wilson and Mr. D'Aeth (of Liverpool Un:versity).</p>
        <p>A Re-appointment for Alderman Bennett.</p>
        <p>On: the recommendation of the General Pur- poses Committee,, the Town Council on Tuesday evening re-appointed Alderman Bennett their representative on the Court of the Supreme Governing Body of Liverpool University for a further period of three years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>For Services Rendered.</head>
        <p>For Services Rendered.</p>
        <p>The recommendation of the Fire Engine Com- mittee that bills be sent to Mr. J. IL Crosby, Collins Green, and the Rev. Father Tallon, Church of Our Lady, Warrington, for .P.. 55 3s. 6d. and 16s. 6d. respectively, for services ren- dered by the Fire Brigade during the month of December, was passed at Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Literary Society. Before the members of the Emmanuel Literary and Debating Society, on Tuesday evening, Mr. W. H. Atkinson gave an interest- ing and instructive paper on "The Mystery of Microscopic Life," his remarks being illus- trated by some wonderful microscopic slides depicting the life of the smaller animal and plant world. Next week the Rev. Wm. Reid has promised to read the members a paper on " Savonarola." •</p>
        <p>Compensation Stopped. Messrs. J. bolan and Son, builders and con- tractors, Napier-street, applied at the Warring- ton County Court on Thursday for the termina- tion or diminution of compensation paid under an award to James Ward, a former employe, of Hall-street, and now a member of H.M. Forces. After hearing the evidence, Judge Gurney found in favour of the applicants, and dieided that compensation should cease as from November last.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Shorthand Writers.</head>
        <p>Shorthand Writers.</p>
        <p>A very interesting paper on the " Shon" hook was read before the members of the War- rington Shorthand Writers' Association at the last weekly meeting. The speaker, after re- ferring to the wonderful foresight of the inventor of phonography, dealt with the recent changes in the " Centenary" Instructor as affecting the termination " Shon." The use of this important abbreviation device was further explained in the course of the paper. At the close of the discussion Miss Buckley conducted the speed practice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Dicketisiana.</head>
        <p>Dicketisiana.</p>
        <p>"Charles Dickens and the War. By 'J.,' Warrington Examiner,' December 12," is an entry which occupies a place of honour in the latest official list of Dickensiana, published by the Dickens Fellowship. "J." is Mr. Sydney Jeffery, a young and pro- mising member of or reporting staff. His -article, published so suitably on the approach ,of Christmas, attracted the attention of many good judges of Dickens and his work. To-morrow is the 103rd anniversary of the novelist's birth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Natus-4 Mil I.</head>
        <p>Natus-4 Mil I.</p>
        <p>During the week ended January 30th 43 births were recorded in Warrington (23 males and 20 females) and 22 deaths. During the correspond- ing week of last year there were the same num- ber of births and, 13 more deaths. Calculated on an estimated population. of 75,532, the death- rate was equal to 15.1, as against 17.3 for the previous six weeks and 24.5 for the correspond- ing week of last year. The birth-rate was 29.6, as against 27.2 and 29.4 respectively. Pneu- monia is prevalent in the town just now, and last week five deaths were due to it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Bands for Recruiting.</head>
        <p>Bands for Recruiting.</p>
        <p>We hear that Lord Derby is sending a band to play through Warrington on Tuesday as a stimulus to recruiting. The Band of the 4th Second Service Battalion of the South Lancashires will also pay a visit to Warrington on a similar errand to-day. This afternoon they will play on the Wilderspool football ground„,and at 4.30 they will sound the Retreat at Market Gate. Two tattoos have been arranged for the evening, the first at Market Gate at 9.30, and the second at the same place .half an hour later.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Rise and Fall in Wheat.</head>
        <p>Rise and Fall in Wheat.</p>
        <p>The price of wheat touched Bs. per bushel of 151 b. in Warrington market on Wednesday, the price having advanced from a penny to two- pence per bushel since the previous week. The rise, however, has been followed by a speed 9 decline, and late prices ruled from 7s. 101 d. to 7s. 11d.</p>
        <p>There was a good demand for oats at Wed- nesday's market, 4s. 3d. to As. 6d. per 451 b. being realised. Clover hay sold better, £3 15s. to £.4: 10s. being offered. Meadow hay sold slowly at £2 15s. to £3. Straw fetched 275. 6d. per ton, XX flour 435. per 2801 b., and best bran is. 6d. per 201 b.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Flourishing Garden Society. – – –</head>
        <p>Flourishing Garden Society. - - -</p>
        <p>At the annual —meeting of the fatchford Garden Society, held on Tuesday evening in the Wash-lane Schoolrooms, satisfactory pro- gress was reported.—The Rev. G. C. Briggs (vicar) presided over a good assembly.—The annual report, presented by the Secretary (Mr. J. S. Burgess), was passed. It was stated that the society's outlook was very hopeful, despite the war. The membership was good. The society has a balance in hand of .28.—Hearty votes of thanks were passed to the retiring offi- cials. On the proposition of Miss Broadbent, seconded by Mr. Nixon, Messrs. Burgess and Hooper were elected joint secretaries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Death of the Rector's Sister.</head>
        <p>Death of the Rector's Sister.</p>
        <p>At London on Tuesday morning the death (took place suddenly of Mrs. Sophia Catherine Dampier, sister of the Rector of Warrington, After a week's illness. The deceased lady, whose age was 73 years, was the widow of the late Rev. Augustus Dampier, rector of Gilling- ham, Norfolk, and third daughter of the late Mr. Daniel Willis, of Halsnead, Prescot, Lan- cashire. Her son is Captain Dampier, R.N., of H.M.S. Audacious.</p>
        <p>Canon Willis, with whom the deepest sym- pathy is felt, has during the past eighteen months lost three sisters by death—the eldest and youngest having previously passed away. -Thus only the rector's second sister now sur- -vives.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mrs. Dampier took place at Gillingham yesterday. Canon Willis Was not able to attend.</p>
        <p>Motor Collision Sequel.</p>
        <p>An action came before his Honour Judge Gurney at the Warrington County Court on Thursday in which William Winsta.nley and Co., plumbers and decorators, Warrington, claimed £B2 18s. sd. from Robert Proctor, J.P., of Macclesfield, for damages done to their motor-oar and the cost of the hiring of another car, through, it was alleged, the negligence of the defendant or his agent on May 13, 1914, at Lower Walton-road in driving a car into that of the plaintiffs', which was being driven by Mr. E. Pitchford, a member of the plaintiff compailiy.</p>
        <p>liurgis, instructed by Mr. A. J. Willett, was for the plaintiffs, and Mr. Wingate Saul, instructed by Mr. James Chapman (Manches- ter), defended.</p>
        <p>Ifis-H--o-n-o-u—r. gave judgment for the plaintiffs for £4B, being the cost of hiring another ear by the plaintiffs whilst the other was under repair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Entertaining Belgian Guests.</head>
        <p>Entertaining Belgian Guests.</p>
        <p>The Belgian Refugees at present quartered in St. Austin's House, Bold-street, spqnt a very happy evening on Wednesday, thanks to the kindness of Miss Lawrence. Mr. Lambert en- tertained them with his gramophone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Town _Planning.</head>
        <p>Town _Planning.</p>
        <p>Special meetings of the StaTei Improvement and Lighting Committee of the Town Council are now held, every alternate Thursday "for the purpose of considering town planning."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Pollee Pensioner's Funeral.</head>
        <p>Pollee Pensioner's Funeral.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mr. John Baxendale, of Algernon-street, who served in the Police Force for twenty-six years, took place at the Cemetery on Monday and was attended by a detachment of local police officers, under the charge of Inspector Hosp. The deceased gentle- man, who retired on pension in May, 1908, had been in failing health for sometime prior to his death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Late Mr. Thos. Daintith.</head>
        <p>The Late Mr. Thos. Daintith.</p>
        <p>At the Cemetery on Monday the mortal re- mains of the late Mr. Thomas Daintith, gun and cycle manufacturer, of Bridge-street, were laid to rest, the Rev. H. Williamson offiCiating. The chief mourners were: Mr. A. T. Daintith and Lance-Corporal L. C. Daintith (sons), Messrs. William Daintith (brother), D. E. Watson and Bennett Storey - (sons-in-law); Arthur; William, and Fred Dainiith (nephews).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>St. Hilda's_Building Fund.</head>
        <p>St. Hilda's_Building Fund.</p>
        <p>Up to the end of last mond; the amount re- ceived on account of the fund for the building of a new church of St. Hilda at Latchford amounted to £1,146 12s. 4d., the sum of £lO4 odd having been added thereto during the pre- vious eight weeks, including .287 ss. 9d. repre- senting a share of the profits on the annual sale of work in connection with the parent church— St. James'.</p>
        <p>Women Teachers—Married or Single. So many teachers having gone to the war, there is great need in the schools for outside help, and the Education Committee are adver- tising for mistresses, single or married, who are not now teaching to return to their pro- fession as supply or temporary assistants, par- ticularly for senior schools. Women, patriotic as the men who have joined the colours, will we feel sure take a delight in filling the gaps that the needs of the war have made.</p>
        <p>Council Have Done a Wise Thing. The Town Hall, Parks and' Baths Committee reported to the Town Council, at their meeting on Tuesday, that the Local Government Board had sanctioned the borrowing of the sum of .X 4,192 for the purchase of the land adjoining Victoria Park, and the laying out of the same as an extension of the Park, and as a play- ground. A term of sixty years is allowed for repayment of the loan.—Alderman Bennett said he was quite sure the Council would find that they had done a wise thing in going in for this extra open space.</p>
        <p>What Every " Church-Person " Ought</p>
        <p>to Know!</p>
        <p>In the current issue of his parish magazine the Vicar of St. James', Latchford, announces his intention of preaching a special series of Lbnten sermons "based on subjects about which every church-person ought to know 'to his soul's 'health,' but concerning which there is a considerable lack of knowledge." The title of the first sermon will be "Vocation," and of the second "Conversion."</p>
        <p>The February Council Meeting. The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) pre- sided over the monthly meeting of the War- rington Town Council, held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening, and there were also pre- sent the Deputy Mayor (Councillor Joseph), Aldermen John Evans, Tinnion, Jaw. Evans, Pierpoint, Smethurst, and Bennett, and Coun- cillors Dr. Fox, J. Broadhurst, 11. Roberts, Dickenson, S. Gough, J. Pemberton, J. Haw- thorn, Wilkinson, Halliwell, Henshall, Locker, Shaw, Skelland, Osborne, Barton, Firth Lind- ley, Lashford, Cunnington, Chrimes, Chantler, 2nd Archer, with the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) and other Corporation officials.</p>
        <p>Surplus Corporation Lands. A joint sub-committee of the Finance and Estate and Street Improvement and Lighting Committees of the Corporation has been ap- pointed to go into the matter of the disposal of surplus lands belonging to , the town. The Finance and Estates Committee is repre- sented on the sub-committee by Alderman John Evans and Councillors Wilkinson, Locker, and Lindley, and the Street Improvement and Lighting Committee by the Mayor (Alderman Peacock), the Deputy Mayor (Councillor Joseph), Alderman Bennett, and Councillor Pemberton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Brewers and Assessments.</head>
        <p>Brewers and Assessments.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Union Assessment Committee, presided over by Mr. W. Furness yesterday, a letter was read from Messrs., Robert Davies and Co., solicitors, with reference to Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and Co.'s assessment, asking that the hearing of an appeal they had lodged should be left over until they had fairly tested the effects of the new beer tax.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Young (Liverpool) appealed unsuc- cessfully on behalf of Messrs. P. Walker and Son, Ltd., against the increased assessment of the Lion Hotel, to which structural alterations had been made.</p>
        <p>, Warrington R.D.C. At the meeting of the Warrington Rural Dis- trict Council, presided over on Wednesday by Mr. W. Furness, rises were granted of £l5 per annum to Mr. Simpson, dust contractor; Great Sankey, and of .25 per annum to Mr. Arstall, as caretaker of the Rixton Cemetery. An agreement between the trustees of the Blue Coat School and the Council relating to a piece of land in Great Sankey was approved of, and a committee was appointed to examine the streets of the Council with a view to call- ing upon the frontagers to sewer and make the streets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Boxing Night Affair.</head>
        <p>Boxing Night Affair.</p>
        <p>At the Warring-ion County Court on Thurs- day Nellie Savory, of 5, Paul-street, unsuccess- fully sued A. H. Dutton and his wife, of the Hop Pole Hotel, Horsemarket-street, for .28 2s. for damages for assault alleged to have been committed by the defendants on December 26. Mr. Longton represented the plaintiffs, and Mr. T. S. Steel the defendants. Giving judgment for the defendants with costs, Judge Gurney said the case ought to have gone to the police court, when there would probably have been a cross-summons by the defendants and both parties have been ordered to pay their own costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Appointment of School Dental Officer:</head>
        <p>Appointment of School Dental Officer:</p>
        <p>Aid. Bennett's Protest.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee recommended to the Town Council, at their monthly meeting on Tuesday, that Mr. Wm. Hutchinson, L.D.S., be appointed school dental officei at a salary of £250 per annum; also that Mr. A. R. M. Mcllraith, M. 8., B.Ch., be appointed temporary Assistant Medical Officer of Health at a salary at the rate of £3OO per annum, , the appoint- ment to be determinable on three months' notice.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett referred to the former ap- pointment, and said it had been made without any consultation with the responsible sub- committee of the Education Committee. He did not think that was quite the proper way to have dealt with the matter, and thought—as had been suggested on previous occasions— that the appointment should have been made in harmonious co-operation with the Medical Inspection Sub-committee of the Education Committee. He knew that the action of the Health Committee had given rise to a good deal of feeling among some members of the Education Committee, because the work of the new officer was confined to the Education De- partment, and the Education Committee natur- ally felt that it would have been bare courtesy to them to have been given the opportunity of having a voice in the matter of the appoint, ment. He-desired to personally protest against what he regarded as a very inconsiderate action. Alderman Jas. Evans (chairman of the Health Committee) replied that the committee's action was in accord with the past .procedure. and the recommendation was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>A6lw</head>
        <p>A6lw</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 1915.</p>
        <p>Mayor and Mayoress's Whist Drive. A whist drive and dance, arranged by the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Peter Peacock), in aid of the Infirmary Special War Fund, will be held in the Parr Hall on February 12th. Tickets may be purchased at the Town Hall on or before the 11th inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>"The War arid Wages 7"</head>
        <p>"The War arid Wages 7"</p>
        <p>The foregoing will be the title of the first of two public lectures to be delivered by Mr. A. N. Shimmin at the Co-operative Hall on February 16th and 23rd. The lectures will be under the auspices of the Workers' Educational Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>"Where are the Dead?"</head>
        <p>"Where are the Dead?"</p>
        <p>This will be the title of the annual series of lectures to be delivered in connection with the New Church, at the Masonic Hall, Bold-street. The first lecture is to be given by the Rev. J. Ashby on Tuesday next.</p>
        <p>Photographic Society. . "Innocents Abroad" was the title of an in- teresting and amusing address which Miss Jack- son delivered to the members of the Warring- ton Photographic Society on Tuesday. The lecture recounted and depicted the adventures of seven members of the society in Holland last July.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Guild Festival.</p>
        <p>The annual festival in connection with the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Guild will be held to- morrow (Sunday), when the special preacher will be the Rev. Jas. W. Dinsdale, of Sheffield. At a musical service in the afternoon the Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock) will preside. On Monday evening the Rev. J. W. Dinsdale lecture on "Glimpses of Bohemian Life and Scenery.."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Prominent Liberal's Death.</head>
        <p>Prominent Liberal's Death.</p>
        <p>With regret we record tile death of a promi- nent Liberal and Wesleya.Mr. Isaac Hayward, aged 59, of 21, Wilson-Patten-street, Warring- ton, which sad event took place on Sunday. For nine months the state of Mr. Hayward's health had been delicate. Only two years ago he re- linquished his duties as fire-rod mill manager with the *hitecross Company, after a useful career, which included the registration of several patents. Mr. Hayward was at one time connected with the Literary and Philosophical Society. He leaves a widow, five sons and seven daughters.—The funeral took place at the Cemetery on Wednesday afternoon, iTltzuiany beautiful floral tributes were laid on the grave. Messrs. W. Hodgkinson, Ltd., had charge of the funeral arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER PANTO WEEK AT THE ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>Pantomime again holds the boards at the Royal Court this week—a merry potted version of "Jack and the Beanstalk," brightly played twice nightly by a crowd of mirthful mummers, assisted by the usual chorus of pretty and well- 41rilled damsels, who give tongue trippingly to the latest songs of the day, and foot it featly in the mazy dance. Though somewhat belated— for one usually connects this sort of theatrical fare with the festive season—the panto. is none the less welcome, as crowded and joyous houses have so far testified. The old story of the guileless youth who disposed of the solitary bovine member of the impecunious widow's farmyard stock in return for a bag of beans, only to find that there was more virtue in the somewhat one-sided exchange than appeared on the surface of the transaction, is told after the familiar fashion-:-the ungainly giant with his cannibalistic proclivities; the winsome princess whom the Brobdinagian gentleman marks down as the hors d'ceuvre of his next meal; the fear- less village wight, who, armed with the en- chanted sword, spoils that mountainous indi- vidual's voracious appetite and incidentally marries the intended appetiser; the good fairy and the individu4 from the place that is never mentioned in polite society—all are there, and between them they provide as pleasant a means of whiling away a. merry couple of hours as could well be devised. The company that is presenting "Jack and the Beanstalk" for the amusement of Warringtonians is toured by Mr. Wm. De Lacy, who, himself, contributes in no small measure to the general merriment by ap- pearing as Dame Durden, the mother of the giant-slaughtering Jack. In his task of mirth- making he has able assistance from Mr. George Millard as King Collywobbles ; . Mr. Freddy Frome as Muggins, the village idiot; and Mr. Fredk. Floyd as the giant; not forgetting IMessrs. Milne and Wood, who are respectively the fore and hind legs of as humoursome a cow as ever frisked behind the footlights. Miss Amy Osborne makes a dashing Jack, and sings her songs with much verve, whilst Miss Elsie Fow- ler is a dainty Princess, and Miss rbrothy Dare a dashing Prince. Little Edith laogan is a charming Fairy Cupid, and the Five Weet- mans dance prettily and sing sweetly as a quin- tette of village belles. The rest of the cast is well filled and several attractive specialities are introduced, whilst the scenery and costumes are on a lavish scale. Of -course, all the latest songs and choruses are introduced, together with an abundance of wheezes* old and new— more of the latter than the former.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Big Sims' Drama Next Week.</head>
        <p>Big Sims' Drama Next Week.</p>
        <p>George R. Sims' great emotional drama of London life, "The Ever Open Door," will form the attraction at the Royal Court Theatre next week, where it will be presented twice nightly at 6.50 and 9. There will be the usual matinee on Monday at 2.30. "The Ever Open Door" is admittedly one of the finest outputs of the pen that gave to the stage "The Two Little Vagabonds" and other great/ dramatic suc- cesses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>FINE FARE AT THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>FINE FARE AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>There is a fine programme at tile Hippodrome this week, and, so far, large ad enthusiastic houses have, twice nightly, revelled in it. "The Heart of a Jew," an intensely interesting and emotional playlet, forms the principal attrao- tion, and is proving a big hit. Elroy, although armless, paints, sketches, plays the cornet, and does a whole host of other things-'and does them well—with his toes, and Gladys Hooley appears in a delightful scena, "My Fiddle and I," in the course of which she extracts much beautiful melody from her violin. Sam Hilton sings and patters breezily, and the Lightning Vinettos perform some wonderfully dexterous juggling feats. Talbot O'Farrell sings sweet Irish songs, and Lena Lynn introduces some novel step dancing. A graceful song and dance turn is supplied by Richardson's Daffodils— eight comely young damsels—and this 'Week's issue of the Gaumont Graphic includes some fine war pictures. Altogether the show is quite tip-top.</p>
        <p>Big Attraction Next Week.</p>
        <p>A big attra-ction has been secured for the Hip- podrome next week in the shape of a gorgeous pantomime, "Cinderella and the Little Glass Slipper," toured by Messrs. E. C. Jazon and M. Montgomery. The well-known fairy story is told in nine scenes, and the cast includes a num- ber of sterling variety artistes to ere point to the humour with which the version abounds and to sing the many popular and up-to-date songs which have been pressed into service. Clever specialities will be introduced by the Sisters Sprightly and Angers and Eseott. Two houses nightly. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>DRAMA AT STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>DRAMA AT STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>The chief place has been given this week to drama—and drama of a very high order. Those who visited the Kinema during the first half Of the week saw "Pimple's Prison," an excruciat- ingly funny farce, depicting another episode in the life of the humorous favourite mentioned in the title. " Pathe's Animated Gazette" this time paid attention to the life and work of the Russian Army and its leaders; it was a most instructive film. The same remark applies to the third edition of " Pathes' War Map," which —by means of moving diagrams—ingeniously illustrated the progress of the War. "St. Elmo" was one of the finest dramas we have seen. "Private Bunny" was the title of a topical, up-to-date, and laughable film. For the latter half of this week—including to-day's performances—ibe management of the Kinema have secured the famous military drama, "The Midnight Wedding."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>TRAMWAY SHELTERS.</head>
        <p>TRAMWAY SHELTERS.</p>
        <p>Will the "Aesthetic Beauty" of the Cemetery Gates Be Spoilt?</p>
        <p>The Electricity and Tramways Committee recommended to the Town Council at their meeting on Tuesday night that the tender of the Lion Foundry Company for the supply and erection of the following tramway shelters should be accepted:- 1. Stockton Heath, at .R 79 ss. 2. Stafford-road, at 0275 10s. 3. Bridge-street, at £B5 16s. 6d. 4. Cemetery, at £BO ss. 5. Latchford, at £BO ss.</p>
        <p>6. Longford,'`at £79 ss.</p>
        <p>The proposed positions of the shelters at the Cemetery and in Bridge-street gave rise to some amount of discussion. That at the Cemetery is to be built round a lamp fronting the main gates, the existing lamp being moved further back towards the gates; whilst that in Bridge- street will be situated on the edge of the kerb opposite the side windows of the Royal Court Hotel, and will be 18 feet in length and en- closed and glazed on the street side. An al- ternative suggestion put forward during the de- bate on the position of the Bridge-street shelter was that a lean-to should be erected over the footpath fronting Mr. Hepherd's premises, whilst it was also suggested that the shelter at the Cemetery should be p".aced in em angle in the gate, approach, instead of in front pf the gates. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>TWO PROPOSED SITES CRITICISED.</head>
        <p>TWO PROPOSED SITES CRITICISED.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT proposed that the minute be deleted. He did so, firstly, because he thought the proposed position of the shelter at the Cemetery needed reconsideration; the posi- tion proposed was a wrong —o-fre—altogether. There was a place in the angle of the wall of the western lodge which was quite ample for a shelter, and if it were placed there it would not in any way interfere with the architectural appearance of the buildings or the approach to the Cemetery. But for some occult reason the committee went out of its way to propose to put the shelter in quite the wrong place; presum- ably, on the same principle as another com- mittee had selected the site of the public con- venience in Howard-place. (Laughter.) If the shelter were put in the posrion proposed, the view of the Whole approach to the Cemetery, and of the beautiful pair of gates—one of the most attractive architectural features in the town—would be absolutely obscured. Still, apart altogether from the artistic point of view, the position in which it was proposed to put the shelter, would, in his opinion, constitute a danger to traffic. As to the shelter which it was proposed to erect at the -Rylands-street end of the car route, he objected on the ground that the street was much too narrow, and as, an alternative he would suggest an overhead shel- ter—something in the nature of a verandah, rather than the kind of erection proposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>AN ALTERNATIVE SUGGESTION.</head>
        <p>AN ALTERNATIVE SUGGESTION.</p>
        <p>Alderman JAMES EVANS seconded Alderman Bennett's proposal. He thought the best place for the shelter at the Cemetery would be in the angle at the western side of the'gates, although, at the same time, he confessed that he did not see any special beauty about the gates. He only went there when he was forced. (Laugh- ter.)</p>
        <p>A VOICE: You'll have to go olie day when you are not forced! (More laughter.) Alderman EVANS said he was thinking more about the safety of persons using the road than about the beauty or otherwise of the gates.</p>
        <p>Councillor ARCHER said that the committee went into the matter thoroughly before making what they considered to be the best suggestions possible, and it was a thousand pities that Alderman Bennett did not collaborate with them when they were drawing up their pro- posals. It was preposterous to And these paltry objections raised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>DIFFICULTY IN THE WAY.</head>
        <p>A DIFFICULTY IN THE WAY.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY 31Avon (Dr. Joseph), replying on behalf of the committee, said that with regard to the suggestion that the shelter at the Ceme- tery would be dangerous, the Surveyor informed him that it would stand 18 feet back from the tramway track and that the position of the lamp, at present there, would be altered. The committee were unanimously agreed That the site they recommended was the best one. It afforded one a splendid opportunity for standing under cover whilst admiring and musing over the esthetic beauty of the gates. (Laughter.) As to the shelter for the Bridge-street cars, if the site were altered it would be neither con- venient for the Latchford passengers, nor for those for Stockton Heath, and the commiffee could not get permission to have a lean-to affixed to the, premises in the part of the street where it was thought the shelter should be. Alderman Tnonox said that, in these days of fast moving traffic, he would like to be assured that the committee did not contemplate putting up any shelter that would Se an bbstruction to the vision of either those -using the road or those going about in motor cars and such like. At a tram terminus it was important that noth- ing should be allowed that would interfere with the view of those either getting on or off the cars, or of those in charge of Other vehicles. As to the shelter for the Bridge-street cars, the Alderman asked whether it would not be pos- sible to put up a lean-to shelter that did not touch the building of the gentleman who re- fused his permission? , The lifAvon : Although the shelter might not touch his premises, there's the question of his light to be remembered. Alderman SMETHURST : We did all we could to get his permission, but he imposed restrictions we could not possibly agree to. The MAYOR said that, with regard to the shel- ter at the Cemetery, it was proposed to move the parapet seven feet farther away, so that traffic would not be interfered with. The amendment was defeated, only the pro- poser and seconder voting for it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>ST. BENEDICTS TEA AND DANCE.</head>
        <p>ST. BENEDICTS TEA AND DANCE.</p>
        <p>Mayor's Good _Wishes for Soldier</p>
        <p>Guests.</p>
        <p>A numerous company spent a most enjoyable evening in the Borough Gymnasium on Monday evening, the occasion being the tenth annual congregational tea party in connection with St. Benedict's R.C. Church. Those present in- cluded the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Peter Peacock), who were accorded an en-</p>
        <p>thusiastic welcome 'Upon entering the room. Tea was served by a willing band of lady members of the congregation, trays of good things having been kindly given by the follow- ing: Lady Greenall, Mesdames Densham, McNichol', Jenks, F. E. Dean, Burn, Morriss, Hardiman, Brady, Dean, Rigby and McGrail, the Catholic Young Men's Society, and Messrs. Peter Walker and Son.</p>
        <p>_ After tea came darking, the music for which was supplied by Mr. Thomas. —Blake's Band. Messrs. Peter Leonard, Albert Edwards, and James Carey acted as M.C.'s. During an interval in the dance programme the Mayor expressed the pleasure it gave the Mayoress and himself to be present at so enjoy- able a gathering, and wished the chnrch and congregation every success in the future. The Rev. Father-Swarbrick thanked his Wor- ship and the Mayoress for their attendance, and the Mayor for his kind remarks. He also expressed the indebtedness of all present to the donors of trays and to all who had assisted in making the funeVon so enjoyable and such a big success.</p>
        <p>A number of soldiers, home on furlough, were present by invitation, and to these the Mayor addressed a few appropriate words, wishing them every success when the time comes for them to go •to the Front, and a safe return.</p>
        <p>TOBACCO FOR SAILORS.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Philomaths have contributed quite an appreciable quantity of tobacco to the "comfort" funds for the Navy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>MARKET GATE SeHEME.</head>
        <p>MARKET GATE SeHEME.</p>
        <p>Brewers Appear in apposition to eouncil Proposals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>LoePiL GOVERNMENT BOARD INQUIRY.</head>
        <p>LoePiL GOVERNMENT BOARD INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>"The Corporation of Warrington" (to use the phraseology of the statutory notice) " hav- ing presented a petition to the Local Govern- ment Board to issue a Provisional Order to empower the said Council to put in force the powers of the Land Clauses Act with respect to the purchase and taking of land otherwise than by agreement with reference to certain land required by the said Council for the widening and improvement of Market Gate and Sankey-street," Mr. F. 0. Stamford) A.M.1.C.E., one of the Local Government Board inspectors, held a public inquiry into the Council's appli- cation at the Town Hall on Thursday morning. The case for the Council was put before the Inspector by Mr. A. T. Hallaway (the Deputy Town Clerk) and Mr. Herbert Hatton, solicitor (Messrs. Robert Davies and Co.), appeared to offer opposition on behalf of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and C,0., the Wilderspool brewers, two of whose licensed houses would be affected by the Council's proposals. No other opposition was forthcoming. _</p>
        <p>Those present-included the Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock), Aldermen Bennett and John Evans, Councillors Gough and Pemberton, the Borough Surveyor (Mr. Andrew M. Ker), and the Assistant Borough Surveyor Mr. M. A. Piercey), Mr. J. E. Seeker (a director of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and Co.), and Messrs. Segar Owen and Garnett (architects).</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S LEGACY OF NARROW</p>
        <p>STREETS.</p>
        <p>in the course of the statement with which he opened the case for the Council, Mr. Holloway said that like many other old towns, Warrington had received a legacy of very narrow streets, and the policy of the Cor- poration for the past thirty years had been to widen them up to something like modern re- quirements, doing the work by a gradual pro- cess, in order that the cost might be spread over a long period of years. The town was roughly divided into quarters by four main arteries—Sankey, Buttermarket, Bridge, and Horsemarket- streets. Sankey and Butter- market streets were traversed by practically the whole of the vehicular traffic between Man- chester and Liverpool, whilst the other two thoroughfares named connected a large and populous district, along a line drawn between Manchester and Liverpool, with South-west Lancashire. The traffic passing through the heart of Warrington was very heavy; indeed, so heavy—especially since motor traction came into such general use—that the streets which sufficed thirty or forty years ago were no longer adequate. Consequently the Council decided on a big scheme of street widening which em- braced all the four streets he had mentioned, A portion of the scheme had been carried out, and it had been intended now to complete the scheme, but unfortunately the war broke out, and the Council now felt that they would not be doing their duty as citizens if they endeav- oured to even slightly hamper the Government by floating a large loan. A Limited Scheme Owing to the War. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>As a result they had decided for the time being to limit their scheme to that portion of it which is known locally as the Market Gate improvement. The market at the present time was approached from the southern side of the town through Corporation-street, which was about thirty feet wide; through Cheapside, which had an effective width of ten feet; through a passage called Temple Court, with an effective width of five feet; and, lower down, through two narrow footways, one called Castle- street and the other of so insignificant a char- acter that it had not been dignified by a name at all. The idea of t4e Council in this matter, as in all others, wad to give the people some- thing better than they already had, and what they proposed to do was to widen Castle-street, which at the present time was an exceedingly useless public highway, from a traffic point of view, but extremely convenient from the point of view of \people who desired to go to certain premises unseen. (Laughter.) , The Council's proposal was to widen Castle-street from a pre- sent minimum width of five '?et to a minimum width of 40 feet, with a width in its widest part of 45 feet to 46 feet.</p>
        <p>New Street for Old.</p>
        <p>That street, so widened, it was proposed to substitute for Corporation-street, as an ap- proach to the Market, the public tlius Offing a roadway that was ten feet wider than the exist- ing one leading to the Market from Sankey- street. Under the scheme, Corporation-street was to be done away with; it became building land, joining up to the site which would be available at Market Gate. The Market Hall having in due course been pulled down, Castle- street widened, and Corporation-street closed, the proposal was to close Cheapside altogether, and to link up the site of Vire-Market Hall with the site of the Market Gate improvements, and to construct a road on the northern side of the Market Hall, right through from the Market- place, widening out the present alley of four feet, and making an effective entrance into Horsemarket-street. The result would be that the Council would have an area surrounded on all sides by streets which were quite sufficient and ample for the traffic which would go through them. Those proposals were part of a very carefully considered and deliberate scheme to obtain the largest possible area of building land at Market Gate in order that the Council might carry out their plan of reinstating the greatest possible number of businesses,affected by the improvements. The properties involved in that part of the scheme which was beiiig dealt with that day, included the Blackburne Arms Hotel and the Pelican Inn, which were the property of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., and ob- jections had been lodged to the scheme by, that firm, and also by Mrs. Crompton, who was the tenant of the Blackburn° Arms. Corporation Open to Come to an Arrangement. The Council had had a great many dealings with Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. in the past and had always been able to come to a settlement with them amicably, either by the , parties agreeing between themselves, or by re- ferring the point at issue to some competent tribunal. He could only say that in the present matter the Corporation were prepared to deal with Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. on the same lines as they were with everyone else con- cerned, viz., if possible, to come to some arrange- ment with them for reinstatement, though, of course, the fact that theirs was licensed pro- perty placed certain special difficulties in the way. It was not part of the policy of the Cor- poration to spend any more money than was absolutely necessary, nor was it part of their policy to reduce licences at the expense of the rates—on the Council they were neither teetotalers, nor were they interested in licensed houses; -their only consideration being the cheapest way they could effect what would undoubtedly be a great public improvement. GROUNDS OF MESSRS. GREENALL'S</p>
        <p>of the most valuable licensed premises in the town. The reinstatement that had been spoken of could well be done without taking his clients' property and without closing Corpora- tion-s.treet, which he, incidentally, pointed out lay in a straight line with Barbauld-street-- which the Council had recently acquired and laid out—provided through communication were established. As regards the Pelican, that was another valuable licensed house, rebuilt some few years ago in accordance with plans approved by the Licensing Authority. Shortly stated, his objection to the Council's proposal to com- pulsorily acquire that house was that what they were trying to do was to buy land, under a Pro- visional Order, in order to enable them to sell to the best advantage land they already held. There was no evidence that the result of such a transaction would show a balance in favour of the Corporation, and there was a presability, or at any rate a possibility, that the result of such a transaction would be the other way. A Suggested Way Out.</p>
        <p>There was a way out, viz.: that the Corpora- tion should acquire the yard of the Pelican, and leave the house standings That would give them all they required, and all that his clients would ask would be that the Corporation should give them, as they would be able to do, a yard in substitution for the one they took. If the Deputy Town Clerk would withdraw the Pelican from the scheme, he (Mr. Hatton) was prepared to give an undertaking to convey the existing yard of the inn in exchange for a substituted site.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallaway said his instructions *ere to proceed with the application, but if Mr. Hatton was prepared to withdraw his objection in the case of the Blackburne Arms, very probably the parties would be able to come to an amicable arrangement. Mr. Hatton said it was rather difficult to come to any arrangement of that sort at the last moment, and , went on to contend that the acquisition of the Pelican Inn was not neces- sary for any scheme. Just in the same way as he had contended that it was open to the Cor- poration to have scheduled part of the Black- burne Arms only, so he also contended that only part of the Pelican need have been scheduled. Mr. Hallaway replied that it was a point of law that when applying for a Provisional Order part only of a property could not be scheduled. Mr. Hatton: If you drop the Pelican now, the matter can be got over by arrangement. I claim that the acquisition of this inn is not shown to be absolutely necessary to the scheme in the sense it ought to be shown to be necessary before my clients can be compelled to sell it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>OBJ ECTI ON.</head>
        <p>OBJ ECTI ON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hatton then 'addressed the Inspectdr, re- marking that as large owners of licensed pro- perty in the town his- clients had an interest, quite apart from that specially attaching to the Blackburne Arms and the Pelican Inn, in seeing that the proposed scheme was carried out in the best possible way and at the lease cost to the town, for they owned the whole of the pro- perty on the east side of the Market-place and two shops and the Post Office on the west side. It was quite true that hitherto his clients had always met the Corporation in a friendly and helpful spirit, and they would not be opposing that day if they did not considek tharthe scheme was one which ought to be opposed to the ut- most. He contended that the schenle of_widen- ing which the Corporation had in view could be carried out without taking the Blackburne Arms at all; though he agreed that a small pro- jecting portion of the hostelry must be acquired if the scheme as at present outlined were to be carried out. But it was not necessary to "buy the whole of the premises, and had Messrs. Grceuall been approachej they would readily have agreed to the sale of the projecting portion, without requiring the Council to buy the whole of the premises. An Undertaking Offered.</p>
        <p>He was prepared to give a binding undertak- ing as to that, for his clients were prepared to enter into any undertaking that might be reasonably necessary to enable the widening of Sankey-street to be carried out. The scheme in view, he submitted, could be carried out with- out taking the whole of the house; without going to the unnecessary expense of buying one</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>MR. SEGAR OWEN'S VIEWS.</head>
        <p>MR. SEGAR OWEN'S VIEWS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Seia"; Owen, architect, 3;i-as then called' by Mr. Hatton, and, in the course of evidence, said that he Viewed the proposal to give access to the Market-place via Castle-street as a mistake, as, by retaining Corporation-street, the Council would have a central entrance, instead of one at the side. He believed that it was physically impossible to carry out the scheme of reinstate- ment of the dispossessed property owners and at the same time keep open a means of access to the Market-place from Sankey-street. He be- lieved that it was possible to reinstate those who were likely to require reinstatement, leav- ing Corporation-street and the Blackburne Arms as they at present stood. As regards the Pelican, if the Corporation would take the yard of the inn and give an equivalent yard space either at the rear or at the side, they would have a relative depth as great as the present shops enjoyed. Mr. Owen was subjected to a long cross-exami- nation by the Deputy Town Clerk. "Is not the sum and substance of your objec- tion this: Take any scheme you like, but leave us the Blackburne Arms?" asked Mr. Hallaway ; to which Mr. Owen replied: "I don't thsnk that is quite fair. I say that we can show that leaving the Blackburne Arms as it is you have land behind that will suit your purpose." As to the Pelican, your objection, so far as I can gather it, is that we are taking the place? —You are taking it when it is unnecessary.</p>
        <p>CENTRAL APPROACH RATHER THAN A SIDE ONE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hatton, addressing the Inspector, con- tended that it was not necessary to take his clients' property in either case. Mention had been made of the possibility of running Bar- bauld-street through so as to continue it in a line with Corporation-street, and if it were true that that was not in contemplation he thought it was a very foolish thing to have gone to the expense of acquiring the street and paving it, whilst he also thought that it would be far better that the street which led from Sankey- street into the Market Place should be in a cen- tral position, as was Corporation-street, rather than that the place should be approached by a dog-leg-like street in one corner, such as was proposed. THE BEST SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallaway said that when the opposition was examined it all resolved itself into this: That the Council were proposing to take the Blackburne Arms and the Pelican. An alterna- tive scheme had been suggested for the purpose of saving the Blackburne Arms; a scheme which he thought, could not be compared with the scheme which the Council had outlined. In- stead of a straight street into Market-street it was suggested that they should have an entrance via narrow Corporation-street, so that which- ever side of the Market- Place one wished to go to he had to turn twice at right angles, which was most inconvenient, especially for vehicular traffic, of which, naturally, there was a great deal in the vicinity of the market. He suggested that the Corporation ought not to have their scheme of improvement held up in order that one or two particular pieces of pro- perty might be left untouched, and ventured to think that a scheme which was brought for- ward for that one purpose, and that alone, was not a good scheme. As to the suggestions that had been made with regard to the Pelican, all he could • say was that if the Inspector could honestly come to the conclusion that in order to save that place the Council should go absolutely contrary to all the canons of good architecture and good town-planning, he (the speaker) would regret that he had ever had to .argue that application. The position of the Council was this: The scheme which they had put forward was a scheme which would give them the greatest effective area of building land and would enable them to reinstate the majo- rity of the tenants whose premises would be affected; and the fact that the Blackburne Arms and the Pelican Inn happened to be con- cerned must not be allowed to stand in the way of what would undoubtedly be a great public improvement. _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The inquiry was then closed, and the inspec- tor went to view the various streets and pro- perties mentioned.</p>
        <p>" Prudential " Staff Dinner.</p>
        <p>This annual event took place on Thursday evening at the Winmarleigh Cafe, when, on the invitation of the superintendent (Mr. S. Brocklehurst) and his assistants, a company of 50 sat down. Amongst the guests were the medical referees (Drs. Ferguson and Hodgkin- son). Justice having been done to a well- served dinner, Mr. Brocklehurst read a letter of apology for non-attendance from the inspec- tor of the division (Mr. James Moon); also a letter from the general manager congratulating the district on its good results during the past critical year, and expressing the hope that 1915 would show even greater results. After briefly referring to the serious crisis through which the country was passing, the chairman went on to express the personal pleasure that the efforts of the staff had given him during the past year, and 'said he felt certain that except for unforeseen obstacles, 1915 would be his record year in Warrington. Mr. F. Mann, the company's senior assistant in Lancashire, referred to his coming retire- ment after 31 years' service with the company, and mentioned that he had accepted a generous pension. Before the proceedings closed a pre- sentation from his colleagues, in the shape of a handsome bookcase and bureau was made to him, and many touching expressions of regret were voiced at his departure. A small presen- tation was also made to Mr. W. Hayes, another assistant, who was leaving the district to take charge of a Prescot section. During the evening songs and recitations were rendered by Miss Brough and members of the staff, Mrs. Dunn ably acting as accom- panist</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <p>40.04.!`.410,,W*‘164i0...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>ANSWERED THE CALL.</head>
        <p>ANSWERED THE CALL.</p>
        <p>Fir st Experiences and Impressions of Warrington Recruit.</p>
        <p>THE SPIRIT THAT WINS.</p>
        <p>Several members of the staff of "The Examiner" have joined the ranks, and alt have spoken highly of their experiences of the new mode of life a Kaiser's madness has thrust upon them. The latest recruit, Mr. F. -Kay, of the report- ing department, suczurnbee, to the attraction of the colours a little over It week ago. He sends us the following acc*unt of his first day experiences, which wilt be of general interest, as it approximately represents' the novel routine' through which hundreds of thousands of young men are now passing.</p>
        <p>A week ago I was one of the thousands in the country who, through` the medium of huge posters, advertisements and newApaper reports, was cognisant of the fact that the King and country needed all the men available to uphold our honour in face of the whole' world, but had failed to answer \" the call." Now, I can say, and proudly, too, that I have done as thousands of others have done—and as thousands of others should do—joined the ranks of Kitchener's Army. I have had a chance of studying what "Tommy" is really like, in a great barracks town, and, in the first place, before going into detail, I must say that I have been surprised to find that the war is a subject of which very little is spoken. I find that surprising, as it must seem to a " am picking up the slang—that it leads to one of " Tommy's " chief characteris- tics—to speak but little, but do a great deal. I don't mean to say that " Tommy " does not use his tongue. Oh no; he is very voluble on general topics, biit the war does not get as much discussion as one would expect amongst those who are the fighters. I have heard less war talk here than in one trip to Stockton Heath on the ear.</p>
        <p>The Only Desire. But the mention of Stockton Heath reminds me that, perhaps, I should relate what hap- pened from the time I left Warrington to arriv- ing here "down South." I must confess I had mingled feelings of pride and regret when I left the Police Station With a "medically fit" certificate. I was proud of the fact that I was fit, but regretted that, being fit, I had not offered my services before. I was somewhat sur- prised at the nubiber of men who were rejected on account of having bad teeth, but was de- lighted to hear, when men were asked if they wanted to join any particular regiment, "Oh ! any one will do, so long as lam in." That is a capital spirit- for a start, and leads to many great things. Travelling from Warrington in the train with three other recruits was very pleasant, and we spent the hours in laughing and joking, and wished each other the best of luck when we had to part to change trains. When I arrived at my destination I was in a bit of a fix, for with hundreds of barrack squares I had no idea where to go for my head- quarters. I soon found out that " Tommy " likes his little joke, for when I asked for the headquarters, I was told "C" square, "G" block. Well, I found the square and the block, and on inquiring was politely informed that I had come too far. I was buffeted about like that- for about half an hour, until, meeting one who looked more like a funeral attender than a practical joker, I repeated my inquiry. I took his word, but learned the wisdom of the moral, "Don't judge by appearances," for he had sent me further out of my way than ever. But " Tommy " knows a joke can go too far, and upon telling one sturdy artilleryman that I had been fooled around about five times, he directed me correctly.</p>
        <p>A Universal Rising.</p>
        <p>I was one of a Platy of over 100 men waiting to report, and it was still early morning. Formalities over, I entered my "block" and waited for dinner. I got chatting to a Scots- man from Glasgow, and together we had a • "walk round." From snatches of conversation I got some idea of the many parts of the coun- try represented. There were Lancashire lads, with their " Ayes " and " Nows "; brawny sons of Somerset, with " Yez " and " Noo"; Cock- neys, with their particular twang ; and I should think there were men from almost every county in England, not to mention those from Scot- land, Wales and Ireland. There are a number of Warringtonians here, and several I know personally who have left good situations for the time being to do their duty. One man came down in a taxi cab, attired in a light grey suit, with spats and patent leather boots complete. An officer? Not he, but a private pure and simple, quartered in the next block to myself for his first night. Of course, when a man joins the Army he does not expect to live in a palace, and to one who leaves a comfortable home, with every attention,' it is a drastic change to be shown a mattress on the floor with two or three blankets, and told, "There's your 'kip,' but don't turn in yet; someone else might have to join you." And where everyone goes for the first night it is generally the case that two "beds" are occupied by three soldiers. "Lights out" goes soon after nine, and just when y.ou are getting "nice and comfortable "—lucky if you do—the order comes "Come on out of it." Five o'clock and a wet morning! Phew! how cold it was my first morning, as I tumbled along with about twenty others to a tap out- side, where we had a " swill " in ice-cold water. You make a resolution and do it, and after three or four days of the same thing think prac- tically little of it. Then comes the ordinary routine—breakfast, "fatigue," or a stroll— about ten miles—dinner, "fatigue," 'or nothing, if you can manage to keep out of the sergeant's way, and then tea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Comfortable Billlt.</head>
        <p>Comfortable Billlt.</p>
        <p>There is one thing to be said about this sta- tion, and that is that the food is not at all bad, although roughly served. At present lamin a. comfortable billet in some schools, and the only fault to find with it is that we have to rise at 4.30 to get down to barracks to parade with the whole party in good time for breakfast. There is plenty of hard work to be done, and more often than not of a very different character to what one has been accustomed. I, myself, am feeling pretty stiff, but hard work never killed anyone, and it must be remembered that the men who will drive the Huns ott of France and Belgium, and cripple the German militarist system for ever, will not be milksops, but men of calibre, and barracks life trains a man to get the most out of the limbs which God gave him to use against those who blaspheme and play the hypocrite, as "the Willies" are doing to- day.</p>
        <p>To those young men who would rather sit in an armchair and read the war news than sacri- fice comfort and help to win the great fight, I would say, "Stay at home and put every feather in its place in the bed"; but to those who are willing to make sacrifices in the way of comfort and who are prepared to work hard, the advice is "Join to-day." There are hun- dreds here who have had every luxury in life, but "rough it with a smile, knowing that they are doing their duty. "Will you do yours?"</p>
        <p>In the near future I will give a more de- tailed description of the life and duties, and then those who are hesitating may be able to decide what to do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>"HONORARY-PRO. TEM."</head>
        <p>"HONORARY-PRO. TEM."</p>
        <p>To prevent as far as possible the loss of its members now serving with the forces, the Philomathic Society has resolved that these gentlemen shall be created honorary members untl the end of the war.</p>
      </div>
