      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Taradiddles,</p>
        <p>Surely that was one of the angling craft whom I encountered in the train the other day. I could see he was wellnigh bursting to talk, so I made the opportunity for chin-wagging on his part, and he didn't half _avail himself of it. Said he:</p>
        <p>"I'm hoping to catch that express for New- castle, for it's very important, by Jove !" " Ah," I returned, sympathetically, not to say encouragingly, "case of life and death, eh?" "I don't mind telling you," he went on. "Fact is, I got a wire from the War Office this morning ordering me to proceed to Newcastle at once to act as substitute for a dispatch rider. Poor beggar's been taken suddenly _ill. See?"</p>
        <p>" Just-S-o. And your bike is—?" "Oh, I don't need that, don't you know? They supply us with bikes; in fact, they give us a new one every week !" He looked out of the corner of his eye to see if I took the bait all right, and, apparently satisfied that he would have no difficulty in Landing his supposed gudgeon, proceeded:' "Yesterday, I was at Sheffield, and saw 'em making big guns—bigger than any of those Ger- man rotters. By-the-bye, you're not one of those newspaper Johnnies, so I may as well con- fide in you a wee bit more. Lord Kitchener was there on a visit of inspection, and he and I went all over the belly place together, arrang- ing—but, of course, I musn't go into details." "Certainly not," I assented; then, "By-the- way, do you happen by any chance to have taken part in the ' Come-to-Church ' Cam- paign?" "Here, I say," said fie, suspiciously, are you getting at?"</p>
        <p>"who</p>
        <p>I was just about to ask you that ver- ques- tion," I murmured; and somehow he withdrew into his shell and didn't tell me any more nice :tittle stories.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Why Don't Men Go To Church?</head>
        <p>Why Don't Men Go To Church?</p>
        <p>That allusion to the Free Churches' cam- paign, formally closed last Sunday, reminded me of some of tKe excuses of the non-churchgoer dealt with by Councillor Jos. Hawthorn at the Wycliffe P.S.A. Essentially the religious pro- blems of the day are implied in the questions: "Why do our people in such large numbers leave the churches on one side?" and "Why do not the churches concentrate on their work rather than on their rivalries?" In a recent substantial contribution to the discussion of the subject, Mr. W. Forbes Gray mentions among other causes for the alienation of many workers from the churches the belief that "organised religion is indifferent, and, in some instances, antagonistic to the ideals of labour. . . Until the churches seriously address themselves to the task of enforcing the claims of social Christianity, they cannot hope to win back large masses of the people whom tlieT should never have lost."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Common Excuse.</head>
        <p>A Common Excuse.</p>
        <p>As to the divided state of Christendom, which is cited as another cause, we are told that "the paramount duty of the churches is t'o thoroughly. master the facts of the situation, then to sum- mon all the moral, intellectual, and spiritual forces at their command, for an attack on the heathenism of our cities." I am sure that at least so far-as the Nonconformist Churches of 'own vwn country are concerned, outside critics make far too much of these supposed divisions. We have travelled a long, long way toward real unity as distinguished from mere paper union since the days when one clerical scholar con- signed another to .everlasting perdition because they could not agree about the use of a Greek particle. Some of us old stagers, indeed, are in- clined to fear that our modern scholars are rushing us along at a breakneck pace, and that in sweeping.mway the miracles and all mystery from what we have been accustomed to regard as the Divine revelation, spiritual loss must re- sult. Not long before his death, Dr. Parker thus summed up the situation in his charac- teristic way: "There was no den, there were no anis, and worst of all, there was no Daniel."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>Another Objection.</head>
        <p>Another Objection.</p>
        <p>Perhaps I am too old-fashioned to appreciate the higher criticism at its true value. Any- how, to return for a moment to our subject proper, Mr. Ramsay Macdonald observes that there is a tendency at work to-day crushing out the spiritual life of the people. At one end of society it is luxury; at the other, it is haras- sing poverty—or the fear of poverty—the fear being more devastating than the actuality. Men lose their spiritual appetites when life crushes too heavily upon them." After all, along with some religious fanaticism, there have in the present century been widespread frivol- ity, subordination of duty to pleasure, add neg- lect of public worship, and these habits, although doubtless attributable to a reaction from the former systematic cramping of the animal spirits of (young folli,"are surely not less objectionable from the point of view of the moralist and the true patriot. "There have been,' I repeat, for there are unmistakable signs of the awakening of a better spirit. Great troubles, losses, disappointments, humiliations, produce one or other effect on the character: they either soften and sweeten, or they harden and sour. It would be easy to illustrate and support this by evidence—appeals to his- tory and the like—but I must abruptly end the homily into which I have been betrayed by re- marking that the present national crisis has had a wonderfully sobering effect upon us as a nation, and has revealed great moral and spirit* qualities previously unsuspected by many of us more or less pessimistic old fogeys. When all is said and done, church-going is not necessarily the final test of essential goodness.</p>
        <p>Unwritten Wills. The paragraph "What a codicil lost the bor- ough" in last week's local news, somehow set me thinking of what a soldier lately told me ab )ut will-making on the battle field and at sea. I was curious to know what all his pockets were for—assuming that they were real pockets. He assured me they were, and explained their uses, one containing a dressing for wnds, another a form of will, and so on. "But you must know," added my friend in khaki, "that a man —and when I say a man I mean a soldier turned 21—may make a verbal will. It'll be all right enough as long as he takes care to do it in the presence and hearing of .friends willing to act as legal witnesses; and if he can secure an</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
