      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>fetus to tht abitor</head>
        <p>fetus to tht abitor</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. THE EDITOR of "The Examiner" invites corre-</p>
        <p>spondence on all subjects of public interest. LETTERS should be brief and pointed. Their object must be to impart or seek information, not abuse.</p>
        <p>LETTERS must in all cases be accompanied by the name and address of the writer. COMMUNICATIONS received early in the week— except urgent matters—can always be given the best attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>"WAITING; IN THE CEMETERY.</head>
        <p>"WAITING; IN THE CEMETERY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am pleased that your editorial embody- ing my letter has 'received a reply from our worthy Rector and "A Nonconformist." I knew the arrangement was the Rector's suggestion. If, after a trial of 20 years, it is still unsatisfactory to, and a hardship on, the public, it is no credit to the Church of England Clergy that they have continued it for so long. Even if five or six clergy have occa- sionally to spend an hour at the Cemetery, it is the duty of each to do so.</p>
        <p>I do not think one man can do the work of six "properly and reverently" in a quarter of the time.</p>
        <p>I also stated that the clergy were willing to be present if requested, but I repeat my query:</p>
        <p>"Why should it be necessary to ask a clergy- man to do what is undoubtedly his duty, viz., to bury the dead of his own parish?"</p>
        <p>"A Nonconformist," I trust, noted that I made no charge against his ministers in this matter. Yet there are many suggestions in his letter which are worthy of consideration by the Cemetery Committee.</p>
        <p>A. M. OURNER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>EARLIER CLOSING OF PUBLIC-HOUSES.</head>
        <p>EARLIER CLOSING OF PUBLIC-HOUSES.</p>
        <p>Sir,—As one of "Uncle Ned's " nephews and a juvenile Rechabite, it gave me the greatest pleasure to notice in "The Examiner" last Saturday the brilliant example that the authorities of Stockton Reath and the outlying districts have shown to the authorities of this town of Warrington in passing a resolution for shortening the hours for tife sale of intoxicating liquors in public-houses during the pf6ent war. I should indeed be grateful if the authorities of Warrington would earnestly consider this important matter,. and let us all have some- thing to be thankful for. For when we con- sider, the seriousness of the present war and the disadvantages that are likely to be brought about, it would, in my opinion, be one of the finest things that could be .done if we lessened the country's expenditure on intoxicating liquors. It would benefit us all, and make us more fit to battle against hardships that are, and will continue to be, set up by this great European war.</p>
        <p>REGINALD GALLOP</p>
        <p>72, Norris-street.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln opening my " Examiner " on Satur- day last I was delighted to read your _splendid leading article, "Wake up, Warrington.!" and I would like to take this opportunity of thank- ing you very sincerely for your outspokenness and your nnflinching devotion to the cause of temperance.</p>
        <p>The demand for earlier closing of public- houses never was more urgent than it is at the present time, seeing that drunkenness among our soldiers is on the increase. Bravo, Stockton Heath! You have led the way. You have listened to the appeal of Lord Kitchener and you are going to play your.part. I raise my hat to you! But what is Warrington going to do? "Busi- ness as usual?" Is the Trade still going to turn a deaf ear to the various appeals for sobriety among our soldiers?</p>
        <p>A WORKING MAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>BEGIN THE RIGHT WAY</head>
        <p>BEGIN THE RIGHT WAY</p>
        <p>TO CURE ANEMIA.</p>
        <p>Beginning the right way means half the battle in curing bloodlessness, or amemia—to , employ the medical term. Without a sufficient supply of good red blood in their veins. women (and men as well) become pale, sallow, languid) they suffer from• indigestion, headaches and heart palpitations and never enjoy life. Bloodlessness, if neglected, opens the way to decline and provides victims for a number of diseases, for the reason that an enfeebled system cannot offer full resistance to disease. In treating bloodlessness it is not necessary to take a different medicine for each symptom, for the whole trouble arises in your blood and when the blood supply is improved and in- creased all the symptoms disappear. That is why Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are so valuable to bloodless young women and weak men—they assist in the formation of new blood. There is nothing mysterious in their action; they steadily increase your blood supply. If you are weak, "run-down," and lacking in vitality, get a box of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People at once from any dealer and take them regularly. Notice how soon you begin to have a sharpened appetite and increased energy. Never accept any useless substitutes; if in doubt se,nd 2s. 9d. for one box, or 13s. 9d. for six, to Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn-viaduct, London.</p>
        <p>FREE.—Send a postcard to Book Dept., 46, Holborn-viaduct, • London, for the helpful little book on The Work of the Blood. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST —continued.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST —continued.</p>
        <p>, • (Regd. Trade Mark.) COFFEE ESSENCE The Cofee Maid, of Symington's, says: "My motto is: Keep the kettle boiling !'— Then I can always have a cup of Symington' instantly." \ TIM. SYMINGTON &amp; CO.. EdinburAk and London.</p>
        <p>P7i:"' •</p>
        <p>executor or two to see to the due fulfilment of hie last wishes in. case his wounds prove mor- tal, better still, of course."</p>
        <p>Lost Property. That reference to pockets reminds me that woman seldom has more than one. That is why one so often sees her carrying Ler purse in her hand—and that is why she is so apt to pave it on the table of the waiting-room or on the carriage seat. As to that I could many a tale unfold—but so could most of us, I dare say; hence I forbear, lest I be tedious beyond my wont. But when we are officially informed that during last Year 444 were found in various parts of the borough and deposited at the police office, some of us can hardly help wishing that, as in the case of accused persons, the owners of the lost property in question might, as far as possible, be separately tabulated, so as to show the proportion of ladies among them.</p>
        <p>~• r 1. . 4 •1• 4111, ,s • ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 1915.</p>
        <p>RACIAL EVOLUTION.</p>
        <p>Dr. Manson's Instructive Address to Philomaths,</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington -Philomathic Society was held in Trinity Chambers on Wednesday evening of last week. There was a moderate attendance. The President (Mr. Nor- man Nield) occupied the chair, and Dr. J. S. Manson delivered a most instructive and lucid lecture upon "Social Philosophy and War."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>BIOLOGICAL TRUTH</head>
        <p>A BIOLOGICAL TRUTH</p>
        <p>The Lecturer said social philosophy might be defined as that ground work of principles under- lying the phenomena of society. These princi- ples related to the ethical or spiritual, biological and physical conditions of human life. War might be defined as that social phenomenon which expressed the antagonism of organised communities in terms of physical combat, re- sulting in death or injury to members. The evolutionary hypothesis, as applied to man as a social being, showed how he had progressed from a state of sullen isolation to „a highly or- ganised dependence upon every other member. There were distinctly biological characters of the human family which must be noted. Sur- vival demanded great care and attention, and the primitive emotions aroused by these condi- tions gave rise to patience, forbearance, thought and reflection; these extended to the tribe and to the nation. Thus the savage impulses yielded to deliberation and self-control, and harshness and cruelty to gentleness and terriderhess. On this change depended the ultimate fate of the race, so that the epigram, "Man did not make society; society made man," was biologically true.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>Diversities of the Human Race.</head>
        <p>Diversities of the Human Race.</p>
        <p>The diversities of fhe human race, their pro- gress and decline, was the theme of history. Buckle failed to establish a philosophy of his- tory because he over-estimated the plasticity of the human race, and took little account of those inherent racial 'Clualities which were uninflu- enced by environmental conditions—the exist- ence of which had now been "-ilhown by the science of anthropology. But a race or nation might alter biologically from its parent stock by the infusion of characters alien to it. -In the present war, the German nation regarded the action of Britain ag opposed to the law of biological evolution, because it was a war with kindred races—" our cousins" they called us, although he had never heard the relntionship reciprocated! (Laughter.) Taking this racial alteration by the infusion of alien blood into account, however—as in this country from its original Celtic inhabitants—they saw that this argument could not hold good. Nor did it even hold good in Germany itself, tor In Prussia there was a very large infusion of Slav blood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Malthus and Bernhardi.</head>
        <p>Malthus and Bernhardi.</p>
        <p>In social philosophy, Malthus (who, by the way, was educated at the Warrington Academy sometime during his youth) had pointed out and elaborated the principle of population, saying that it grew in geometrical ratio whilst the means of subsistence only grew in arithmetical ratio. What he called "preventive checks of popu- lation" were wars, famines and pestilences, and he advocated that these miseries might be miti- gated by controlling the rate of reproduction so as to keep the population within the means of subsistence. But Bernhardi stated that "strong, healthy and flourishing nations increase in num- bers. From a-given moment they require a con- tinual expanse of frontiers for the accommoda- tion of their surplus population. And since almost every part of the globe is inhabited, new territory must as a rule bb obtained at the cost of its possessors—that is to say, by conquest, which thus becomes a law of necessity." The moral necess,ity of war had its advocate in Treitschke, who said that war was "God's purge for mankind." Treitschke must have been un- fortunate in his doctor when he went to medi- cine for that metaphor! (Hear, hear.) The sentimental grounds were patriotism and that subtle fascination of novelty and change, and especially alluring by the promise of rapid suc- cess at minimum sacrifice. The Clausewitz doc- trine, "That war is but an extension or policy," came to be accepted by the German people as an elemental principle of State policy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>"Monotony and Stagnation of Peace."</head>
        <p>"Monotony and Stagnation of Peace."</p>
        <p>It is the boast of some German professors, said Dr. Manson, that Germany is the most homogenous national unit on the earth to-day, because national self-consciousness is better de- veloped in its citizens than in those of other nations. While other nations are hap-hazard and seemingly careless of the future, Germany has a mission, a world policy, and for this she deserves credit, if its basis is sound, and in accordance with evolutionary progress. This self-appointed mission of Germany is to spread her culture and organisation, and however ridiculous the vision of a German millenium may appear by reason of its arrogant claims to superiority, yet it teaches to oflier natlons a much needed lesson. It shows that a highly organised and educated nation composed of various states and made up of a race of mixed origin, can be systematically trained to think and act for a common purpose, and why, it may be asked, can other civilised states not simi- larly unite foi a common purpose—such as the practical abolition of war between themselves? With the continued increase of population it is always possible that there must be a struggle for the limited food and fuel supply provided by the earth for its inhabit-ants, and it can hardly be gainsaid that these should be con- served for what we know as the higher civilisa- tion. From this point of view alone does war seem justified on biological and evolutionary theory, and even here there is an alternative— the limitation of reproduction in the lower races.</p>
        <p>War among higher races arrested their evolu- tionary progress by fomenting the baser pas- sions of national hate; it deteriorated the race by exterminating the fitter members and wasted food and fuel to no purpose. Whatever purpose war between Christian States has served in the past, it is now out of date on rational grounds. There will always linger the sentimental attraction, the glamour; excitement and romance, contrasted with the monotony and stagnation of peace. No one has expressed this better than the late William James. "The plain truth is people want war. They want it anyhow. It is the final bouquet or life's fireworks. The born soldier wants it hot and actual. The non-combatants want it in the background, and always as an open pos- sibility to feed the imagination on and keep er... citement going. . . . Let the general possi- bility of war be kept open in Heaven's name for the imagination, to dally with. Let soldiers dream of killing as old maids dream of marry- ing, but organise in every possible way the prac- tical machinery for making each successive chance of war abortive."</p>
        <p>Welfare of the Human Race.</p>
        <p>On what broad principles can this practical machinery be established. First, that war among civilised races is in opposition to the law of biological sociality, except in such grave and tremendous issues when the means of life--food and fuel—is • threatened by the opposition of races of a lower type or civilisation. Second, that even if civilisation spreads over the whole surface of the globe, this main cause of war could be averted by keeping the population within the earth's limits of subsistence. Both these propositions involve two main concep- tions. First, the social co-operation of the civilised nations for. common aims, interests and ideals; that each race, each nation will contribute its part to the development of the common purpose. Second, that man's control of the physical forces of the earth must be paralleled by a similar control of the forces of his own nature.</p>
        <p>What evidence have we that either of these conceptions are already being evolved? Social co-operation is exemplified in the rela- tionship between this country and the U.S., America. •as shown by the Alabama, Behring Sea, and Venezuelan arbitration settlements. All civilised nations are represented at the Hague tribunal, and the peaceful settlement of a dispute between the Argentine and Chili as commemorated by a gigantic statute of the Prince of Peace made from molten cannon, and erected on the. main Andean Pass between the countries, 14,000 feet above sea level. International congresses to promote health and check pestilence and further education are further evidences of social co-operation, by which each nation Dives its contribution to the common stock, but all are animated with the same idea—the future welfare of the race. The quality of the race is being investigated, and tentative efforts to check degenerative ten-</p>
        <p>Continued in next Column,'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>ROBBED A LITTLE CHILD.</p>
        <p>Fo ttr teen-Year-Old _Girl's Impudent</p>
        <p>Theft.</p>
        <p>A girl aged 14 appeared at a Juvenile Court on Wednesday for having stolpn 2s. 6d. in silver from a child aged 6, outside the Lovely- lane Co-operative Stores, about a week ago. , It was stated that defendant stopped the *child in the lane and asked her if she was going to the stores and how niuch she had got. The child told her 35., whereupon the •elder girl knoeked the money out of her hand on to the ground, and picking up the 2s. 6d. piece and the 6d:, handed the latter back to the child, keeping the former. She then said: "I'll treat you to the picture-place to-night," and ran off. It was found she had spent Is. 6d. When Detective-sergeant Reddington was called in, the child gave a good description of the ' girl who had taken the money, and thus the thief was found.</p>
        <p>The girl's father was ordered to pay ss. and costs, and to refund the stolen money to the child's mother.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>" BOOKIE'S " PLEA.</head>
        <p>THE " BOOKIE'S " PLEA.</p>
        <p>"Only One Eye and Crippled."</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor (Alderman Peac&amp;k), Alder- men Smethurst, Bolton and Evans, and Messrs. S. Wright, T. Locker, W. Furness and A. J. Jolley, William Robert Hunt, labourer, of 144, Eve- lyn-street, was summoned for loitering in Liver- pool-road on Saturday, January 30, for the pur- pose of betting.—He pleaded guilty. Detective-Sergeant Holland stated that on the day in question, in company with Detective Skelton, he saw the defendant standing in front of the Coach and Horses, Liverpool-road, from 12.25 to 12.40 p.m. During that time several men went to Hunt and, after a conversation, handed something to him. One gave him a piece of paper coloured red. When the witness approached the defendant and took him into custody Hunt said "Why don't you take others as well as me?" —There was £l3 13s. 54d. in his possession, also three football coupons, which had been made out relating to football matches played that day, 16 plain coupons, and four horse racing bets, relating to some horses run- ning at Kempton Park on Saturday. When asked if he had anything to say, Hunt replied that he was very sorry. He had only one eye and was a cripple. Supt. Williamson said the defendant was very sorry he was caught, and mentioned that the police had great difficulty in catching these men, and he must ask for the support of the magis- trates in dealing with them. A fine of .115 and costs was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>DEPRESSED SINCE HUSBAND'S DEATH. •</head>
        <p>DEPRESSED SINCE HUSBAND'S DEATH. •</p>
        <p>Cut Her Throat.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (Alderman Peacock) and other magistrates at the Borough Police Court on Monday, Mary Alice Yarwood (57), widow, of 8, Gari- baldi-street, was charged with attempting to commit suicide by cutting her throat with a knife at the address stated on the previous Tuesdtfy. _ _</p>
        <p>Supt. Williamson stated that on Tuesday morning last, about six o'clock, when the woman's son went to work, she got up and gave him a cup of tea. She appeared to be all right then. When the son returned at 8.30 a.m. he found his mother sitting in a chair by the fire with a knife by her side. She told him that she had been trying to do- away with herself. He called in a doctor, and the woman was sub- sequently removed to the Hospital. It appeared that the woman lost her husband some months ago, and had had a good deal a trouble, -and had been drinking the last feW weeks. In reply to the charge, accused said she had been very depressed since she lost her husband, and had had no means coming into the house. A married son came forward and said he would be prepared to take charge of his mother. The magistrates agreed to dismiss the case, and handed the woman over to the care of the married son on condition that she signed the pledge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. R. T. Fairclough (in the chair), Alderman Tinnion, and Messrs. T. Pember- ton, A. J. Jolley and J. Upson. Robbing Pawnbrokers.—The solicitors' bench was littered with clothing apparel which Sarah Jane Houghton, a married woman, residing in Haydock-street, was charged on remand with stealing.--I-Supt. Williamson said there were two charges of theft against the prisoner. One was stealing, during the last 2 months, from the pawnshop, 55, Pinners-brow, a quantity of clothing, value £1 95., the property of Mar- garet Mather; and, secondly, with stealing dur- ing the same period lothing from the pawn- shop, 133. Orford-lane, value 145., the property of G. II Jackson and Sons. He stated that the woman had been in the habit of pledging numerous articles, and the pawnbrokers became suspicious. A watch was set on the woman, and she was arrested. The woman destroyed the pawn tickets, so that h•)* husband would not know what she had been doing. During the past feiv wears she had been before the Court on three previous occasions.—She was sentenced to three months' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>COLNTV PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>COLNTV PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>before Messrs. Roger C. Parr (in the chair)</p>
        <p>and -W. Furness</p>
        <p>Robbed Servant and Fellow Lodger.s.—Wright Haslam, weaver, lodging at 478, Wargrave-road, Newton-le-Willows, was Charged with having between January 26th and 27th stolen Bd. in copper, a silver watch and guard .(value 405.), and a pair of men's boots (value 4s, 6d.), respec- tively the properties of May Dearden, William Davies and Henry Walker.—He pleaded guilty. —May Dearden, servant at the house, said she left her satchel hanging on the kitchen wall on the night of the 26th, and when she got up the next morning found the money gone.— William Davies, a joiner, lodging at the same house, said his watch and guard were taken from his bedroom whilst he was out working before breakfast on the 27th. When he' came back for his meal they were gone.--Further evi- dence showed that Haslam pawned the boots under the name of John King, 16, Rose-street, Liverpool. It was stated that prisoner had re- spectable parents at Darwen, but that they would have nothing to do witii him. Ife- was separated from his wife, who was in service at Morecambe, whilst his child was kept- some- where in Darwen.—Sentence of one month's bard labour was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>RACIAL EVOLUTlON—Continued.</head>
        <p>RACIAL EVOLUTlON—Continued.</p>
        <p>dencies are being made. The biologist and the men and women of ;the mediCal profession are greatly concerned in all these questions, and to no other profession or class is war so futile. Professor Hobhbuse, in "Development and Purpose," says: "If the world process were to be arrested here, it *might plausibly be con- tended that in the actual fruition, the life of Athens Was something finer and more worth having than the life of Englarid a France. The 'modern world stands higher because it is further on the road to the goal . . . and it is further on the road beoause its thought has advanced a clear stage in the control of the conditions of life and in the conception of its own aim and 6nd."</p>
        <p>And it will be admitted that one of the chid pieces of evidence that is so "will be organisa- tion of practical machinery so as to render each successive chance of war abortive."</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Gordon Burgess proposed a hearty vote Of thanks to the lecturer for his discourse, and Mr. H. Sherburn seconded. The motion was accordingly carried with acclamation, and the Chairman added his tribute to the lecturer's interest in and enthusiasm for the tociety.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>WELSH DEACON WEDDED.</head>
        <p>WELSH DEACON WEDDED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Evan Hughes Brings Home His Bride From Llanidloes.</p>
        <p>The, wedding of Mr. Evan E. Hughes, of Crosfield-street, 'Warrington, and Red Wharf Bay, Anglesey, and Miss Elizabeth Jones, youngest daughter of Mrs. Jones, of Bethel- street Temperance House, Llanidloes, was sol- emnised on January 14th at the Welsh Calvin- istic Methodist Chapel, Llanidloes. A very large concourse of relatives and frientg—wit- nessed the ceremony, which was impressively conducted by the Revs. R. Parry-Jones (of War- rington) and J. T. Davies (of Llanidloes). The bride, who was beautifully attired, was given away by her brother-in-law, Mr. Dewi Jones. The bridesmaids were the Misses Lily Hughes (of Orrell Park, Liverpool), and Ethel Jones (of South Wales). 31r. H. 0. Hughes (of Aigburth, Liverpool, brother-in-law of the bridegroom) discharged the duties of best man, and Mr. W. Mills presided di the organ.—Afterwards a re- ception was helirat the Town Hall Restaurant, Llanidloes, which had been fittingly decorated. for the occasion. There was a large number of guests.—Later in the day Mr. and Mrs. Hughes left for the coast, where the honeymoon was spent. Numerous valuable presents were re- ceived.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>GOOD WISHES FROM, WARRINGTON FFIENDS.</head>
        <p>GOOD WISHES FROM, WARRINGTON FFIENDS.</p>
        <p>A second reception was held for Warrington friends on Thursday evening of last week, at Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge-street. Mrs. Hughes wore her wedding dress, which was of cream satin, with pearl trimming, and decorated. with orange blossoms and Brussels net.—After the goodly number present had partaken of tea, an excellent entertainment was provided. Mr. W. T. Williams occupied the chair. A pleasant sur- prise for the host and hostess was the presen- tation to them—on behalf of the members of the Crosfield-street Welsh Chapel—of a hand- some silver tea and coffee service. The presen- tation was followed by speeches in appreciation of Mr. Hughes' services to the Welsh Chapel from Messrs. J. Humphries (senior deacon), W. T. Williams (deacon), H. 0. Hughes and J. Griffiths', and the Rev. R. Parry-Jones. The recipients suitably acknowledged the splendid gift. . . _</p>
        <p>The artistes' who contributed -to the pro- gramme were: Mesdames H. 0. Hughes and Sanders, and Messrs. W. Hughes, E. T. Jones, and R. J. Williams. The Rev. Parry-Jones pro- posed, and Mr. A. Woodward seconded, a hearty vote of thanks to the host and hostess, the chair- man and the artistes. This was 'unanimously carried, and the enjoyable evening concluded with the singing of "0, Land of My Fathers."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>EIGHTEEN YEARS A DEACON.</head>
        <p>EIGHTEEN YEARS A DEACON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Evan E. -Hughes came to Warrington from Anglesey about 26 years ago. Since then he has been a devoted member of the Welsh Chapel in the street in which he lives. In frils cause he has rendered valuable service.. Con- scientiously, he has in turn fulfilled the offices of general secretary, Sunday school teacher, superintendent and deacon. This last-named position he has had the honour of occupying for 18 years. "Ile is always known," the Rev. R. Parry-Jones . told "The Examiner" represen- tative, "to have at heart the wlifare of his church. He has done noble work for it. But his labours in other circles also have been greatly appreciated."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>1911 AND 1913.</head>
        <p>1911 AND 1913.</p>
        <p>A RUNCORN COMPARISON.</p>
        <p>Two statements made by the same Runcorn resident are given here. One was made three years before the other. Compare the two, and then ask yourself—" Could better proof be?" On January 10th, 1911, Mr. W. Bell, of 12, Ivy-street, Norman-road, Runcorn, said: "At various times I have suffered with severe pains in my back and other symptoms of kidney dis- orders. The attacks generally came on after catching cold. The pains were like lumbago. "I have taken Doan's Backache Kidney Pills for these complaints, and I am glad to say that I have derived splendid benefit from them. They quickly and effectively drove away the pains and strengthened my kidneys. "I can recommend strongtr Doan's Pills to all who may be troubled with their kidneys, for I have prved the great value of /the Pills by experience. (Signed) W. Bell." On November 27th, 1913-NEARLY THREE YEARS LATER—Mr. Bell said: "I am keeping very well, and heartily confirm all I said for Doan's Pills in my testimonial. I have frequently advised other sufferers to try Doan's Pills." Price 2/9 a box, 6 boxes 13/9; of all dealers, or from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Don't ask for back- ache or kidney pills—ask DISTINCTLY for Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mr. Bell hod. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>PROMINENT BANKER AND THE STUDY</head>
        <p>PROMINENT BANKER AND THE STUDY</p>
        <p>OF SHORTHAND.</p>
        <p>Mr. •Thomas Farrow, the chairman and founder of the "People's Bank," who has done so much to assist the small trader, has written under the non-de-plume of Geoffrey Swift, a most excellent work, entitled "Shorthand in Twelve Lessons."</p>
        <p>Mr. Farrow has always taken keen interest in shorthand and pluenetic writing. An expert in it himself, he set out to devise a system which would do away with a large amount of the tedious study which pupils have been accus- tomed to experience. The result is that he guaranteed success in twelve weeks to any student of ordinary intelligence, who would devote a little time each day in diligent study. It, is a well-known fact that a trade boom such as never before known in this country will immediately follow the termination of this hor- rible war, and the young people of to-day should not lose this golden opportunity of fitting themselves to fill the posts which will inevitably be open to the competent. This excellent _work can now be obtained from the publisher, Mr. E. B. Smith, 35, Dock- head, Tooley-street, -London, S.E., for is. hi., post free. Further particulars will be found in our advertisement columns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>FE A_RNHEAD.</head>
        <p>FE A_RNHEAD.</p>
        <p>Funeral of Victim of Saw Mill Accident.— The funeral took, place at Warrington ceme- tery on Saturday of the late Mr. Herbert Hill, of 30, Brookland-street, Warrington, who, as was reported in oir last issue, met with a shockfng death whilst 'following his employ- ment as a sawyel. at Messrs. Ashworth and Sons' works in Trafford Park. The deceased, who was very highly respected, was followed to his last resting-place by numerous old friends and workmen, and his employers sent a beauti- ful artificial wreath. • The Rev. H. Williamson was the officiating minister. Deceased, who was well-known in the Fearnhead . district, leaves a wife, mother, and three sisters to mourn their loss.</p>
        <p>ACHILD IS SURE TO BE FRETFUL if his stomach is out of order. For this trouble there is no _medicine so useful as Steedman's (EE) Powders. Full directions in every packet. Booklets free on application to J. STEEDMAN (Si Co., 272, Walworth Road, London. S.E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>– ...MS, I ~._ . • _, . .0,- ff . , , ..., .0. DIST- ICT PIE .-,',. _-,,: 1. :,„.., • ..,._ ~. ,_____ . ~............, . _._ __. ..... ..._ __</head>
        <p>- ...MS, I ~._ . • _, . .0,- ff . , , ..., .0. DIST- ICT PIE .-,',. _-,,: 1. :,„.., • ..,._ ~. ,_____ . ~............, . _._ __. ..... ..._ __</p>
        <p>LYIIIM.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wn.. Mort's Address.—Our repLrt of the address given on Sunday afternoon at the Wycliffe P.S.A., Warrington, by Mr. William Mort, of Lymm, appears on another page.</p>
        <p>Methodist Tea Party. —The Cherry-lane Schoolroom presented a very happy scene on Wednesday evening, for then the infants of the Sunday School held their tea party. A large number were present, and after tea Mr. Richard Hughes presided over the presentation of prizes.</p>
        <p>Scoutmasters Confer. —At the home of Scoutmaster Nash, of the Ileatley Troop, the six scoutmasters of Lymm Sand district met on Wednesday evening, and discussed the general work and plans of the Boy Scout movements in the vicinity.</p>
        <p>"The Spy" in Lymm !—The Heatley Troop of Boy Scouts intend to give a concert in the Lymin • Drill Hall on Saturday, April 10th. The principal attraction Will be a sketch entitled "The Spy," and the object of the con- cert will be decided later.</p>
        <p>Soldiers' Holiday Ended.—,We understand that on Sunday evening the second (and last) batch of Lymm "Tommies" left the village and returned to their training centre in the eastern counties. They very much enjoyed their five days' holiday. Captain Fletcher, of " Wormald Green," was in charge.</p>
        <p>From Hartlepool to Lymm.—ln the shop- window of Mr. W. E. Hillier, outfitter, is dis- played a large Piece of spent German shrapnel. To it is affixed the following notice: . German Kultur. •</p>
        <p>This piece of. German shell was found in West Hartlepool, after the bombardment, by a Lymm youth (William Twiss). It is part of a 6.7 German 'shell which wrecked three houses, killing two people, 'and injur- ing several others at Rugby Terrace, West Hartlepool.</p>
        <p>How Wounded Spend Their Time.—The work at Brookfield, the Lymm Red Cross Hospital, is going on as well as ever. Eighteen imunded warriors—an increase of three over any pre- vious number—are now recuperating. The cases have been serious ones of frostbite and shrapnel wounds, whilst one was "a bad crush." These men will not leave the hospital for at least a week yet. In the meantime they are very happy—" just like a large family." Frequently, when the weather is fine, they are taken out for enjoyable spins in motor-cars. Indoors they read, play games, indulge in music (one of the patients is an accomplished violinist, and there is also a piano), and—doze No operations have been performed at Brook- field during the last month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>. HOME DEFENCE CORPS.</head>
        <p>. HOME DEFENCE CORPS.</p>
        <p>XX ill it Affiliate with Volunteer</p>
        <p>Training Corps?</p>
        <p>A most important meeting of the Lymm Home Defence Corps was held in the village earlier in the week, for the purpose of considering the proposed affiliation of that corps with the Cen- tral Association Volunteer Training Corps. The rules 'of this latter corps are: (1) To en- courage recruits for the Regular and Territorial Army. (2) To encourage men not of age for service in the ranks, or otherwise disqualified for service, to drill and learn the elements of musketry in their spare time. (3) To co- ordinate all existing organisations with similar objects and to promote uniformity in their rules and regulations. After considerable discussion, the meeting was adjourned for a week. The important decision is awaited with widespread interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>FOOTBALLERS DINE:</head>
        <p>FOOTBALLERS DINE:</p>
        <p>Wednesday and Saturday Clubs 'Celebrate Christmas Match.</p>
        <p>very enjoyable time was -ra,s- -sed at the Millstone Hotel on Wednesday evening, when the members of the Lymm Wednesday and Sat- urday Football Clubs held a hotpot supper to celebrate the memorable match played on Christmas Day in aid of the Red Cross Hospi- tal. Mrs. Creasey catered excellently. Mr. Arthur Sutton presided over a goodly company, and gave a short and suitable address.</p>
        <p>Afterwards a very enjoyable entertainment waw provided by Messrs. F. Owen, E. Creasey, S. Moeton, W. Moston and Bowen, who gave solos. Mr. S. Moston also earned hearty ap- plause with a patriotic recitation, and Mr. Garvey caused great merriment with his read- ing, "Don't Count Your Chickens.!" _ _</p>
        <p>Before the assembly broke up, Mr. Reed pro- posed a hearty vote of thanks to the artistes and caterers. The Chairman seconding, this was carried with acclamation.</p>
        <p>Most of those who attended, it should be ex- plained, have been unable to recruit for various reasons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>TRADESMEN COMPLAIN</head>
        <p>TRADESMEN COMPLAIN</p>
        <p>That Local Public Bodies are Not Trading with Them Enough.</p>
        <p>FLAT CONTRADICTIONS.</p>
        <p>An important resolution was passed at the quarterly meeting of the Lymm Tradesmen's Association—held in Hirst's Pavilion on Tues- day evening—with reference to "the present dearth of trade." The trouble arose from the allegation that local public money is not being spent with the Lymm tradesmen to the extent which it should be.</p>
        <p>The President, Mr. David Daniels, occupied the chair, and a good attendance included the following past officials: Messrs. H. S. Saville, jun., F. Druce, E. Hirst, J. Rogerson, W. All- man and H. E. Odber. Mrs. A. B. Richard- son (hon. treasurer) and W. E. Hillier (hon. secretary) were also present. Mr. W. E. Hillier called the attention of the assembjy to "the lack of opportunity extended to the .traders of Lymm by the several local public bodies spending public money." Mr. H. E. Odber supported the Sec'retary's views, and after a long discussion, Mr. H. S. Saville, jun., proposed that a report of these remarks be given to the public, asking them, in face of the present dearth of trade, to Support the local traders as far as lies in their power.</p>
        <p>This resolution was seconded, and carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>LOCAL OPINIONS.</p>
        <p>Yesterday "The Examiner" representative interviewed some -members of the local public bodies on the matter, having first been assured that the sentiments expressed at the trades- men's meeting might safely be taken as coincid- ing with the views of every Lymm tradesmen. Mr. W. Mull'ird, clerk to the Urban District Council, howeN or, informed our representative, without hesitation, that the allegation could in no circumstances be applied to the Council. In every case, so far as the Council were concerned, the Lymm traders were given every opportunity of tendering for supplies and labour; indeed, it is, said Mr. Mallard, the Council's custom to circularise the local tradesmen, as they des_irer.</p>
        <p>very much to look after the interests of the Lymm business men. Mr. T. F. W. Dixon, treasurer of the War Refugees' Funds, said that as far as his com- mittee were concerned, the remarks made at the tradesmen's meeting didsnot apply in the lest. Since the arrangements had been got into work- ing order, everything purchased for "Beech- wood " and its occupants rad 'been obtained through the local shops, in accordance with the committee's arrangements to patronifach in turn.</p>
        <p>With this statement the secretary to the War Refugees' Committee concurred. Mr. Dixon is also treasurer of the National Relief Funds for the Lymm district, and he in- formed us that the tradesmen's remarks cer- tainly could • not refer to the work of the National Relief Fund Committee either. In the few cases in which relief' had so far been granted, all the money expended had gone into the hands of Lymm tradesmen.</p>
        <p>With regard to the Red Cross Hospital com- mittee, our representative was informed by a member of the executive that no money which could possibly be spent in LYmm has been spent elsewhere. 4.•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>MOTOR WRECKED.</head>
        <p>MOTOR WRECKED.</p>
        <p>Three Lymm Gentlemen Receive Severe Injuries.</p>
        <p>A serious accident befel a party of Lynim gentlemen whilst returning by motor from Southport on Sunday. The car was that of Mr. Ti. S. Saville. It was driven by his son, 'Mr. H. S. Saville, jun., and also contained that ovntleman's father, together with Mr. Rowland 'iVilkes, of Statham; Mr. W. G. Jeeves, of Red- dish, Lymm ; and Mr. Saville's two sons. This party had been to pay a visit to Dr. Jeeves, of Southport, who is engaged on Territorial duties. As the car was turning round a rather dan- gerous corner on the wad at Scarbrick, about three miles from Ormgkirk, it skidded on the footpath and overturned. A complete wreck, it was promptly lifted by two police officers and two soldiers, and Mr.. Wilkes, who had been pinned down under it, was thus liberated. He was found to have sustained a compound frac- ture of the upper part of the left arm, there was a cut near his left eye, and he was suffering from shock. He was conveyed at once to the Manchester Infirmary, where he is now making nice progress. Mr: Jeeves' collar bone was frac- tured, and his head and face damaged. Mr. Saville, sen., had his left elbow cut, but other- wise escaped with a severe shaking, as did also the -other two gentlemen. \</p>
        <p>All the injured ones are making satisfactory progress. Curiously enough, another Lymm gentleman, Mr. George Bradbury, passed the scene of the accident only a few minutes after its occur- rence. He never thought its injured occupants belonged to his own village!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>GRA PPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRA PPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Pretty 'Wedding.—The Parish Church on Saturday was the scene of a pretty wedding, th-e-- contracting parties being Miss Mabel Starkey, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Jodeph Starkey, of Rossglen, Sunny Side Villas, Grappenhall, and Mr. John Hitchinson, son of Mr. and Mrs. Richard Hitchinson, of Rose Bank, Moulton, near Northwich. The Rector (the Rev. H. V. Pigot) officiated, and the bride, who was given away by her father, vas attired in a dark brown costume, and carried a shower bouquet.</p>
        <p>of white lilies. The bridesmaids were Miss Selina 'Starkey (sister of the bride) and Miss Ada Thomas (cousin of the bride), whilst Mr.</p>
        <p>Sam Clarke acted as best man. After the cere- mony a reception was held in the Parish Room, Latchford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>GREAT SAN KEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SAN KEY.</p>
        <p>Well-deserved Promotion.—Sergeant T. Smith, who has been in charge of this district for the last eight years, it is a pleasure to announce, has been promoted to the rank of inspector. His tuture sphere of • action- will be at Hindley, in the Wigan district, where he will not be alto- gether a stranger, for he served as a constable in and around Wigan for some considerable time prior to settling down at Sankey. During his term of office varying circumstances have proved his individuality, which has been acknowledged by the presentation of the Merit Badge. His many well-wishers regret his re- moval keenly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—The monthly devotional meet- ing of the Wesley Guild was held on Monday evening, when a very interesting address was given by the Rev. A. J. Morton, the subject being "Parables of Wealth." Mrs. J. Bennett gave a sweet rendering of "Calvary," and Miss E. Bennett officiated at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>HATTON.</head>
        <p>HATTON.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. Walter Dooley.—Much sym- pathy is felt with the wife and two young chil- dren of the late Mr. Walter Dooley, who passed away at a comparatively early age a few days i ago. Mr. Dooley was well known n. the village as the secretary of the Parish Room and Insti- tute. A' printer by trade, Mr. Dooley served his apprenticeship in a Warrington newspaper office, and latterly had been employed in the printing department of Messrs. CroAeld's Soapery.</p>
      </div>
