      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>• • • 4.;Y 412•.„,, 4 , „ , . – . ‘'• "40' t 4 " – ,</head>
        <p>• • • 4.;Y 412•.„,, 4 , „ , . - . ‘'• "40' t 4 " - ,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S FINE WIN.</p>
        <p>Oldham Outclassed Ery epportuaist Tactics.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>HOWIE PACK IN GRUM FORM.</head>
        <p>HOWIE PACK IN GRUM FORM.</p>
        <p>Notes by "Centre."</p>
        <p>Warrington we're in their happiest vein on Baturday; their pack in particular being on the top of their form, and at time clever almost to the point of brilliance. Oldham were the visit- ors at Wildersppol, and they Were sent back to Spindleton pointless themselves, and with an adverse balance to ruminate dyer. Warring- ton's win was no flash in the pan sort of affair, but was secured on merits, for the players' ran their visitors practically ,to a standstill, and Were worth all the points they scored, and Thiore. Their play made glad the hearts of their supporters, and must have been in thQ nature of a rude surprise to the Cotton .Towners, with memories of the last meeting comparatively fresh in their minds. With a continuance of the form they displayed on Saturday, Warring- ton should do much in the second half of the season to compensate for the disappointments a the first, and may--et be seen occupying a position much higher up the League table. The way they went to work against their doughty opponents would seem to imply that the tougher the proposition with which they are faced the more ready are they to rise to the.occasion, and that they are yet capable of great things. Sat- urday's result, say the critics, was a surprise. So it was, t'O.a certain extent, and local devotees of the Rugby game are hoping for a series of similar pleasant surprises.</p>
        <p>take, and certainly not one of real importance, save when it came to goal-kicking.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S SUPERIOR</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S SUPERIOR</p>
        <p>ADAPTABILLTY.</p>
        <p>Warrington's win was in a measure due to their superiorfty in the matter of adaptability. The ground, though in a considerably better condition than it has been of late, was in a state far removed from the ideal, and after it had been played upon for some little time be- came treacherous in the extreme. Its slipper- iness seemed to upset Oldham's calculations, and to put them off their usual overbearing tactics, whilst the home team sedulously applied themselves to adapting their play to the awk- ward conditions, countering Oldham's ortho- dox methods with methods of opportunist-un- orthodoxy, which told all the time and enabled them to do most of the attacking from begin- ning to end. Their forwards showed a praise- Worthy amount of originality and initiative, and if at times they did appear to miss chances through want of cohesion, the ends !notified the Means, and would suggest that under the ter- restial conditions, the tactics they adopted were quite the right ones. There can be no doubt that the bulk of the credit for the win must be ascribed to the front-rankers, though the suc- cessful marking ef Tranter and Brookes was Ilso an important contributing factor. The 'erwards used their feet splendidly on the slip- Pery turf, and their tackling was safe and was good to watch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>THOROUGHLY DESERVED WIN.</head>
        <p>A THOROUGHLY DESERVED WIN.</p>
        <p>All the more credit attaches to Warrington for their win, from the fact that they were Without the services of Dainteth—always a most handy man, and one who cannot be lightly spared. His place was taken by Laws, who, whilst not completely filling the gap, gave nevertheless, a very commendable display. Before the interval was called it was obvious that Oldham were a beaten team, and the only point at issue in the second, moiety was as to the size of the defeat, and on the whole, one watching the turn of play, could not but con- clude that they must, in a theasure, think them- selves somewhat fortunate that the adverse balance totted up against them was net a larger one than proved to be the case. The Oldham forwards were unusually lacking in enterprise and there was an uncertainty about the whole of their back division which War- rington were not slow to perceive, and to turn to good account, calling in much sound foot- vork to their aid. Better in front, Warrington Were also better behina, the three-quarters Owing soundly, whilst behind them Jolley was a veritable pillar of reliability, working with rare judgment and hardly ever making a mis-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>SKELHORNE'S BRILLIANT RE-APPEAR-</head>
        <p>SKELHORNE'S BRILLIANT RE-APPEAR-</p>
        <p>ANCE.</p>
        <p>A feature of the- game was the re-appearance of Sergt. Skelhorne, who was at home with many fellow Territorials, in the Warrington forward line, and he came in for a rousing,re- ception at the hands and lungs of the crowd, when, looking in the pink of condition, he trotted out from the dressing-room to take up his place in the front rank. The gallant Ser- geant had - been spending a few days on fur- lough, and the club committee were pleaSed and proud once again to be able to avail themselves of his valuable services—for valuable they have been in the past and valuable were they also on Saturday. He proved to ffave lost :none of his old turn of speed, and he was as tricky as ever. The second try of the :match was due to him; and aS'he crossed the line, after out-manceuv- ring Wood, and trickingl several • other' oppo- nents, a great shout went up that set the welkin a-ringing, though it ended On a dolorous note as it was seen that Brookes made a mess of the goal kick. The first try came a few minutes earlier as the result of a fine combined run by the Warrington three-quarters, Tranter get- ting over amid great excitement. Jolley disap- pointed the crowd by failing with the goal kick. Fairly early on Oldham were placed at something of a disadvantage through— Hall being injured. The visitors had to send back one of their forwards and thus their frontal attack was weakened.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>DASHING FORWARD WORK</head>
        <p>DASHING FORWARD WORK</p>
        <p>• Warrington were six points to the good at the cross-over and they showed every sign of having the game well in hand, and opened the second moiety in most determined fashion, early on causing the Oldham defenders a great deal or trouble and no little anxiety. The loose game was freely adopted, and it was in this class of play that the home forwards particularly shone. Skelhorne led many an onslaught in dashing style, his impetuosity proving infectious, with the result that the Warrington men gave the crowd a taste—and they liked the taste exceed- ingly—of the class of play at which, of old, they used to excel. Still, Wood, the Oldham back, kept his head, and more than once plaudits were wrung from the crowd by the brilliant manner in which he succeeded in taking the sting out of seemingly Irresistible rushes. Had the Spindle Towners had a less reliable man behind them their defeat woutd have been on a scale approaching the overwhelming. But one man, however doughty his feats, cannot avert disaster, and Wood sadly lacked the aid' that he had a right to expect from his three- quarters, whose display was only moderate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>HOW THE PLAYERS PLAYED.</head>
        <p>HOW THE PLAYERS PLAYED.</p>
        <p>This second half only yielded one try, and that fell to Warrington, per Bradshaw. It Tfas a particularly fine try, and Jolley inaugurated the movement which led up to it. The War- rington back got the ball near his ,line, and dodged his way half up the field, and then passed to Brookes, who transferred to Brad- shaw, just when that player was nicely placed. Bradshaw made full use of the opportunity provided him, and nipped across near the corner, Brookes setting the imprimatur of com- plete success on the effort by adding the goal points with a hefty and well-judged kick. When the long whistle sounded Oldham had failed to trouble the man :at the score-board, whilst Warrington's figures read 1 goal 3 tries (11 points).</p>
        <p>- Seldom have Warrington better deserved to win. It was the just reward of hard work in all departments. The forwards were full of life, taking their cue from Skelhorne, who was übiquitous and daring, and seemed to instil courage and spirit into all his coadjutors, who gave him loyal and ungrudging support. Tranter and Brookes were the pick of the home three-quarters, the Warrington captain's hust- ling tactics proving most effective. Bradshaw was resourceful on occasions, and Baker was responsible for more than one smart tackle. Young was exceedingly reliable, and, as we have said, Laws gave him valuable assistance. Old- ham's three-quarter line worked hard, but the home team saw to it that they never had Enna latitude. The visitors' forwards were fre- quently completely at sea, quite failing to get the hang of Warrington's game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>ANGLING..</head>
        <p>ANGLING..</p>
        <p>Warrington Working Men's</p>
        <p>Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>N.A. CENTRE.</head>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>For the first time this year one or two mem- bers paid a visit to the Gowy. This stream is now getting quite famous, if not in the abund- ance of takes; it is now coming into "all its glory or deplorable state. We will leave these matters to our delegate while We go along our usual course, as we are sure he will convince the angler locally that the Gowy does not de- serve the criticism it has received. Mr. F. Jolley took a roach of 14oz. on Sunday, and the report he brought back was, although the water was Of a muddier nature than is good for our local rods, it was fishable at all parts. The banks were in a better condition than he ex- pected to find them. The attention of members is drawn to the fact that the first club night is on Monday next, and those Who are going out must take out their new travelling cards, and put themselves straight on the books. Last year's cards are of no use.</p>
        <p>The special prizes won for 1914 —§Ta§on amounted to £6 is., instead of- £5 Bs. as an- nounced previously. Any member who did not, put in appeaeance_ at the annual meeting for his special can have it at any time by applying to the Treasurer, Arr. Brookes, Gladstone Arms. A general meeting will be held on Thursday to continue business left over from the annual meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE.</p>
        <p>GT. SANKEY LOSE A POINT AT HOME. Gt. Sankey had a fright at home on Saturday in -their match against Runcorn Victorians, The game was very strenuously contested throughout, and at the call of time the honours were equal, each side annexing a couple of goals. This is the first point the Gt. Sankey team have yielded on their own ground. for three seasons. The home team were at full strength, and the visitors must be congratu- lated on their display. The spectators were keenly excited as the game proceeded, and each side scored their goals. The interval arrived with the score two goals •each. The second portion was full of excitement, and many keen tussles were witnessed, but no further goals were registered, and at the call of time the result was : Gt. Sankey, 2 goals; Runcorn Vic- torians, 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON SECONDARY SCHOOL 2nd v. LYMM GRAMMAR SCHOOL 2nd.</p>
        <p>Played at Padgate. Warrington opened be- fore a strong wind, Lymm scoring first. A. George soon equalised for Warrington. Half- time arrived with the score 2-2. Resuming, with the wind, the home team dashed away, and kept the visitors at bay for the rest of the game. Result: Warrington 14 goals; Lymm, 2.</p>
        <p>The scorers for the home -team- were: A. George (3), R. George (2), Holden (3), Whit- field (2), R. Jones, D. Jones, Knowles, and Ken- wright (1).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>STARKEY CUP COMPETITION:</p>
        <p>Newton Cons. v. Earlestown Mission; referee, R. Brandreth. St. Barnabas v. Longford United; M. J. Kite. Fairfield United v. Grappenhall; J. Smith. Hollins Green v. Warrington Central; B. Howell.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Elmwood Cons. v. Earlestown</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>ELMWOOD AVENUE v. EARLESTOWN Y.M.C.A.</p>
        <p>On the Avenue ground; kick off 3 o'clock. Elmwood: A. Holt; Jos. Phcenix, Jas. Phee- nix; A. N. Other, W. C. Ashton, A. Lamb; Jos. Smith, T. Wells, E. Kendrick, W. Hall, and W. Phcenix. Reserves: N. Ellam and E. Derbyshire.</p>
        <p>BIG DEMAND Fon CLOCKS.</p>
        <p>I am informed, writes our Association corm- spondent, that quite recerif,y one of the Hollins Green players joined the liciiedl.3ts' Society, and as some slight recognilioo of his services to the club its supporters n3ade him a presen- tation of a 400-day clock. Since that occasion an. epidemic appears to haw caught on and several more of the lads are following suit. If they have all to be presented with a 400-day timepiece, the Hollins Green Club will have to find some strong, healthy supporters to the funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>CANAL TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>CANAL TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>Woman Falls in and Dies from</p>
        <p>Shock.</p>
        <p>An inquiry into the death of Ann Harris, 60, a canal boat woman, was held by Mr. S. Brig- house at the Sloop Inn, Sankey, on, Monday afternoon.</p>
        <p>The evidence showed that the deceased had lived with Samuel Harrison, a canal boatman, for the last seven or eight years. Harrison did not know her .name or address, and only that she belonged to Wigan. On Saturday; January 30, she was living on the canal manure boat . (Hooper, of Liverpool), which was moored in the St. Helens and Sankey Canal at Sankey Bridges. At 9 p.m. on that date the deceased was climbing into the boat, when she fell into the canal. Harrison saw her fall and jumped into the water and got hold of her. He then shouted for help. Joseph Taylor, of Hood-lane, Sankey, pulled them both out. Deceased was taken to the house of Alice Plumb, _near by, and supplied with dry clothing. Soon she ap- peared to be none the worse for her immersion. At 11 p.m.. the same night she returned to the boat and went to bed in a bunk in the cabin. Harrison occupied a bunk beneath hers. Soon after going to bed the deceased said she was cold and Harrison put inire coal' on the fire, and then went to sleep. He awoke at 2 a.m. and spoke to the woman, but got no answer. He examined her and found she was dead. The jury found that the deceased lost her life through shock following immersion. The Coroner congratulated the witnesses on getting the man and woman out of the water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>BOVRIL, LIMITED.</head>
        <p>BOVRIL, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>Largely Increased Profits in the War Year. The report of -Bovril, Limited, for the year ended 31st December last shows net profits amounting to £137,584 ss. 11d. Including the balance brought forward, the total available for appropriation is £143,241 9s. 11d. The debenture-interest for the year and the interim dividends on the preference and ordinary shares absorbed £55,000. After the payment of the 51 per cent. dividend for the second half of the year on the preference shares and 9 per cent. for the'second half-year on the ordinary shares (making 7 per cent. for the year) the directors recommend a distribution of 6d. per share free of income tax on the deferred shares, and to place to reserve £17.500, bringing that account up to ,t,267,500. The report' states that the sales for the year, apart from Government orders altogether, exceed all previous •records. Notwithstanding the-high cost of raw materials and a reduction of £lO,OOO in the dividend re- ceived on the ordinary shares of Estates Con- trol, Limited, which for the period under review was at the rate of 1,000 per cent., as against 3,000 per cent. for the preceding twelve months, the net profit shows a satisfactory improve- ment at £137,584 ss. lid., as compared with .2119,813 s. Cd. for, the previou:s year.</p>
      </div>
