      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>PilinDlirt!GTOLti REVIVE.</head>
        <p>PilinDlirt!GTOLti REVIVE.</p>
        <p>On Saturday there were several Very interegt- ing contests in connection with our local League, and the conditions overhead were ideal, but in some instances the grounds were very heavy going for the players owing 'to a top thaw.</p>
        <p>Notes by "Linesman."</p>
        <p>in a melee in front of the home goal Rodgers opened the score for the Mission. A penalty kick to the visitors failed to produce anything, and, the half-time score was: Mission, 1 goal; White Star, 0. The home team had the best of the exchanges on resuming, and set up a long bombardment of the visitors' goal. At a sharp angle Stubbs 'shot into the goalkeeper's hands. From the clearance the same player secured, and from his centre Gifford made the scores. -equal. Encouraged by this success the homesters played more strenuously to gain the lead, and although the visitors made desperate efforts, the home half-baCks returned the ball to the visitors' goal area. A melee in their goal fol- lowed, ;.und Maddock shooting in, the referee decided that the ball had crossed the line. The visitors disputed the decision, and left the field a: few :minutes before the expiry of time, the scores at that time being: Orford White Star, 2 goals; Earlestown Mission, 1; White Star: Miller; Evans, Pilling; Gifford, Denny, Caldwell; Maddock, Gregory, Meachin, llankinson, and Stubbs. Mission Wallas; Winders, A. Eaton; :Rodgers, F. Eaton, Theobald; W. Wallas, Ald- ridge, Rothwell, SlterloCk, and Plumpton. Referee, M. T.' Kite; \</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>CENTRAL AT HOLLINS GREEN</head>
        <p>CENTRAL AT HOLLINS GREEN</p>
        <p>The Warrington . Central paid their first visit to Hollins Green in .League fixtures, and the game could be considered as a .trial for the more strenuous game ih the Starkey Cup 'com- petition, in which the teams are in opposition to each other again to-day on the same ground. The . Greenites were handicapped by the absence of four, of their regular players, whilst the Central team has improved almost beyond recognition. The. game. ended in favour of the Central by three goals to none, and by the dis- play shown they were fully entitled to the points obtained. At the outset,of the game the home backs defended well, but the 'visitors adapted themselves better to the conditions of the 'g,round. In the second portion it was fully expected the home team would overhaul their opponents, but the forwards did not give many glimpses of combination and the visitors' goal was rarely in danger. Neither did the home defence play up to their usual form, and the backs were many times outpaced by Farrell and Mason. :rhe result will certainly give the Central team more confidence for the Cup ties.</p>
        <p>Early in the first half an attack by the home forwards was driven back by the visitors' backs. Mellor and Gilbert, the centre-halves of their, 'respective teams, were responsible for good play in midfield. Good combination by the visitors led to an attack on the home goal, and Farrell eluding the home- backs beat Perrins with a shot near to the upright after ten minutes' play. The homesters returned more vigorously to the attack, and good shots were sent in by W. Perrin and Atkinson, but W. Ashcroft, in the visitors' goal, brought off some excellent saves, and the attack was eventually staved off, much to the delight of the visitors' sup- porters. Several raids on the home goal were unsuccessful owing to good defensive play by the home backs, and a long shot by Farrell was eaSilrsaved by Perrin. The home lot made a strong effort to get on equal terms and gained a couple of corner kicks, but failed to improve upon them. Half-time: Central, 1 goal; Hollins Green, 0. Farrell and Mason, on the visitors' left, owing to their fine turn of speed, were a source of trouble to the home defence, who were often hard, pressed to clear their lines. A free kick to the visitors just outside the penalty area resulted in their second goal being scored off one of the home .backs. The home team tried hard, but their forwards did not combine well enough, to become dangerous, and W. Perrin on several occasions placed wide of the upright. Several efforts of the visitors were repulsed in midfield, where. Mellor .was. playing.. a. good game, although at times he was inclined to be very selfish. - The visitors at length broke through, and:following an' unproductive.corner kick Hands scored the third goal. Atkinson in a good positiOn, dallied too long and Glover cleared, and at the opposite • end Farrell beat the backs, and when closing' in upon the goal overran the ball and lose a very easy chance of increasing the lead. Result:. Central, 3 goals; Hollins Green, 0. Hollins Green: A. Perrin; Clarke, Harrison; Buckley, Mellor, Atherton; Wright, Cleworth, Billington, Atkinson, and W. Perrin.</p>
        <p>_ Central: W. Ashcroft; R. Ashcroft, Glover; Brown, Gilbert, Williams; E. Mason, Hands, Sankey, Farrell, and T. Mason. Referee, J. Smith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>LONGFORD UNITED EVEN WITH ST.</head>
        <p>LONGFORD UNITED EVEN WITH ST.</p>
        <p>BARNABAS.</p>
        <p>Longford United and St. Barnabas met on the Horse and Jockey ground in their return fix- ture, and, as the first game resulted in a win for the Saints, it was only to be expected that the United would strive hard to reverse the previous result. The visitors were minus the useful services of Darlington, whilst the United were, expected to improve their attack by the inclusion of Eccleston, late of Bank Quay Recreation. From the commencemenibthe game was keenly contested, and it. was only a few minutes before the interval that Sherratt con- verted a centre from Eccleston. In the second portion the visitors led off with a strong attack, and it was only the splendid display of Sturgess in the home goal that prevented the visitors equalising, and the United just managed to gain the verdict by the only goal scored. To- day these teams are again in opposition for the Starkey Cup tie, this time on the Lovely- lane ground if the latter permits, and there is no doubt as to the keenness of the contest. It should prove to be a game worth watching. After the opening exchanges Challinor sent the ball out to Shaw on the extreme right, and from his centre Woods just shaved the upright. The homesters made a raid into their oppo- nents' quarters, where a timely clearance by Long foiled their efforts to lower the visitors' citadel. For awhile the defence on both sides proved too good fOr the attack. From a goal kick ' Smart, the United's centre-half, gained posses- sion, and after, beating three opponents passed out to Eccleston, and from his cehtre Sherratt- .scored with a good-, shot, Davenport beink well beaten. Half-time: Longford, 1 goal; St. .Barnabas, 0. The visitors led off in the second half with a strong attack on the home goal, and Sturgess, Holden, and Mason were very hard pressed, the former bringing off some capital saves, whilst it may be said the visitors were "unfortunate, not to get on equal terms. The homesters gradually gained relief, and /made several ad- vances on the Saints' goal, but Long and Jeffer- son were on the alert, and Davenport was not seriously troubled. Good play by Challinor resulted in another fierce attack on the home goal, but Sturgess would not yield his charge, Towards the end the Saints made a final effort to get on equal terms, and the home. goal had a very lucky escape from a dangerous centre by Lemons, the ball curling into the goal mouth, dropping on the crossbar, and bounding, out of play. The result of a strongly contested game was: Longford, 1 goal; St. Barnabas, 0. Longford: Sturgess; Holden, Mason; Fearn- ley, Smart, Smith; Eccleston, Hands, Sherratt, Shelmardine, and Hughes. St. Barnabas: Davenport; Jefferson, E. Long; O'Neil, P. . Challinor Ray; Shaw, Deakin, Woods, Vernon, and Lemons. Referee, J. T. Hadkiss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>PLAYERS LEAVE THE FIELD.</head>
        <p>PLAYERS LEAVE THE FIELD.</p>
        <p>The Orford White• Star team had opponents in the Earlestown Mission for their return fix- ture. There cannot be much doubt that the teams were very evenly matched. • After 'a very strenuous first half the visitors held the lead., In the second portion, playing againq a very strong defence, the home team equalised through Gifford. Both teams played hard for the lead, and the White Star half-back line proved a stumbling block to the Mission for- wards. As the end was approaching the home team doubled their efforts, and Maddock sent a shot into goal, and the referee judged the ball to have been over the line. This decision did not satisfy the Mission, and players left the field of play six minutes before the expiry of time. In taking this course the Mission players showed a very unsportsmanlike action and put themselves into a serious position, as they left the referee with no other option but to report their action to the Liverpool Association. The White Star won the toss, and Bothwell having kicked off made an advance on the visi- tors' goal, but had to retire. The Mission then improved and made .several raids on the home citadel, but Miller proved equal to the calls made upon him. The visitors came again, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>PADDINGTON WIN A MATCH</head>
        <p>PADDINGTON WIN A MATCH</p>
        <p>The returni fixture at Paddington against Elmwood Avenue Congregationalists was the means of restoring the .home players into favour again with their supporters, as this was their first victory since Christmas. During January the players seem to have deteriorated in their display, and they took advantage of the weak opposition against them last week to collect a couple of welcome points. The general run of the play was not much to enthuse about, and a few minutes before the call' of time the referee had to perform the unsatisfactory task of sending a player from each side to the dressing-room. The visitors, defending the goal nearest the road, kicked off, and during the opening por- tion of the game they attacked the home goal very strongly. Smith raced away aloilg the touch line and centred to Kendrick. The latter passed on to Lamb, and he shot across the goal- mouth, and several players missed a good oppor- tunity of scoring. After ten minutes' play the homesters began to assert themselves, and gradually forced their opponents to defend their own lines. The homesters were awarded a free kick just inside the visitors' half, and the ball was placed 'well in front of the goal. A misunderstanding between the visitors' backs allowed Derbyshire to get through and score* for the home team. The visitors were chiefly .on the defensive and rarely got near the home goal, the; interval, score being: Paddington, .1 goal; Elmwood, 0. On restarting the home team made the first advance, which was repulsed by Ashton, and his forwards, receiving the ball, could, not make' headway against the home defence. Chamber- lain returned the leather very sniartly, and then the Paddingtonians gained several fruit- less corner kicks. Following some good clear- ances by the brothers -Phcenix, another 'shot Was sent in, which Holt failed to get clear away, and the ball was rushed through for the second goal. After this reverse the visitors made several attempts to raid the home goal, but their final efforts were very feeble, and the home backs easily cleared. The play was soon again in the vicinity of the visitors' goal, when a stinging shot sent in by Derbyshire hit the upright and glanced through the goal. Result: Paddington, 3 goals; Elmwood Cons., 0. GAME GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall at Knutsford-road made their first acquaintance with the League leaders on Saturday, and although the homesters were unable to put their best eleven in the field a very keen. contest was witnessed.</p>
        <p>The visitors won the toss, and H. Shakeshaft set the ball rolling, passing on to Ford. The latter was dispossessed by Hunter, who fed his forwards with judgment, and the home goal was under attack, and Millward scored after seven minutes' play with a shot from outside the penalty area; Shakeshaft making a feeble attempt to save. From the centre kick the home forwards visited their opponents' area, where, Woodhouse cleared finely from Parr. 01k-int to the effective play of the visitors' half- backs, the liome forwards failed to combine, J. Ford and Shakeshaft getting little support, and Hunter and Lewis repeatedly broke up their efforts to get within shooting range of the home goal. Half-time: Fairfield United, 1 goal; Grappenhall, 0. On restarting the visitors at once became aggressive, and T. Ford and Evans had a busy time until Hankey relieved. Davies, getting well away on the wing, put across a fine centre, but offside spoiled the effort. Then, by good combination by Long and Short, play was transferred to the home end, where Ford finely intercepted and gave H. Shakeshaft an accurate pass. Shakeshaft sent in a splendid shot which hit the crossbar and rebounded into play, Beb- bington finally getting the ball away. Mill- ward had a try, but failed to get past Evans, and then Woodhouse, being well served by Hunter, succeeded in scoring the second goal, Millward . adding a third directly after. Re- suit: Fairfield United, 3 goals, Grappenhall, 0. Grappenhall: S. Shakeshaft; T. Ford, B. Evans; L. H. Hankey, F. Parr, A. Brazendale; F. Davies, J. Ford, H. Shakeshaft, H. Parr, and Hough. Fairfield: Potter; Bebbington, Woodhouse; Gleave, Hunter, Lewis; Long, Short, Millward, Woodhouse, and Cole. Referee, B. Howell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>ALTRINCHANI JUNIOR CUP.</head>
        <p>ALTRINCHANI JUNIOR CUP.</p>
        <p>WEST-TIMPERLEY v..STOCKTON HEATH. In the Altrincham and District Junior Cup competition the Stockton team had to snake the journey to Broadheath minus several of their best players owing to illness and injuries. In these circumstances they were hardly expected to win. Their regular goalkeeper having to assume the duties of leader of the attack, the custodianship of the goal was entrusted to their energetic and hard-working secretary, who per- formed very creditably and made several fine saves. No blame could be attached to him for the shots that scored.</p>
        <p>Good .play by West, Smith, and Rylance, re- sulted in Woodhead having to concede a corner from a shot by Noden. The ball was well placed, and after some smart exchanges Rey- nolds cleared. B; Reynolds and Higson gaining possession made a splendid run' for the honk.- sters, and passed to Comar, who rounded Gos- ling and beat Rushton with a splendid drive. At the opposite end Hooper, the visitors' centre; missed a very easy opening. Half-time: West Timperley, 1 goal; Stockton Heath, 0. The Visitors opened the second• half with a rush, which was repulsed, and Llewellyn trans- ferred play to the visitors' quarters, Rushton saving well. Barlow by a well-judged pass set Smith and Rylance going, but their final efforts were weak: The 'homesters again attacked strongly, and Chesters and Rushton • cleared several times, but were unable to stave off their downfall, Higson scoring. The visitors now made another effort to penetrate the home de- fence, but failed. Gosling gave a penalty kick, which was entrusted to B. Reynolds. Rushton saved, but unfortunately stumbled and lost possession, and Reynold -easily scored. Result: West Timperley, 3 goals; Stockton Heath, 0. West Timperley: Woodhead; Bradbury, Rey- nolds; Higgins, Pownall, Kenworthy; Llewel- lyn, Higson, B. Reynolds, Hulme, and Comar. Stockton Heath: Rushton; Gosling, Chesters; Hamblett, Barlow, West; Pearce, Noden, Ifooper; Smith, and Rylance. Referee, Ur. Shaw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
