      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY. FEBRUARY 6, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. FEBRUARY 6, 1915.</p>
        <p>GUNPOWDER PLOT.</p>
        <p>In "The Examiner" of January 9th we wrote about the first-known English newspaper, "The Mercurie," published in 1588, containing the stirring news of the Armada .and the plan for the invasion of England. To-day we give extracts from a paper called "The Weekely Newes," published in 1606. There may have been other news-sheets issued between these dates, but "The Weekely Newes" is the first we know of since "The Mercurie." Of course there was news to be had before the advent of the newspaper. Professional writers in London gathered the gossip and rumour of the city together with items of general interest from the news of the day and wrote news-letters to the aristocracy in the provinces, or to any- one else who was willing to pay them for their trouble. These gentlemen plied busy quills day by day and found subscribers in large numbers. Three centuries ago, by the coming of the printed news-sheet, they discovered themselves to be members of a rapidly decaying industry. The issue of "The Weekely Newes" from which we have taken the following extracts is Number 19, and contains the record of the "Guy Fawkes" plot, and the execution of " Guy " and his associates. This piece of history is too well known to require introduction or comment. The plot was discovered through a vague anony- mous letter left at the house of Lord Monteagle, which advised him to "think not slightly of this advertisement, but retire yourself into your own country, where you may expect the event in safety. For though there be no appearance of any stir, yet I say they shall receive a ter- rible blow this Parliament." The letter was shown to the King and his Councillors, and led to the searching of the Parliament House, the discovery, and the subsequent execution.</p>
        <p>How a Famous Event was Described in the Newspaper of the Period.</p>
        <p>of until the rest shall give you leave."</p>
        <p>unless it were by the dozen upon their beads— and taking tobacco as if that hanging were no trouble to them; craving mercy neither of God nor the King for their offences, and making their conscience, as it were, as wide of the mind, and to the very Gates of Hell to be the cause of their Hellish courses to make a work meritorious."</p>
        <p>Then follows in detail a somewhat ghastly recital of how the conspirators met their death by hanging, and ends, "Then came Bates," the last of the conspirators, "and when he was hanged the Executioners prepared to Draw and Quarter them; and when this was done the business of the day ended?'</p>
        <p>The Oath of the Conspirators was: "You shall swear by the blessed Trinity, and by the Sacrament you now propose to receive, never to disclose directly or indir- ectly, by word or circumstance, the matter that shall be proposed to you to keep secret—nor desert from the execution there-</p>
        <p>The Objects of the Conspiracy as set out in the Trial:</p>
        <p>First, To Deprive the King of his Rights. Secondly, To Murder the King, the Queen and the Prince. Thirdly, To Stir Rebellion and Sedition in the Kingdom. Fourthly, To Bring a Miserable Destruc- tion among the Subjects. Fifthly, To Change, Alter, and Subvert the Religion here Established. Sixthly, To Eminate the State of the Commonwealth, and to bring in Strangers to invade it.</p>
        <p>"That after the destruction of the King, the Queen, the Prince, and Royal Male Issue, the Lords Spiritual and Temporal, the Knights, Burgesses, they should notify the same to Foreign States, and thereupon Sir Edmund Baynam—an attainted person of treason, and styling himself prince of the damned crew— should be sent and make the same known to the Pope and crave his aid, an Ambassador fit both for the message and pergon to be sent be- twixt the Pope and the Devil. That the Par- liament, being prorogued till 'the 7th February, they in December made a mine under the Houses of Parliament, proposing to place their powder there, but, the Parliament being then further adjourned till the 3rd of October, they in Lent following hired the vault, and placed therein twenty barrels of powder. "20 July: They laid in more ten barrels of powder, laying upon them divers great bars of iron and pieces of timber and great passive stones, and covering the sante with fagots, de. "20 Septemb: They lay in more four hogs- heads of powder, with other stones and bars of iron thereon.</p>
        <p>"4 Novem. (the Parliament being prorogued to the 5); at eleven o'clock at night Fawkes had prepared by We procurement of the rest, tonchwood and matches to give fire to the powder the next day."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>WEEKELY NE‘VES.</head>
        <p>THE WEEKELY NE‘VES.</p>
        <p>Numb. 19.</p>
        <p>London:. Printed for Jeffrey Chorllon, and are to be Sold at his Shop, at the great North Door of St. Paul's, 1606. Munday, 31st January, 1606.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <p>"A Brief Discourse upon the Arraignment and execution of the eight traytors—Digby, the two Winters, Graunt, Rookewood, Keyes, Bates, and Johnson, alias Guy Fawkes, four of which were executed in St. Paul's Churchyard, in London, upon Thursday, the 27th last, the other four in the Old Palace Yard, in West- minster, over against the Parliament House, and with a relation of the other traytors which were executed at Worcester.</p>
        <p>"Not to aggravate the sorrow of the living in the shame of the dead, but to dissuade the idolatrously blind from seeking their own destruction, the following account is written of the carrage of the eight papists herein named, of their little show of sorrow, their usage in prison, and their obstinacy to their end. First for their offence—it is so odious in the ears of all human creatures that it could hardly be believed that so many monsters in nature should carry the shapes of men-murder! Oh! it is the crying sin of the world, and such an intended murder as, had it taken effect, would have made a world to cry; and, therefore, the horror thereof must needs be hateful to the whole world to hear of it. My intent is chiefly to make report of the manner of their Execu- tion: for after their apprehension in the coun- try they were brought up to London upon the appearance of their foul treason before his Majesties most honourable Council, they were, by their commandment, committed to his Majesty's Tower of London, where they wanted nothing that, in the mercy of a Christian Prince, was thought fit, and indeed too good for so unchristian offenders.</p>
        <p>"After the traytors went from the Tower by water, and came to Westminster, before they came into the hall they made some half-hour stay or more in the Star Chamber, wether being brought and remaining till the Court was all ready to hear them. It was strange to note their carriage even in their very counten- ance—some hanging down the head as if their hearts were full of doggedness, and others forcing a stern look as if they would "fear death with a frown," never seeming to pray—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>DISCOVERY OF THE PLOT.</head>
        <p>THE DISCOVERY OF THE PLOT.</p>
        <p>"It was then resolved' that the houses and rooms around the Parliament House should be searched, and that same evening, the Lord Chamberlain, accompanied by Lord Monteagle, Sir Thomas Knevet, and others, proceeded to view a house, where they found in a vault under ground, great store of billets, faggots, and coal, brought there, as they were told, for the use of Mr. Piercy; and they espied Fawkes standing in a corner of the cellar, who said "That he was Mr. Piercy's servant, and left there by him for the keeping of the house." Upon the nam- ing of Piercy, Lord Monteagle told the Cham- berlain that he suspected Piercy to be the author of the letter, and as this increased the suspicions, when it was reported by the Lord Chamberlain to the King, in the presence of the Lord Admiral, Lord Treasurer, the Earls of Worcester, Northampton, and Salisbury, it was resolved that further search should be made what was that great pile of fuel in such a house where Piercy had so little occasion to reside. At midnight Sir Thomas Knevet, with a small party of armed men, repaired thither, and found Fawkes standing at the door booted and spurred, whom he at once apprehended. "Upon him were three matches, a tinder-box, and a dark lantern. The King was informed of it as well as the Privy Council, while the arch traytor was brought to the Tower of London."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>GAS FOR POWER PURPOSES.</head>
        <p>GAS FOR POWER PURPOSES.</p>
        <p>War Will Necessitate a Rise of Price.</p>
        <p>In submittingothe minutes of the Gas Com- mittee for the approval of the Town Council at their monthly meeting on Tuesday evening, Councillor LOCKER drew attention to the follow- ing item appearing therein "That application be made to the Local Gov- ernment Board for their sanction to the bor- rowing of the sum of .R 18,250 for the purposes of the gas undertaking, made up as follows: £5,500 to cover coat of Telpher scheme; £12,000 for new mains; £750 for water tank."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>LABOUR-SAVING DEVICE.</head>
        <p>A LABOUR-SAVING DEVICE.</p>
        <p>The Telpher scheme referred to, Councillor Locker explained, was a system for transferring coal, a more up-to-date method than they had at the present moment and a very great labour-saving appliance. Most of the members of the Gas Committee, who had visited works where the system was in use, were thoroughly convinced of the necessity of its being adopted in the Warrington works, and he, personally, felt sure that the gas consumer would have a good return on the outlay that would be in- volved. He only wished they could have intro- duced the scheme some time ago. Still, by waiting, they would have the benefit of other people's experience, and also would have the advantage of getting the best possible type of the machinery yet put on the market. The committee were thoroughly satisfied that the system was a good one, an that it was needed at their works. As, no doubt, most of the Council were aware, the gas undertaking was, just now, going through a very critical period. What with the extraordinary advance in the price of fuel and the shortage of labour, they were confronted with many serious difficulties which would, certainly, not allow the com- mittee to do as much for the rates of the bor- ough as they had done in past years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>WHY THE COST HAS ADVANCED.</head>
        <p>WHY THE COST HAS ADVANCED.</p>
        <p>Moreover, with the price of materials up and the cost of labour also, it was scarcely to be expected that the gas undertaking, like other commercial concerns, could be run at the same rates as previously. He would just like to give a word of warning to users of gas for power purposes. Up to the present they had been re- ceiving their gas at practically cost price, as an inducement to manufacturers, &amp;c., to come to Warrington with their works. The Council had endeavoured to give them power at the very lowest possible rate; indeed, the gas had been sold to them at practically cost price. Now that the cost of fuel and labour had been advanced, it was evident that they would be compelled in the near future to ask an advance in the price of gas for power purposes. As yet the committee had not definitely decided on what the new rates were to be, and when they had done so, due notice would be given to users.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>PROPOSAL TO THE GOVERNMENT.</head>
        <p>A PROPOSAL TO THE GOVERNMENT.</p>
        <p>The difficulties as to fuel and labour did not belong to Warrington alone; the whole of the country was labouring under them. At the end of the present week a meeting, convened by the Manchester Corporation, was being held, to which other towns were invited to send dele- gates, with a view to drawing up a case for presentation to Parliament, endeavouring to in- duce them to withdraw the Miners' Eight Hours Act during the period of the war. Owing to reservists having been called up, and to the num- ber of recruits, the collieries had had their staffs depleted to an extent felt by few other con- cerns, the result, of course, being that the output of the mines was greatly diminished. By temporarily withdrawing the Eight Hours Act it was hoped that the output of the collieries might be materially improved,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>OLD COKE STOCK SOLD OUT.</head>
        <p>OLD COKE STOCK SOLD OUT.</p>
        <p>Councillor HAWTHORN said he noticed that it had been proposed to increase the price of coke, and he would like to know whether, before they did so, the Gas Committee had cleared out their old stock, or whether they were merely propos- ing to take advantage of a rise in the market? If the committee had stocks on hand, he thought the consumers should have the advantage of them.</p>
        <p>Councillor SKELLAND said that the committee had cleared out the whole of their stock. Councillor LOCKER replied that there had been an excellent demand for coke, and the whole of the old stock had been sold; indeed, the position at the moment was that the committee could not take on further contracts. The Gas Committee's minutes were then adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <p>THE RETURN OF THE RAIDER. KAISER: "Well, I am surprised!" "IRPITZ: "So were we."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <p>Proprietors of "Punch.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>WITH THE COLOURS.</head>
        <p>WITH THE COLOURS.</p>
        <p>Teachers and Policemen Who Have Gone on Active Service.</p>
        <p>QUESTION OF ALLOWANCES.</p>
        <p>So far, twenty-three members of the Borough Police Force have either gone to the Front as reservists, or have volunteered, and been accepted, for active service with our troops. This fact was made known by the Mayor (Alder- man Peacock) at Tuesday evening's meeting of the Warrington Town Council, and his Worship added that he thought the force had done very well with regard to the Army. The fact that so many of the men had offered their services to their country reflected great credit not only on themselves, but also on the force generally. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>INCREASING USEFULNESS OF THE</head>
        <p>INCREASING USEFULNESS OF THE</p>
        <p>FORCE.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY MAYOR, (Councillor Joseph) agreed with his Worship, and said there were one or two remarks he would like to make with regard to matters mentioned in the Chief Constable's annual report. In the first place, it was very satisfactory to find from the report that during the last two or three years there had been a slight, gradual diminution in crime, and more especially in the number of indictable offences. It might be said that some of the decrease this year was due to the war. Perhaps that was so, but the decrease could not be ascribed solely to fiat cause, for itehad been gradually going on for the last few years. Another feature that ttiuck him, in reading the Chief Constable's report, had to do with the increasing usefulness of the members of the force. For instance, eighty out of the ninety meniters of the force ha,' qualified under the rules of the St. John Ambulance Brigade. That number of the men had passed the first examination, and several had gone further. Another point which struck him about the report was that 8,000 notices lulu been delivered by the police during the year to the various members of the Corporation and tt, thf magistrates. That showed how much work the Aldermen and Councillors did. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>SEASONABLE SUGGESTION.</head>
        <p>A SEASONABLE SUGGESTION.</p>
        <p>Councillor OSBORNE wondered whether it would not be possible to provide the police officers, who had to do point duty in wet weather, with overalls. The MAYOR said the matter had been before the Watch Committee, and the Chief Constable v. as c..-.idering it. Counciller OSBORNE : I wish to press the point.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: it is not on the minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor OSBORNE said that the reason he pressed the matter was that, though he drew atientnon t. it so long as twelve months ago, !lofting t &amp;I been done. The MAYOR repeated that the matter was already under consideration, and promised that it should be dealt with at the next meeting of the Watch Committee.</p>
        <p>ALLOWANCES TO POLICEMEN-SOLDIERS The minutes of the Watch Committee as laid before the Council contained the following item! It was resolved that the sum of ss. per week be granted to all unmarried members of the force who have joined H.M. Forces, and a sum not exceeding ss. per week to married men, but which with the existing allowance must not exceed the maximum of 15s. per week; the allowance of ss. per week as from this date to be retained and kept in hand for the benefit of the men until after the resumption of duties, and then paid to the men in accordance with the recommendations of the Chief Constable. In case of accident or death the moneys to be dealt with according to the instructions of the committee."</p>
        <p>On the suggestion of the MAYOR, the minute was allowed to be withdrawn.</p>
        <p>- AND TO TEACHER WARRIORS.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS previously drew attention to the fact that the Education Committee's minutes contained a resolution dealing with the proposed allowances to teachers who had gone on active service, and said that, as the Mayor had been good enough to point out to him before the Council meeting, the allowances, &amp;c., re- ferred to in that resolution did not quite agree with what the Corporation recommended in the case of certain other public servants, and he (Councillor Roberts), therefore, thought it would be better if the committee's recommenda- tion were withdrawn, with a view to a round- table conference, at which terms to apply equally all round could be drawn up. The committee's minutes were passed, with the exception of the one referred to by Coun- cillor Roberts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>IN CASE OF PROMOTION-NO RISE.</head>
        <p>IN CASE OF PROMOTION-NO RISE.</p>
        <p>In this connection the General Purposes Com- mittee reported as follows: " The scale of allow- ances to Corporation employes serving in His Majesty's Forces was re-considered with regard to those above the rank of private, or who hereafter obtain promotion in H.M. Forces, and it was resolved that the allowances made to employes who are serving in H.M. Forces in any rank higher than that of a private be dealt with from this date as if such employes were privates (in receipt of Army pay of 78. per week), and that the Mayor and the Advisory Committee appointed to deal with the allowances to soldiers and sailors and their dependants be authorised in their discre- tion to deal with such cases, retaining such sums for the benefit of the soldier on his return to Corporation employment as they consider desirable."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>Scarlet Fever Epidemic.</head>
        <p>The Scarlet Fever Epidemic.</p>
        <p>During last week 29 fresh scarlet fever cases were reported. On Saturday there were 93 patients •suffering from this disease in the Borough Hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>Cold Weather.</head>
        <p>Cold Weather.</p>
        <p>Last week the mean temperature in this dis- trict was only 35 degrees, or 3 degrees lower than in the preceding week. Monday week was the coldest day recently, the thermometer regis- tering only 27 degrees, or 20 degrees lower than the day before.</p>
      </div>
