      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>WARRIORS' WOMENFOLK.</head>
        <p>WARRIORS' WOMENFOLK.</p>
        <p>Entertained by Latchford B.W.T.A.</p>
        <p>A social gathering for the benefit of the wives and mothers of soldiers and sailors on active service was held at the Latchford Wesleyan Schoolrooms on Monday evening by the mem- bers of the local branch of the British Women's Temperance Association. The rooms were gaily decked out with bunting, and the 154) women present, many of whom were accompanied by their children appeared to thoroughly enjoy the proceedings, which consisted of a tea and con- cert. Among those who took part in the con- cert were the Misses G. Renshaw, Lewis, L. Foden, E. Tanner, Littlewood, Ditchfield, and members of the Baptist Girls' Union, and Messrs. Conway; Griffiths, and Mons. J. Bar- rix, a Belgian refugee. At an interval in the. programme,</p>
        <p>- Mrs. Dodds (Penketh), in a short address,. said she thought the men at the Front ought most certainly to be temperate men, and hoped that all wives and mothers would urge this point on their men folk whenever they wrote to them. She had had a letter from one "Tommy" who had sent her a signed pledge. It gave her great pleasure, she added, to be present because she admired the men in the trenches, and the wives and motheiTs of the men at the Front. She wished she were a man, so. that she could take her hat off to the women, and show her admiration for them in sending their loved ones to fight. (Applause.) The schoolrooms were kindb- lent by the Mis- sion for the occasion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>OFF TO THE FRONT.</head>
        <p>OFF TO THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>South Lancashire Sergeants Say "Au Revoir" to Departing Comrades.</p>
        <p>A merry company foregathered in the Ser- geants' Mess at Orford Barracks the other even- ing in order to say au revoir to Company-Ser- geant-Major Webster and Sergeant Campbell, both of the South Lancashires, *lto are under orders for the Front. The chair was taken by Sergeant-Major Quick, who, during an interval in an enjoyable musical programme, presented Company-Sergeant-Major Webster with a ease of pipes and Sergeant Campbell with a cigarette case and silver cigarette holder, the gifts having been subscribed for by members of the Mess. Among those present, in addition to the mili- tary men, were the Revs. Fathers Charles and Frank Swarbrick, the first-named obliging the company with "Tommy's favcitirite marching song, "It's a long way to Tipperary," whilst he also made a brief speech, in which he referred to the splendid work done by the "boys" of the South Lancashire Regiment at the Front, say- ing that they had covered themselvel with glory. The Chairman thanked the Rev. Fathers for their attendance, and said how greatly pleased the members of the Mess were to have had them amongst them. The Sergeants' Mess also hoped that Sergeants Webster and Campbell would have as good a time as circumstances permitted abroad, and would return safe and sound at the close of the war.</p>
        <p>Sere. Williams opened the programme with the song "Our Kiddy," which was well re- ceived, as were also Mr. Lambert's contributions "Soldiers of the King" and "Where are the Boys of the Old Brigade?" and his recitals of two of the king's speeches from Shakespeare's "Henry the Fifth." Company-Sergeant-Major Campbell obliged with "Just a Song at Twi- light" and "The Song that reached my Heart," and Bugler Hughes won loud applause for his renditions of "The Language of Your Eyes". and "The Blind Boy." Before the company dispersed they drank the -healths of their departing comrades, ar fl joined hands in singing "For Auld Lang Syne." During an interval refreshments were served /and as they were being discussed Bugler Hughes regaled the company with a series of pianoforte selections. Prior to the concert Mr. and Mrs. Lambert entertained ." the boys" quartered in the Bar- racks with a splendid series of gramophone. selections.</p>
      </div>
